{"surah": 21, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "ٱقْتَرَبَ لِلنَّاسِ حِسَابُهُمْ وَهُمْ فِى غَفْلَةٍ مُّعْرِضُونَ ", "translation": "[The time of] their account has approached for the people, while they are in heedlessness turning away.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱقْتَرَبَ", "translit": "َbَrَtْqٱ", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حِسَابُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُbāَsِḥ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "غَفْلَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَlْfَغ", "root": "غفل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُّعْرِضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍِrْʿعُّm", "root": "عرض"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "مَا يَأْتِيهِم مِّن ذِكْرٍ مِّن رَّبِّهِم مُّحْدَثٍ إِلَّا ٱسْتَمَعُوهُ وَهُمْ يَلْعَبُونَ ", "translation": "No mention [i.e., revelation] comes to them anew from their Lord except that they listen to it while they are at play", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَأْتِيهِم", "translit": "mِhyِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ذِكْرٍ", "translit": "ٍrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَّبِّهِم", "translit": "mِhِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُّحْدَثٍ", "translit": "ٍhtَdْḥُّm", "root": "حدث"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱسْتَمَعُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُʿعَmَtْsٱ", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَلْعَبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbَʿعْlَy", "root": "لعب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "لَاهِيَةً قُلُوبُهُمْۗ وَأَسَرُّوا۟ ٱلنَّجْوَى ٱلَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا۟ هَلْ هَٰذَآ إِلَّا بَشَرٌ مِّثْلُكُمْۖ أَفَتَأْتُونَ ٱلسِّحْرَ وَأَنتُمْ تُبْصِرُونَ ", "translation": "With their hearts distracted. And those who do wrong conceal their private conversation, [saying], \"Is this [Prophet] except a human being like you? So would you approach magic while you are aware [of it]?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَاهِيَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyِhāَl", "root": "لهو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قُلُوبُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَأَسَرُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّrَsَaʾَw", "root": "سرر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلنَّجْوَى", "translit": "àَwْjgَّnlٱ", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ظَلَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَlَẓ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "هَٰذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَشَرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّثْلُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُlْhtِّm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَفَتَأْتُونَ", "translit": "َnwُtْaʾَtَfَaʾ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلسِّحْرَ", "translit": "َrْḥِّslٱ", "root": "سحر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَأَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تُبْصِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِṣْbُt", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "قَالَ رَبِّى يَعْلَمُ ٱلْقَوْلَ فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ وَٱلْأَرْضِۖ وَهُوَ ٱلسَّمِيعُ ٱلْعَلِيمُ ", "translation": "He [the Prophet (ﷺ)] said, \"My Lord knows whatever is said throughout the heaven and earth, and He is the Hearing, the Knowing.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّى", "translit": "àِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلَ", "translit": "َlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمِيعُ", "translit": "ُʿعyِmَّslٱ", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْعَلِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِlَʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "بَلْ قَالُوٓا۟ أَضْغَٰثُ أَحْلَٰمٍۭ بَلِ ٱفْتَرَىٰهُ بَلْ هُوَ شَاعِرٌ فَلْيَأْتِنَا بِـَٔايَةٍ كَمَآ أُرْسِلَ ٱلْأَوَّلُونَ ", "translation": "But they say, \"[The revelation is but] a mixture of false dreams; rather, he has invented it; rather, he is a poet. So let him bring us a sign just as the previous [messengers] were sent [with miracles].\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَضْغَٰثُ", "translit": "ُhtَٰغْḍَaʾ", "root": "ضغث"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَحْلَٰمٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭmَٰlْḥَaʾ", "root": "حلم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَلِ", "translit": "ِlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱفْتَرَىٰهُ", "translit": "ُhٰàَrَtْfٱ", "root": "فري"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شَاعِرٌ", "translit": "ٌrِʿعāَhs", "root": "شعر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَلْيَأْتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtْaʾَyْlَf", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أُرْسِلَ", "translit": "َlِsْrُaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْأَوَّلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَّwَaʾْlٱ", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "مَآ ءَامَنَتْ قَبْلَهُم مِّن قَرْيَةٍ أَهْلَكْنَٰهَآۖ أَفَهُمْ يُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "Not a [single] city which We destroyed believed before them,[1] so will they believe?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامَنَتْ", "translit": "ْtَnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَبْلَهُم", "translit": "mُhَlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَرْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْrَq", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَهْلَكْنَٰهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhَٰnْkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَفَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَا قَبْلَكَ إِلَّا رِجَالًا نُّوحِىٓ إِلَيْهِمْۖ فَسْـَٔلُوٓا۟ أَهْلَ ٱلذِّكْرِ إِن كُنتُمْ لَا تَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "And We sent not before you, [O Muḥammad], except men to whom We revealed [the message], so ask the people of the message [i.e., former scriptures] if you do not know.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَبْلَكَ", "translit": "َkَlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رِجَالًا", "translit": "āًlāَjgِr", "root": "رجل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نُّوحِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِḥwُّn", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَسْـَٔلُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlَٔـْsَf", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَهْلَ", "translit": "َlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلذِّكْرِ", "translit": "ِrْkِّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "تَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَt", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَمَا جَعَلْنَٰهُمْ جَسَدًا لَّا يَأْكُلُونَ ٱلطَّعَامَ وَمَا كَانُوا۟ خَٰلِدِينَ ", "translation": "And We did not make them [i.e., the prophets] forms not eating food,[1] nor were they immortal [on earth].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَسَدًا", "translit": "āًdَsَjg", "root": "جسد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَأْكُلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlُkْaʾَy", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلطَّعَامَ", "translit": "َmāَʿعَّṭlٱ", "root": "طعم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "خَٰلِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "ثُمَّ صَدَقْنَٰهُمُ ٱلْوَعْدَ فَأَنجَيْنَٰهُمْ وَمَن نَّشَآءُ وَأَهْلَكْنَا ٱلْمُسْرِفِينَ ", "translation": "Then[1] We fulfilled for them the promise, and We saved them and whom We willed and destroyed the transgressors.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَدَقْنَٰهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَٰnْqَdَṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْوَعْدَ", "translit": "َdْʿعَwْlٱ", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأَنجَيْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْyَjgnَaʾَf", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَّشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَّn", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَهْلَكْنَا", "translit": "āَnْkَlْhَaʾَw", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْمُسْرِفِينَ", "translit": "َnyِfِrْsُmْlٱ", "root": "سرف"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "لَقَدْ أَنزَلْنَآ إِلَيْكُمْ كِتَٰبًا فِيهِ ذِكْرُكُمْۖ أَفَلَا تَعْقِلُونَ ", "translation": "We have certainly sent down to you a Book [i.e., the Qur’ān] in which is your mention.[1] Then will you not reason?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنزَلْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كِتَٰبًا", "translit": "āًbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ذِكْرُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَعْقِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِqْʿعَt", "root": "عقل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "وَكَمْ قَصَمْنَا مِن قَرْيَةٍ كَانَتْ ظَالِمَةً وَأَنشَأْنَا بَعْدَهَا قَوْمًا ءَاخَرِينَ ", "translation": "And how many a city which was unjust[1] have We shattered and produced after it another people.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَمْ", "translit": "ْmَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَصَمْنَا", "translit": "āَnْmَṣَq", "root": "قصم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قَرْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْrَq", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَانَتْ", "translit": "ْtَnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ظَالِمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmِlāَẓ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَنشَأْنَا", "translit": "āَnْaʾَhsnَaʾَw", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَعْدَهَا", "translit": "āَhَdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "قَوْمًا", "translit": "āًmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ءَاخَرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrَhkāَء", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "فَلَمَّآ أَحَسُّوا۟ بَأْسَنَآ إِذَا هُم مِّنْهَا يَرْكُضُونَ ", "translation": "And when they [i.e., its inhabitants] perceived Our punishment, at once they fled from it.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَلَمَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّmَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَحَسُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّsَḥَaʾ", "root": "حسس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَأْسَنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَsْaʾَb", "root": "باس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هُم", "translit": "mُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَرْكُضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍُkْrَy", "root": "ركض"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "لَا تَرْكُضُوا۟ وَٱرْجِعُوٓا۟ إِلَىٰ مَآ أُتْرِفْتُمْ فِيهِ وَمَسَٰكِنِكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُسْـَٔلُونَ ", "translation": "[Some angels said], \"Do not flee but return to where you were given luxury and to your homes - perhaps you will be questioned.\"[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَرْكُضُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḍُkْrَt", "root": "ركض"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱرْجِعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُʿعِjgْrٱَw", "root": "رجع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أُتْرِفْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْfِrْtُaʾ", "root": "ترف"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَسَٰكِنِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِnِkَٰsَmَw", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَعَلَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تُسْـَٔلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَٔـْsُt", "root": "سال"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "قَالُوا۟ يَٰوَيْلَنَآ إِنَّا كُنَّا ظَٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "They said, \"O woe to us! Indeed, we were wrongdoers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَٰوَيْلَنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَlْyَwَٰy", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ظَٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَٰẓ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "فَمَا زَالَت تِّلْكَ دَعْوَىٰهُمْ حَتَّىٰ جَعَلْنَٰهُمْ حَصِيدًا خَٰمِدِينَ ", "translation": "And that declaration of theirs did not cease until We made them [as] a harvest [mowed down], extinguished [like a fire].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَمَا", "translit": "āَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "زَالَت", "translit": "tَlāَz", "root": "زيل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تِّلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlِّt", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "دَعْوَىٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhٰàَwْʿعَd", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "حَصِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِṣَḥ", "root": "حصد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "خَٰمِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِmَٰhk", "root": "خمد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "وَمَا خَلَقْنَا ٱلسَّمَآءَ وَٱلْأَرْضَ وَمَا بَيْنَهُمَا لَٰعِبِينَ ", "translation": "And We did not create the heaven and earth and that between them in play.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَٰعِبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbِʿعَٰl", "root": "لعب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "لَوْ أَرَدْنَآ أَن نَّتَّخِذَ لَهْوًا لَّٱتَّخَذْنَٰهُ مِن لَّدُنَّآ إِن كُنَّا فَٰعِلِينَ ", "translation": "Had We intended to take a diversion,[1] We could have taken it from [what is] with Us - if [indeed] We were to do so.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرَدْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْdَrَaʾ", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَّتَّخِذَ", "translit": "َhdِhkَّtَّn", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَهْوًا", "translit": "āًwْhَl", "root": "لهو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَّٱتَّخَذْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْhdَhkَّtٱَّl", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَّدُنَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّnُdَّl", "root": "لدن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَٰعِلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlِʿعَٰf", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "بَلْ نَقْذِفُ بِٱلْحَقِّ عَلَى ٱلْبَٰطِلِ فَيَدْمَغُهُۥ فَإِذَا هُوَ زَاهِقٌۚ وَلَكُمُ ٱلْوَيْلُ مِمَّا تَصِفُونَ ", "translation": "Rather, We dash the truth upon falsehood, and it destroys it,[1] and thereupon it departs. And for you is destruction from that which you describe.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَقْذِفُ", "translit": "ُfِhdْqَn", "root": "قذف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْبَٰطِلِ", "translit": "ِlِṭَٰbْlٱ", "root": "بطل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَيَدْمَغُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُغَmْdَyَf", "root": "دمغ"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "زَاهِقٌ", "translit": "ٌqِhāَz", "root": "زهق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْوَيْلُ", "translit": "ُlْyَwْlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَصِفُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfِṣَt", "root": "وصف"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "وَلَهُۥ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِۚ وَمَنْ عِندَهُۥ لَا يَسْتَكْبِرُونَ عَنْ عِبَادَتِهِۦ وَلَا يَسْتَحْسِرُونَ ", "translation": "To Him belongs whoever is in the heavens and the earth. And those near Him [i.e., the angels] are not prevented by arrogance from His worship, nor do they tire.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عِندَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَسْتَكْبِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِbْkَtْsَy", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَنْ", "translit": "ْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عِبَادَتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَسْتَحْسِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِsْḥَtْsَy", "root": "حسر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "يُسَبِّحُونَ ٱلَّيْلَ وَٱلنَّهَارَ لَا يَفْتُرُونَ ", "translation": "They exalt [Him] night and day [and] do not slacken.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يُسَبِّحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥِّbَsُy", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلَ", "translit": "َlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّهَارَ", "translit": "َrāَhَّnlٱَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَفْتُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُtْfَy", "root": "فتر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "أَمِ ٱتَّخَذُوٓا۟ ءَالِهَةً مِّنَ ٱلْأَرْضِ هُمْ يُنشِرُونَ ", "translation": "Or have they [i.e., men] taken for themselves gods from the earth who resurrect [the dead]?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمِ", "translit": "ِmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَالِهَةً", "translit": "ًẗَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُنشِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِhsnُy", "root": "نشر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "لَوْ كَانَ فِيهِمَآ ءَالِهَةٌ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ لَفَسَدَتَاۚ فَسُبْحَٰنَ ٱللَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَرْشِ عَمَّا يَصِفُونَ ", "translation": "Had there been within them [i.e., the heavens and earth] gods besides Allāh, they both would have been ruined. So exalted is Allāh, Lord of the Throne, above what they describe.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِيهِمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَالِهَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَفَسَدَتَا", "translit": "āَtَdَsَfَl", "root": "فسد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَسُبْحَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰḥْbُsَf", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْعَرْشِ", "translit": "ِhsْrَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عرش"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَصِفُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfِṣَy", "root": "وصف"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "لَا يُسْـَٔلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْـَٔلُونَ ", "translation": "He is not questioned about what He does, but they will be questioned.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُسْـَٔلُ", "translit": "ُlَٔـْsُy", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَفْعَلُ", "translit": "ُlَʿعْfَy", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يُسْـَٔلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَٔـْsُy", "root": "سال"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "أَمِ ٱتَّخَذُوا۟ مِن دُونِهِۦٓ ءَالِهَةًۖ قُلْ هَاتُوا۟ بُرْهَٰنَكُمْۖ هَٰذَا ذِكْرُ مَن مَّعِىَ وَذِكْرُ مَن قَبْلِىۗ بَلْ أَكْثَرُهُمْ لَا يَعْلَمُونَ ٱلْحَقَّۖ فَهُم مُّعْرِضُونَ ", "translation": "Or have they taken gods besides Him? Say, [O Muḥammad], \"Produce your proof. This [Qur’ān] is the message for those with me and the message of those before me.\"[1] But most of them do not know the truth, so they are turning away.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمِ", "translit": "ِmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "دُونِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَالِهَةً", "translit": "ًẗَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هَاتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtāَh", "root": "هات"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بُرْهَٰنَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَnَٰhْrُb", "root": "برهن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ذِكْرُ", "translit": "ُrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَّعِىَ", "translit": "َàِʿعَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَذِكْرُ", "translit": "ُrْkِhdَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "قَبْلِى", "translit": "àِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَكْثَرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrَhtْkَaʾ", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقَّ", "translit": "َّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "فَهُم", "translit": "mُhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مُّعْرِضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍِrْʿعُّm", "root": "عرض"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَا مِن قَبْلِكَ مِن رَّسُولٍ إِلَّا نُوحِىٓ إِلَيْهِ أَنَّهُۥ لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّآ أَنَا۠ فَٱعْبُدُونِ ", "translation": "And We sent not before you any messenger except We revealed to him that, \"There is no deity except Me, so worship Me.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قَبْلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَّسُولٍ", "translit": "ٍlwُsَّr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نُوحِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِḥwُn", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَآ", "translit": "ٓāَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِلَٰهَ", "translit": "َhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَٱعْبُدُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُdُbْʿعٱَf", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "وَقَالُوا۟ ٱتَّخَذَ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ وَلَدًاۗ سُبْحَٰنَهُۥۚ بَلْ عِبَادٌ مُّكْرَمُونَ ", "translation": "And they say, \"The Most Merciful has taken a son.\" Exalted is He! Rather, they[1] are [but] honored servants.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذَ", "translit": "َhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَلَدًا", "translit": "āًdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سُبْحَٰنَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnَٰḥْbُs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عِبَادٌ", "translit": "ٌdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُّكْرَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَrْkُّm", "root": "كرم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "لَا يَسْبِقُونَهُۥ بِٱلْقَوْلِ وَهُم بِأَمْرِهِۦ يَعْمَلُونَ ", "translation": "They cannot precede Him in word, and they act by His command.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسْبِقُونَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnwُqِbْsَy", "root": "سبق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلْقَوْلِ", "translit": "ِlْwَqْlٱِb", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَهُم", "translit": "mُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِأَمْرِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِrْmَaʾِb", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "يَعْلَمُ مَا بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَمَا خَلْفَهُمْ وَلَا يَشْفَعُونَ إِلَّا لِمَنِ ٱرْتَضَىٰ وَهُم مِّنْ خَشْيَتِهِۦ مُشْفِقُونَ ", "translation": "He knows what is [presently] before them and what will be after them,[1] and they cannot intercede except on behalf of one whom He approves. And they, from fear of Him, are apprehensive.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَيْدِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِdْyَaʾ", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَلْفَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَfْlَhk", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَشْفَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَfْhsَy", "root": "شفع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِمَنِ", "translit": "ِnَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱرْتَضَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَḍَtْrٱ", "root": "رضو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَهُم", "translit": "mُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "خَشْيَتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَyْhsَhk", "root": "خشي"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مُشْفِقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqِfْhsُm", "root": "شفق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "وَمَن يَقُلْ مِنْهُمْ إِنِّىٓ إِلَٰهٌ مِّن دُونِهِۦ فَذَٰلِكَ نَجْزِيهِ جَهَنَّمَۚ كَذَٰلِكَ نَجْزِى ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And whoever of them should say, \"Indeed, I am a god besides Him\" - that one We would recompense with Hell. Thus do We recompense the wrongdoers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَقُلْ", "translit": "ْlُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَٰهٌ", "translit": "ٌhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "دُونِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "نَجْزِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِzْjgَn", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نَجْزِى", "translit": "àِzْjgَn", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "أَوَلَمْ يَرَ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوٓا۟ أَنَّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ كَانَتَا رَتْقًا فَفَتَقْنَٰهُمَاۖ وَجَعَلْنَا مِنَ ٱلْمَآءِ كُلَّ شَىْءٍ حَىٍّۖ أَفَلَا يُؤْمِنُونَ ", "translation": "Have those who disbelieved not considered that the heavens and the earth were a joined entity, and then We separated them and made from water every living thing? Then will they not believe?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَرَ", "translit": "َrَy", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَفَرُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَانَتَا", "translit": "āَtَnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَتْقًا", "translit": "āًqْtَr", "root": "رتق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَفَتَقْنَٰهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَٰnْqَtَfَf", "root": "فتق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmْlٱ", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "حَىٍّ", "translit": "ٍّàَḥ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "وَجَعَلْنَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ رَوَٰسِىَ أَن تَمِيدَ بِهِمْ وَجَعَلْنَا فِيهَا فِجَاجًا سُبُلًا لَّعَلَّهُمْ يَهْتَدُونَ ", "translation": "And We placed within the earth firmly set mountains, lest it should shift with them, and We made therein [mountain] passes [as] roads that they might be guided.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَوَٰسِىَ", "translit": "َàِsَٰwَr", "root": "رسو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَمِيدَ", "translit": "َdyِmَt", "root": "ميد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فِجَاجًا", "translit": "āًjgāَjgِf", "root": "فجج"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "سُبُلًا", "translit": "āًlُbُs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَّعَلَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّlَʿعَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَهْتَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَtْhَy", "root": "هدي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "وَجَعَلْنَا ٱلسَّمَآءَ سَقْفًا مَّحْفُوظًاۖ وَهُمْ عَنْ ءَايَٰتِهَا مُعْرِضُونَ ", "translation": "And We made the sky a protected ceiling, but they, from its signs,[1] are turning away.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "سَقْفًا", "translit": "āًfْqَs", "root": "سقف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَّحْفُوظًا", "translit": "āًẓwُfْḥَّm", "root": "حفظ"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَنْ", "translit": "ْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُعْرِضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍِrْʿعُm", "root": "عرض"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "وَهُوَ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ ٱلَّيْلَ وَٱلنَّهَارَ وَٱلشَّمْسَ وَٱلْقَمَرَۖ كُلٌّ فِى فَلَكٍ يَسْبَحُونَ ", "translation": "And it is He who created the night and the day and the sun and the moon; all [heavenly bodies] in an orbit are swimming.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلَ", "translit": "َlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّهَارَ", "translit": "َrāَhَّnlٱَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلشَّمْسَ", "translit": "َsْmَّhslٱَw", "root": "شمس"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلْقَمَرَ", "translit": "َrَmَqْlٱَw", "root": "قمر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كُلٌّ", "translit": "ٌّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَلَكٍ", "translit": "ٍkَlَf", "root": "فلك"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَسْبَحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥَbْsَy", "root": "سبح"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "وَمَا جَعَلْنَا لِبَشَرٍ مِّن قَبْلِكَ ٱلْخُلْدَۖ أَفَإِي۟ن مِّتَّ فَهُمُ ٱلْخَٰلِدُونَ ", "translation": "And We did not grant to any man before you eternity [on earth]; so if you die - would they be eternal?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِبَشَرٍ", "translit": "ٍrَhsَbِl", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْخُلْدَ", "translit": "َdْlُhkْlٱ", "root": "خلد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَفَإِي۟ن", "translit": "n۟yِiʾَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِّتَّ", "translit": "َّtِّm", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْخَٰلِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِlَٰhkْlٱ", "root": "خلد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "كُلُّ نَفْسٍ ذَآئِقَةُ ٱلْمَوْتِۗ وَنَبْلُوكُم بِٱلشَّرِّ وَٱلْخَيْرِ فِتْنَةًۖ وَإِلَيْنَا تُرْجَعُونَ ", "translation": "Every soul will taste death. And We test you with evil and with good as trial; and to Us you will be returned.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَفْسٍ", "translit": "ٍsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذَآئِقَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَqِyʾٓāَhd", "root": "ذوق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتِ", "translit": "ِtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَنَبْلُوكُم", "translit": "mُkwُlْbَnَw", "root": "بلو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِٱلشَّرِّ", "translit": "ِّrَّhslٱِb", "root": "شرر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلْخَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَhkْlٱَw", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِتْنَةً", "translit": "ًẗَnْtِf", "root": "فتن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَإِلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تُرْجَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَjgْrُt", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "وَإِذَا رَءَاكَ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوٓا۟ إِن يَتَّخِذُونَكَ إِلَّا هُزُوًا أَهَٰذَا ٱلَّذِى يَذْكُرُ ءَالِهَتَكُمْ وَهُم بِذِكْرِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ هُمْ كَٰفِرُونَ ", "translation": "And when those who disbelieve see you, [O Muḥammad], they take you not except in ridicule, [saying], \"Is this the one who mentions [i.e., insults] your gods?\" And they are, at the mention of the Most Merciful, disbelievers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَءَاكَ", "translit": "َkāَءَr", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَفَرُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَتَّخِذُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُhdِhkَّtَy", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "هُزُوًا", "translit": "āًwُzُh", "root": "هزا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَهَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰhَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَذْكُرُ", "translit": "ُrُkْhdَy", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ءَالِهَتَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَtَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَهُم", "translit": "mُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِذِكْرِ", "translit": "ِrْkِhdِb", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "كَٰفِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِfَٰk", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "خُلِقَ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ مِنْ عَجَلٍۚ سَأُو۟رِيكُمْ ءَايَٰتِى فَلَا تَسْتَعْجِلُونِ ", "translation": "Man was created of haste [i.e., impatience]. I will show you My signs [i.e., vengeance], so do not impatiently urge Me.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "خُلِقَ", "translit": "َqِlُhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَجَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَjgَʿع", "root": "عجل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَأُو۟رِيكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkyِr۟wُaʾَs", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِى", "translit": "àِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَسْتَعْجِلُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُlِjgْʿعَtْsَt", "root": "عجل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "وَيَقُولُونَ مَتَىٰ هَٰذَا ٱلْوَعْدُ إِن كُنتُمْ صَٰدِقِينَ ", "translation": "And they say, \"When is this promise, if you should be truthful?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَتَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَtَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْوَعْدُ", "translit": "ُdْʿعَwْlٱ", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صَٰدِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِdَٰṣ", "root": "صدق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "لَوْ يَعْلَمُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ حِينَ لَا يَكُفُّونَ عَن وُجُوهِهِمُ ٱلنَّارَ وَلَا عَن ظُهُورِهِمْ وَلَا هُمْ يُنصَرُونَ ", "translation": "If those who disbelieved but knew the time when they will not avert the Fire from their faces or from their backs and they will not be aided...[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حِينَ", "translit": "َnyِḥ", "root": "حين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَكُفُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّfُkَy", "root": "كفف"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وُجُوهِهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhِhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارَ", "translit": "َrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ظُهُورِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِrwُhُẓ", "root": "ظهر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يُنصَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَṣnُy", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "بَلْ تَأْتِيهِم بَغْتَةً فَتَبْهَتُهُمْ فَلَا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ رَدَّهَا وَلَا هُمْ يُنظَرُونَ ", "translation": "Rather, it will come to them unexpectedly and bewilder them, and they will not be able to repel it, nor will they be reprieved.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَأْتِيهِم", "translit": "mِhyِtْaʾَt", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَغْتَةً", "translit": "ًẗَtْغَb", "root": "بغت"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَتَبْهَتُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُtَhْbَtَf", "root": "بهت"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَسْتَطِيعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعyِṭَtْsَy", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَدَّهَا", "translit": "āَhَّdَr", "root": "ردد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يُنظَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَẓnُy", "root": "نظر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "وَلَقَدِ ٱسْتُهْزِئَ بِرُسُلٍ مِّن قَبْلِكَ فَحَاقَ بِٱلَّذِينَ سَخِرُوا۟ مِنْهُم مَّا كَانُوا۟ بِهِۦ يَسْتَهْزِءُونَ ", "translation": "And already were messengers ridiculed before you, but those who mocked them were enveloped by what they used to ridicule.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدِ", "translit": "ِdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱسْتُهْزِئَ", "translit": "َyʾِzْhُtْsٱ", "root": "هزا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِرُسُلٍ", "translit": "ٍlُsُrِb", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَحَاقَ", "translit": "َqāَḥَf", "root": "حيق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "سَخِرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrِhkَs", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَسْتَهْزِءُونَ", "translit": "َnwُءِzْhَtْsَy", "root": "هزا"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "قُلْ مَن يَكْلَؤُكُم بِٱلَّيْلِ وَٱلنَّهَارِ مِنَ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِۗ بَلْ هُمْ عَن ذِكْرِ رَبِّهِم مُّعْرِضُونَ ", "translation": "Say, \"Who can protect you at night or by day from the Most Merciful?\" But they are, from the remembrance of their Lord, turning away.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَكْلَؤُكُم", "translit": "mُkُwʾَlْkَy", "root": "كلا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱِb", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّهَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَhَّnlٱَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ذِكْرِ", "translit": "ِrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رَبِّهِم", "translit": "mِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مُّعْرِضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍِrْʿعُّm", "root": "عرض"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "أَمْ لَهُمْ ءَالِهَةٌ تَمْنَعُهُم مِّن دُونِنَاۚ لَا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ نَصْرَ أَنفُسِهِمْ وَلَا هُم مِّنَّا يُصْحَبُونَ ", "translation": "Or do they have gods to defend them other than Us? They are unable [even] to help themselves, nor can they be protected from Us.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَالِهَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَمْنَعُهُم", "translit": "mُhُʿعَnْmَt", "root": "منع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "دُونِنَا", "translit": "āَnِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَسْتَطِيعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعyِṭَtْsَy", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "نَصْرَ", "translit": "َrْṣَn", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَنفُسِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "هُم", "translit": "mُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يُصْحَبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbَḥْṣُy", "root": "صحب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "بَلْ مَتَّعْنَا هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ وَءَابَآءَهُمْ حَتَّىٰ طَالَ عَلَيْهِمُ ٱلْعُمُرُۗ أَفَلَا يَرَوْنَ أَنَّا نَأْتِى ٱلْأَرْضَ نَنقُصُهَا مِنْ أَطْرَافِهَآۚ أَفَهُمُ ٱلْغَٰلِبُونَ ", "translation": "But, [on the contrary], We have provided good things for these [disbelievers] and their fathers until life was prolonged for them. Then do they not see that We set upon the land, reducing it from its borders?[1] Is it they who will overcome?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَتَّعْنَا", "translit": "āَnْʿعَّtَm", "root": "متع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَءَابَآءَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَءٓāَbāَءَw", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "طَالَ", "translit": "َlāَṭ", "root": "طول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْعُمُرُ", "translit": "ُrُmُʿعْlٱ", "root": "عمر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَرَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَrَy", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نَأْتِى", "translit": "àِtْaʾَn", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "نَنقُصُهَا", "translit": "āَhُṣُqnَn", "root": "نقص"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَطْرَافِهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhِfāَrْṭَaʾ", "root": "طرف"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَفَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلْغَٰلِبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbِlَٰغْlٱ", "root": "غلب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "قُلْ إِنَّمَآ أُنذِرُكُم بِٱلْوَحْىِۚ وَلَا يَسْمَعُ ٱلصُّمُّ ٱلدُّعَآءَ إِذَا مَا يُنذَرُونَ ", "translation": "Say, \"I only warn you by revelation.\" But the deaf do not hear the call when they are warned.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أُنذِرُكُم", "translit": "mُkُrِhdnُaʾ", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلْوَحْىِ", "translit": "ِàْḥَwْlٱِb", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَسْمَعُ", "translit": "ُʿعَmْsَy", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصُّمُّ", "translit": "ُّmُّṣlٱ", "root": "صمم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلدُّعَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَʿعُّdlٱ", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُنذَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَhdnُy", "root": "نذر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 46, "text": "وَلَئِن مَّسَّتْهُمْ نَفْحَةٌ مِّنْ عَذَابِ رَبِّكَ لَيَقُولُنَّ يَٰوَيْلَنَآ إِنَّا كُنَّا ظَٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And if [as much as] a whiff of the punishment of your Lord should touch them, they would surely say, \"O woe to us! Indeed, we have been wrongdoers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَئِن", "translit": "nِyʾَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَّسَّتْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْtَّsَّm", "root": "مسس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَفْحَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَḥْfَn", "root": "نفح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَيَقُولُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُlwُqَyَl", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَٰوَيْلَنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَlْyَwَٰy", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ظَٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَٰẓ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 47, "text": "وَنَضَعُ ٱلْمَوَٰزِينَ ٱلْقِسْطَ لِيَوْمِ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ فَلَا تُظْلَمُ نَفْسٌ شَيْـًٔاۖ وَإِن كَانَ مِثْقَالَ حَبَّةٍ مِّنْ خَرْدَلٍ أَتَيْنَا بِهَاۗ وَكَفَىٰ بِنَا حَٰسِبِينَ ", "translation": "And We place the scales of justice for the Day of Resurrection, so no soul will be treated unjustly at all. And if there is [even] the weight of a mustard seed,[1] We will bring it forth. And sufficient are We as accountant.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنَضَعُ", "translit": "ُʿعَḍَnَw", "root": "وضع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوَٰزِينَ", "translit": "َnyِzَٰwَmْlٱ", "root": "وزن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقِسْطَ", "translit": "َṭْsِqْlٱ", "root": "قسط"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِيَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَyِl", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تُظْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْẓُt", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَفْسٌ", "translit": "ٌsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِثْقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqْhtِm", "root": "ثقل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "حَبَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّbَḥ", "root": "حبب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "خَرْدَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَdْrَhk", "root": "خردل"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtَaʾ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَكَفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfَkَw", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "بِنَا", "translit": "āَnِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "حَٰسِبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbِsَٰḥ", "root": "حسب"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 48, "text": "وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَا مُوسَىٰ وَهَٰرُونَ ٱلْفُرْقَانَ وَضِيَآءً وَذِكْرًا لِّلْمُتَّقِينَ ", "translation": "And We had already given Moses and Aaron the criterion and a light and a reminder[1] for the righteous", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَاتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَهَٰرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَٰhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْفُرْقَانَ", "translit": "َnāَqْrُfْlٱ", "root": "فرق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَضِيَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَyِḍَw", "root": "ضوا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَذِكْرًا", "translit": "āًrْkِhdَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِّلْمُتَّقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqَّtُmْlِّl", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 49, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ يَخْشَوْنَ رَبَّهُم بِٱلْغَيْبِ وَهُم مِّنَ ٱلسَّاعَةِ مُشْفِقُونَ ", "translation": "Who fear their Lord unseen,[1] while they are of the Hour apprehensive.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَخْشَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَhsْhkَy", "root": "خشي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبَّهُم", "translit": "mُhَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلْغَيْبِ", "translit": "ِbْyَغْlٱِb", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَهُم", "translit": "mُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلسَّاعَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَʿعāَّslٱ", "root": "سوع"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُشْفِقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqِfْhsُm", "root": "شفق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 50, "text": "وَهَٰذَا ذِكْرٌ مُّبَارَكٌ أَنزَلْنَٰهُۚ أَفَأَنتُمْ لَهُۥ مُنكِرُونَ ", "translation": "And this [Qur’ān] is a blessed message which We have sent down. Then are you with it unacquainted?[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ذِكْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُّبَارَكٌ", "translit": "ٌkَrāَbُّm", "root": "برك"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَنزَلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَفَأَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُنكِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِknُm", "root": "نكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 51, "text": "وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَآ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ رُشْدَهُۥ مِن قَبْلُ وَكُنَّا بِهِۦ عَٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And We had certainly given Abraham his sound judgement before,[1] and We were of him well-Knowing", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَاتَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رُشْدَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdْhsُr", "root": "رشد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَكُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَٰʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 52, "text": "إِذْ قَالَ لِأَبِيهِ وَقَوْمِهِۦ مَا هَٰذِهِ ٱلتَّمَاثِيلُ ٱلَّتِىٓ أَنتُمْ لَهَا عَٰكِفُونَ ", "translation": "When he said to his father and his people, \"What are these statues to which you are devoted?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِأَبِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِbَaʾِl", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَقَوْمِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِmْwَqَw", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَٰذِهِ", "translit": "ِhِhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلتَّمَاثِيلُ", "translit": "ُlyِhtāَmَّtlٱ", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَٰكِفُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfِkَٰʿع", "root": "عكف"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 53, "text": "قَالُوا۟ وَجَدْنَآ ءَابَآءَنَا لَهَا عَٰبِدِينَ ", "translation": "They said, \"We found our fathers worshippers of them.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَجَدْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْdَjgَw", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَابَآءَنَا", "translit": "āَnَءٓāَbāَء", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَٰبِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِbَٰʿع", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 54, "text": "قَالَ لَقَدْ كُنتُمْ أَنتُمْ وَءَابَآؤُكُمْ فِى ضَلَٰلٍ مُّبِينٍ ", "translation": "He said, \"You were certainly, you and your fathers, in manifest error.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَءَابَآؤُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُwʾٓāَbāَءَw", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ضَلَٰلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَٰlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُّبِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 55, "text": "قَالُوٓا۟ أَجِئْتَنَا بِٱلْحَقِّ أَمْ أَنتَ مِنَ ٱللَّٰعِبِينَ ", "translation": "They said, \"Have you come to us with truth, or are you of those who jest?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَجِئْتَنَا", "translit": "āَnَtْyʾِjgَaʾ", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّٰعِبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbِʿعَّٰllٱ", "root": "لعب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 56, "text": "قَالَ بَل رَّبُّكُمْ رَبُّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ ٱلَّذِى فَطَرَهُنَّ وَأَنَا۠ عَلَىٰ ذَٰلِكُم مِّنَ ٱلشَّٰهِدِينَ ", "translation": "He said, \"[No], rather, your Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth who created them, and I, to that, am of those who testify.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَل", "translit": "lَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَّبُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبُّ", "translit": "ُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَطَرَهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhَrَṭَf", "root": "فطر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَأَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكُم", "translit": "mُkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلشَّٰهِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِhَّٰhslٱ", "root": "شهد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 57, "text": "وَتَٱللَّهِ لَأَكِيدَنَّ أَصْنَٰمَكُم بَعْدَ أَن تُوَلُّوا۟ مُدْبِرِينَ ", "translation": "And [I swear] by Allāh, I will surely plan against your idols after you have turned and gone away.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَتَٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱَtَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَأَكِيدَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَdyِkَaʾَl", "root": "كيد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَصْنَٰمَكُم", "translit": "mُkَmَٰnْṣَaʾ", "root": "صنم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَعْدَ", "translit": "َdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تُوَلُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّlَwُt", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُدْبِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِbْdُm", "root": "دبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 58, "text": "فَجَعَلَهُمْ جُذَٰذًا إِلَّا كَبِيرًا لَّهُمْ لَعَلَّهُمْ إِلَيْهِ يَرْجِعُونَ ", "translation": "So he made them into fragments, except a large one among them, that they might return to it [and question].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَجَعَلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَʿعَjgَf", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جُذَٰذًا", "translit": "āًhdَٰhdُjg", "root": "جذذ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَبِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِbَk", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَعَلَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَرْجِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِjgْrَy", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 59, "text": "قَالُوا۟ مَن فَعَلَ هَٰذَا بِـَٔالِهَتِنَآ إِنَّهُۥ لَمِنَ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "They said, \"Who has done this to our gods? Indeed, he is of the wrongdoers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَf", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِـَٔالِهَتِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِtَhِlāَٔـِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 60, "text": "قَالُوا۟ سَمِعْنَا فَتًى يَذْكُرُهُمْ يُقَالُ لَهُۥٓ إِبْرَٰهِيمُ ", "translation": "They said, \"We heard a young man mention them who is called Abraham.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَمِعْنَا", "translit": "āَnْʿعِmَs", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَتًى", "translit": "àًtَf", "root": "فتي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَذْكُرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrُkْhdَy", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يُقَالُ", "translit": "ُlāَqُy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 61, "text": "قَالُوا۟ فَأْتُوا۟ بِهِۦ عَلَىٰٓ أَعْيُنِ ٱلنَّاسِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَشْهَدُونَ ", "translation": "They said, \"Then bring him before the eyes of the people that they may testify.\"[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَأْتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtْaʾَf", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَعْيُنِ", "translit": "ِnُyْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَعَلَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَشْهَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَhْhsَy", "root": "شهد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 62, "text": "قَالُوٓا۟ ءَأَنتَ فَعَلْتَ هَٰذَا بِـَٔالِهَتِنَا يَٰٓإِبْرَٰهِيمُ ", "translation": "They said, \"Have you done this to our gods, O Abraham?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَأَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾَء", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَعَلْتَ", "translit": "َtْlَʿعَf", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِـَٔالِهَتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَhِlāَٔـِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَٰٓإِبْرَٰهِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِhَٰrْbِiʾَٰٓy", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 63, "text": "قَالَ بَلْ فَعَلَهُۥ كَبِيرُهُمْ هَٰذَا فَسْـَٔلُوهُمْ إِن كَانُوا۟ يَنطِقُونَ ", "translation": "He said, \"Rather, this - the largest of them - did it, so ask them, if they should [be able to] speak.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَعَلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlَʿعَf", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَبِيرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُryِbَk", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَسْـَٔلُوهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhwُlَٔـْsَf", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَنطِقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqِṭnَy", "root": "نطق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 64, "text": "فَرَجَعُوٓا۟ إِلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ فَقَالُوٓا۟ إِنَّكُمْ أَنتُمُ ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ ", "translation": "So they returned to [blaming] themselves and said [to each other], \"Indeed, you are the wrongdoers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَرَجَعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُʿعَjgَrَf", "root": "رجع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنفُسِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَقَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَنتُمُ", "translit": "ُmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 65, "text": "ثُمَّ نُكِسُوا۟ عَلَىٰ رُءُوسِهِمْ لَقَدْ عَلِمْتَ مَا هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ يَنطِقُونَ ", "translation": "Then they reversed themselves,[1] [saying], \"You have already known that these do not speak!\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نُكِسُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُsِkُn", "root": "نكس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رُءُوسِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِswُءُr", "root": "راس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلِمْتَ", "translit": "َtْmِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَنطِقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqِṭnَy", "root": "نطق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 66, "text": "قَالَ أَفَتَعْبُدُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ مَا لَا يَنفَعُكُمْ شَيْـًٔا وَلَا يَضُرُّكُمْ ", "translation": "He said, \"Then do you worship instead of Allāh that which does not benefit you at all or harm you?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَفَتَعْبُدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdُbْʿعَtَfَaʾ", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَنفَعُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُʿعَfnَy", "root": "نفع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَضُرُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّrُḍَy", "root": "ضرر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 67, "text": "أُفٍّ لَّكُمْ وَلِمَا تَعْبُدُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِۖ أَفَلَا تَعْقِلُونَ ", "translation": "Uff[1] to you and to what you worship instead of Allāh. Then will you not use reason?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أُفٍّ", "translit": "ٍّfُaʾ", "root": "افف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلِمَا", "translit": "āَmِlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَعْبُدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdُbْʿعَt", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَعْقِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِqْʿعَt", "root": "عقل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 68, "text": "قَالُوا۟ حَرِّقُوهُ وَٱنصُرُوٓا۟ ءَالِهَتَكُمْ إِن كُنتُمْ فَٰعِلِينَ ", "translation": "They said, \"Burn him and support your gods - if you are to act.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "حَرِّقُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُqِّrَḥ", "root": "حرق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱنصُرُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُrُṣnٱَw", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَالِهَتَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَtَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَٰعِلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlِʿعَٰf", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 69, "text": "قُلْنَا يَٰنَارُ كُونِى بَرْدًا وَسَلَٰمًا عَلَىٰٓ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ ", "translation": "We [i.e., Allāh] said, \"O fire, be coolness and safety upon Abraham.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَٰنَارُ", "translit": "ُrāَnَٰy", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُونِى", "translit": "àِnwُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَرْدًا", "translit": "āًdْrَb", "root": "برد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَسَلَٰمًا", "translit": "āًmَٰlَsَw", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 70, "text": "وَأَرَادُوا۟ بِهِۦ كَيْدًا فَجَعَلْنَٰهُمُ ٱلْأَخْسَرِينَ ", "translation": "And they intended for him a plan [i.e., harm], but We made them the greatest losers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَرَادُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdāَrَaʾَw", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَيْدًا", "translit": "āًdْyَk", "root": "كيد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَجَعَلْنَٰهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَٰnْlَʿعَjgَf", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَخْسَرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrَsْhkَaʾْlٱ", "root": "خسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 71, "text": "وَنَجَّيْنَٰهُ وَلُوطًا إِلَى ٱلْأَرْضِ ٱلَّتِى بَٰرَكْنَا فِيهَا لِلْعَٰلَمِينَ ", "translation": "And We delivered him and Lot to the land which We had blessed for the worlds [i.e., peoples].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنَجَّيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَّjgَnَw", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَلُوطًا", "translit": "āًṭwُlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَٰرَكْنَا", "translit": "āَnْkَrَٰb", "root": "برك"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِلْعَٰلَمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmَlَٰʿعْlِl", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 72, "text": "وَوَهَبْنَا لَهُۥٓ إِسْحَٰقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ نَافِلَةًۖ وَكُلًّا جَعَلْنَا صَٰلِحِينَ ", "translation": "And We gave him Isaac and Jacob in addition, and all [of them] We made righteous.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَوَهَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَhَwَw", "root": "وهب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِسْحَٰقَ", "translit": "َqَٰḥْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيَعْقُوبَ", "translit": "َbwُqْʿعَyَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نَافِلَةً", "translit": "ًẗَlِfāَn", "root": "نفل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَكُلًّا", "translit": "āًّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صَٰلِحِينَ", "translit": "َnyِḥِlَٰṣ", "root": "صلح"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 73, "text": "وَجَعَلْنَٰهُمْ أَئِمَّةً يَهْدُونَ بِأَمْرِنَا وَأَوْحَيْنَآ إِلَيْهِمْ فِعْلَ ٱلْخَيْرَٰتِ وَإِقَامَ ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ وَإِيتَآءَ ٱلزَّكَوٰةِۖ وَكَانُوا۟ لَنَا عَٰبِدِينَ ", "translation": "And We made them leaders guiding by Our command. And We inspired to them the doing of good deeds, establishment of prayer, and giving of zakāh; and they were worshippers of Us.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَئِمَّةً", "translit": "ًẗَّmِyʾَaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَهْدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdْhَy", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِأَمْرِنَا", "translit": "āَnِrْmَaʾِb", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَأَوْحَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَḥْwَaʾَw", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِعْلَ", "translit": "َlْʿعِf", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْخَيْرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrْyَhkْlٱ", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَإِقَامَ", "translit": "َmāَqِiʾَw", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَإِيتَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَtyِiʾَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلزَّكَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَkَّzlٱ", "root": "زكو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَكَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَنَا", "translit": "āَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَٰبِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِbَٰʿع", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 74, "text": "وَلُوطًا ءَاتَيْنَٰهُ حُكْمًا وَعِلْمًا وَنَجَّيْنَٰهُ مِنَ ٱلْقَرْيَةِ ٱلَّتِى كَانَت تَّعْمَلُ ٱلْخَبَٰٓئِثَۗ إِنَّهُمْ كَانُوا۟ قَوْمَ سَوْءٍ فَٰسِقِينَ ", "translation": "And to Lot We gave judgement and knowledge, and We saved him from the city that was committing wicked deeds. Indeed, they were a people of evil, defiantly disobedient.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلُوطًا", "translit": "āًṭwُlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَاتَيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حُكْمًا", "translit": "āًmْkُḥ", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَعِلْمًا", "translit": "āًmْlِʿعَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَنَجَّيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَّjgَnَw", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْقَرْيَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَyْrَqْlٱ", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَانَت", "translit": "tَnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَّعْمَلُ", "translit": "ُlَmْʿعَّt", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْخَبَٰٓئِثَ", "translit": "َhtِyʾَٰٓbَhkْlٱ", "root": "خبث"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "قَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "سَوْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْwَs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَٰسِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِsَٰf", "root": "فسق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 75, "text": "وَأَدْخَلْنَٰهُ فِى رَحْمَتِنَآۖ إِنَّهُۥ مِنَ ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ ", "translation": "And We admitted him into Our mercy. Indeed, he was of the righteous.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَدْخَلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَhkْdَaʾَw", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَحْمَتِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِtَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ", "translit": "َnyِḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 76, "text": "وَنُوحًا إِذْ نَادَىٰ مِن قَبْلُ فَٱسْتَجَبْنَا لَهُۥ فَنَجَّيْنَٰهُ وَأَهْلَهُۥ مِنَ ٱلْكَرْبِ ٱلْعَظِيمِ ", "translation": "And [mention] Noah, when he called [to Allāh][1] before [that time], so We responded to him and saved him and his family from the great affliction [i.e., the flood].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنُوحًا", "translit": "āًḥwُnَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَادَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdāَn", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَٱسْتَجَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَjgَtْsٱَf", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَنَجَّيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَّjgَnَf", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَأَهْلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlْhَaʾَw", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْكَرْبِ", "translit": "ِbْrَkْlٱ", "root": "كرب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْعَظِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِẓَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 77, "text": "وَنَصَرْنَٰهُ مِنَ ٱلْقَوْمِ ٱلَّذِينَ كَذَّبُوا۟ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَآۚ إِنَّهُمْ كَانُوا۟ قَوْمَ سَوْءٍ فَأَغْرَقْنَٰهُمْ أَجْمَعِينَ ", "translation": "And We aided [i.e., saved] him from the people who denied Our signs. Indeed, they were a people of evil, so We drowned them, all together.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنَصَرْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْrَṣَnَw", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَذَّبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "قَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "سَوْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْwَs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَأَغْرَقْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْqَrْغَaʾَf", "root": "غرق"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَجْمَعِينَ", "translit": "َnyِʿعَmْjgَaʾ", "root": "جمع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 78, "text": "وَدَاوُۥدَ وَسُلَيْمَٰنَ إِذْ يَحْكُمَانِ فِى ٱلْحَرْثِ إِذْ نَفَشَتْ فِيهِ غَنَمُ ٱلْقَوْمِ وَكُنَّا لِحُكْمِهِمْ شَٰهِدِينَ ", "translation": "And [mention] David and Solomon, when they judged concerning the field - when the sheep of a people overran it [at night],[1] and We were witness to their judgement.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَدَاوُۥدَ", "translit": "َdۥُwāَdَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَسُلَيْمَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰmْyَlُsَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَحْكُمَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَmُkْḥَy", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْحَرْثِ", "translit": "ِhtْrَḥْlٱ", "root": "حرث"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَفَشَتْ", "translit": "ْtَhsَfَn", "root": "نفش"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "غَنَمُ", "translit": "ُmَnَغ", "root": "غنم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَكُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لِحُكْمِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِmْkُḥِl", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "شَٰهِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِhَٰhs", "root": "شهد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 79, "text": "فَفَهَّمْنَٰهَا سُلَيْمَٰنَۚ وَكُلًّا ءَاتَيْنَا حُكْمًا وَعِلْمًاۚ وَسَخَّرْنَا مَعَ دَاوُۥدَ ٱلْجِبَالَ يُسَبِّحْنَ وَٱلطَّيْرَۚ وَكُنَّا فَٰعِلِينَ ", "translation": "And We gave understanding of it [i.e., the case] to Solomon, and to each [of them] We gave judgement and knowledge. And We subjected the mountains to exalt [Us], along with David and [also] the birds. And We were doing [that].[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَفَهَّمْنَٰهَا", "translit": "āَhَٰnْmَّhَfَf", "root": "فهم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سُلَيْمَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰmْyَlُs", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَكُلًّا", "translit": "āًّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَاتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حُكْمًا", "translit": "āًmْkُḥ", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَعِلْمًا", "translit": "āًmْlِʿعَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَسَخَّرْنَا", "translit": "āَnْrَّhkَsَw", "root": "سخر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "دَاوُۥدَ", "translit": "َdۥُwāَd", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْجِبَالَ", "translit": "َlāَbِjgْlٱ", "root": "جبل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُسَبِّحْنَ", "translit": "َnْḥِّbَsُy", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَٱلطَّيْرَ", "translit": "َrْyَّṭlٱَw", "root": "طير"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَكُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فَٰعِلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlِʿعَٰf", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 80, "text": "وَعَلَّمْنَٰهُ صَنْعَةَ لَبُوسٍ لَّكُمْ لِتُحْصِنَكُم مِّنۢ بَأْسِكُمْۖ فَهَلْ أَنتُمْ شَٰكِرُونَ ", "translation": "And We taught him the fashioning of coats of armor to protect you from your [enemy in] battle. So will you then be grateful?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَعَلَّمْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْmَّlَʿعَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَنْعَةَ", "translit": "َẗَʿعْnَṣ", "root": "صنع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَبُوسٍ", "translit": "ٍswُbَl", "root": "لبس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِتُحْصِنَكُم", "translit": "mُkَnِṣْḥُtِl", "root": "حصن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَأْسِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِsْaʾَb", "root": "باس"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَهَلْ", "translit": "ْlَhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "شَٰكِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِkَٰhs", "root": "شكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 81, "text": "وَلِسُلَيْمَٰنَ ٱلرِّيحَ عَاصِفَةً تَجْرِى بِأَمْرِهِۦٓ إِلَى ٱلْأَرْضِ ٱلَّتِى بَٰرَكْنَا فِيهَاۚ وَكُنَّا بِكُلِّ شَىْءٍ عَٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And to Solomon [We subjected] the wind, blowing forcefully, proceeding by his command toward the land which We had blessed. And We are ever, of all things, Knowing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلِسُلَيْمَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰmْyَlُsِlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلرِّيحَ", "translit": "َḥyِّrlٱ", "root": "روح"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَاصِفَةً", "translit": "ًẗَfِṣāَʿع", "root": "عصف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِأَمْرِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِrْmَaʾِb", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَٰرَكْنَا", "translit": "āَnْkَrَٰb", "root": "برك"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَكُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِb", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَٰʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 82, "text": "وَمِنَ ٱلشَّيَٰطِينِ مَن يَغُوصُونَ لَهُۥ وَيَعْمَلُونَ عَمَلًا دُونَ ذَٰلِكَۖ وَكُنَّا لَهُمْ حَٰفِظِينَ ", "translation": "And of the devils [i.e., jinn] were those who dived for him and did work other than that. And We were of them a guardian.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيَٰطِينِ", "translit": "ِnyِṭَٰyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَغُوصُونَ", "translit": "َnwُṣwُغَy", "root": "غوص"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَيَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَyَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَمَلًا", "translit": "āًlَmَʿع", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "دُونَ", "translit": "َnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَكُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "حَٰفِظِينَ", "translit": "َnyِẓِfَٰḥ", "root": "حفظ"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 83, "text": "وَأَيُّوبَ إِذْ نَادَىٰ رَبَّهُۥٓ أَنِّى مَسَّنِىَ ٱلضُّرُّ وَأَنتَ أَرْحَمُ ٱلرَّٰحِمِينَ ", "translation": "And [mention] Job, when he called to his Lord, \"Indeed, adversity has touched me, and You are the most merciful of the merciful.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَيُّوبَ", "translit": "َbwُّyَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَادَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdāَn", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبَّهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنِّى", "translit": "àِّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَسَّنِىَ", "translit": "َàِnَّsَm", "root": "مسس"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلضُّرُّ", "translit": "ُّrُّḍlٱ", "root": "ضرر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَأَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَرْحَمُ", "translit": "ُmَḥْrَaʾ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلرَّٰحِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِḥَّٰrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 84, "text": "فَٱسْتَجَبْنَا لَهُۥ فَكَشَفْنَا مَا بِهِۦ مِن ضُرٍّۖ وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ أَهْلَهُۥ وَمِثْلَهُم مَّعَهُمْ رَحْمَةً مِّنْ عِندِنَا وَذِكْرَىٰ لِلْعَٰبِدِينَ ", "translation": "So We responded to him and removed what afflicted him of adversity. And We gave him [back] his family and the like thereof with them as mercy from Us and a reminder for the worshippers [of Allāh].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱسْتَجَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَjgَtْsٱَf", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَكَشَفْنَا", "translit": "āَnْfَhsَkَf", "root": "كشف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ضُرٍّ", "translit": "ٍّrُḍ", "root": "ضرر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَهْلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمِثْلَهُم", "translit": "mُhَlْhtِmَw", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَّعَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَʿعَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رَحْمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عِندِنَا", "translit": "āَnِdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَذِكْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْkِhdَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لِلْعَٰبِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِbَٰʿعْlِl", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 85, "text": "وَإِسْمَٰعِيلَ وَإِدْرِيسَ وَذَا ٱلْكِفْلِۖ كُلٌّ مِّنَ ٱلصَّٰبِرِينَ ", "translation": "And [mention] Ishmael and Idrees and Dhul-Kifl; all were of the patient.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِسْمَٰعِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِʿعَٰmْsِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَإِدْرِيسَ", "translit": "َsyِrْdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَذَا", "translit": "āَhdَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكِفْلِ", "translit": "ِlْfِkْlٱ", "root": "كفل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كُلٌّ", "translit": "ٌّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰبِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِbَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 86, "text": "وَأَدْخَلْنَٰهُمْ فِى رَحْمَتِنَآۖ إِنَّهُم مِّنَ ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ ", "translation": "And We admitted them into Our mercy. Indeed, they were of the righteous.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَدْخَلْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْlَhkْdَaʾَw", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَحْمَتِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِtَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنَّهُم", "translit": "mُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ", "translit": "َnyِḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 87, "text": "وَذَا ٱلنُّونِ إِذ ذَّهَبَ مُغَٰضِبًا فَظَنَّ أَن لَّن نَّقْدِرَ عَلَيْهِ فَنَادَىٰ فِى ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ أَن لَّآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّآ أَنتَ سُبْحَٰنَكَ إِنِّى كُنتُ مِنَ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And [mention] the man of the fish [i.e., Jonah], when he went off in anger[1] and thought that We would not decree [anything] upon him.[2] And he called out within the darknesses,[3] \"There is no deity except You; exalted are You. Indeed, I have been of the wrongdoers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَذَا", "translit": "āَhdَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنُّونِ", "translit": "ِnwُّnlٱ", "root": "نون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِذ", "translit": "hdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ذَّهَبَ", "translit": "َbَhَّhd", "root": "ذهب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُغَٰضِبًا", "translit": "āًbِḍَٰغُm", "root": "غضب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَظَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَẓَf", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَّن", "translit": "nَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "نَّقْدِرَ", "translit": "َrِdْqَّn", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَنَادَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdāَnَf", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰmُlُّẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِلَٰهَ", "translit": "َhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "سُبْحَٰنَكَ", "translit": "َkَnَٰḥْbُs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "كُنتُ", "translit": "ُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 88, "text": "فَٱسْتَجَبْنَا لَهُۥ وَنَجَّيْنَٰهُ مِنَ ٱلْغَمِّۚ وَكَذَٰلِكَ نُۨجِى ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ ", "translation": "So We responded to him and saved him from the distress. And thus do We save the believers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱسْتَجَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَjgَtْsٱَf", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَنَجَّيْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْyَّjgَnَw", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْغَمِّ", "translit": "ِّmَغْlٱ", "root": "غمم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَكَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نُۨجِى", "translit": "àِjgُۨn", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 89, "text": "وَزَكَرِيَّآ إِذْ نَادَىٰ رَبَّهُۥ رَبِّ لَا تَذَرْنِى فَرْدًا وَأَنتَ خَيْرُ ٱلْوَٰرِثِينَ ", "translation": "And [mention] Zechariah, when he called to his Lord, \"My Lord, do not leave me alone [with no heir], while You are the best of inheritors.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَزَكَرِيَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّyِrَkَzَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَادَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdāَn", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَذَرْنِى", "translit": "àِnْrَhdَt", "root": "وذر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَرْدًا", "translit": "āًdْrَf", "root": "فرد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَأَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْوَٰرِثِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhtِrَٰwْlٱ", "root": "ورث"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 90, "text": "فَٱسْتَجَبْنَا لَهُۥ وَوَهَبْنَا لَهُۥ يَحْيَىٰ وَأَصْلَحْنَا لَهُۥ زَوْجَهُۥٓۚ إِنَّهُمْ كَانُوا۟ يُسَٰرِعُونَ فِى ٱلْخَيْرَٰتِ وَيَدْعُونَنَا رَغَبًا وَرَهَبًاۖ وَكَانُوا۟ لَنَا خَٰشِعِينَ ", "translation": "So We responded to him, and We gave to him John, and amended for him his wife. Indeed, they used to hasten to good deeds and supplicate[1] Us in hope and fear, and they were to Us humbly submissive.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱسْتَجَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَjgَtْsٱَf", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَوَهَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَhَwَw", "root": "وهب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَحْيَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَyْḥَy", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَصْلَحْنَا", "translit": "āَnْḥَlْṣَaʾَw", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "زَوْجَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَjgْwَz", "root": "زوج"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُسَٰرِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِrَٰsُy", "root": "سرع"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْخَيْرَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰrْyَhkْlٱ", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَيَدْعُونَنَا", "translit": "āَnَnwُʿعْdَyَw", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "رَغَبًا", "translit": "āًbَغَr", "root": "رغب"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَرَهَبًا", "translit": "āًbَhَrَw", "root": "رهب"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَكَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَنَا", "translit": "āَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "خَٰشِعِينَ", "translit": "َnyِʿعِhsَٰhk", "root": "خشع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 91, "text": "وَٱلَّتِىٓ أَحْصَنَتْ فَرْجَهَا فَنَفَخْنَا فِيهَا مِن رُّوحِنَا وَجَعَلْنَٰهَا وَٱبْنَهَآ ءَايَةً لِّلْعَٰلَمِينَ ", "translation": "And [mention] the one who guarded her chastity [i.e., Mary], so We blew into her [garment] through Our angel [i.e., Gabriel], and We made her and her son a sign for the worlds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّتِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِtَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَحْصَنَتْ", "translit": "ْtَnَṣْḥَaʾ", "root": "حصن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَرْجَهَا", "translit": "āَhَjgْrَf", "root": "فرج"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَنَفَخْنَا", "translit": "āَnْhkَfَnَf", "root": "نفخ"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رُّوحِنَا", "translit": "āَnِḥwُّr", "root": "روح"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَٰهَا", "translit": "āَhَٰnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱبْنَهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhَnْbٱَw", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ءَايَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لِّلْعَٰلَمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmَlَٰʿعْlِّl", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 92, "text": "إِنَّ هَٰذِهِۦٓ أُمَّتُكُمْ أُمَّةً وَٰحِدَةً وَأَنَا۠ رَبُّكُمْ فَٱعْبُدُونِ ", "translation": "Indeed this, your religion, is one religion,[1] and I am your Lord, so worship Me.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَٰذِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أُمَّتُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُtَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُمَّةً", "translit": "ًẗَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٰحِدَةً", "translit": "ًẗَdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَٱعْبُدُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُdُbْʿعٱَf", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 93, "text": "وَتَقَطَّعُوٓا۟ أَمْرَهُم بَيْنَهُمْۖ كُلٌّ إِلَيْنَا رَٰجِعُونَ ", "translation": "And [yet] they divided their affair [i.e., that of their religion] among themselves,[1] [but] all to Us will return.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَتَقَطَّعُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُʿعَّṭَqَtَw", "root": "قطع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَمْرَهُم", "translit": "mُhَrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كُلٌّ", "translit": "ٌّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَٰجِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِjgَٰr", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 94, "text": "فَمَن يَعْمَلْ مِنَ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ وَهُوَ مُؤْمِنٌ فَلَا كُفْرَانَ لِسَعْيِهِۦ وَإِنَّا لَهُۥ كَٰتِبُونَ ", "translation": "So whoever does righteous deeds while he is a believer - no denial will there be for his effort,[1] and indeed We [i.e., Our angels], of it, are recorders.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَمَن", "translit": "nَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَعْمَلْ", "translit": "ْlَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُؤْمِنٌ", "translit": "ٌnِmْwʾُm", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كُفْرَانَ", "translit": "َnāَrْfُk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لِسَعْيِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِyْʿعَsِl", "root": "سعي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَإِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كَٰتِبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbِtَٰk", "root": "كتب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 95, "text": "وَحَرَٰمٌ عَلَىٰ قَرْيَةٍ أَهْلَكْنَٰهَآ أَنَّهُمْ لَا يَرْجِعُونَ ", "translation": "And it is prohibited to [the people of] a city which We have destroyed that they will [ever] return[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَحَرَٰمٌ", "translit": "ٌmَٰrَḥَw", "root": "حرم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَرْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْrَq", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَهْلَكْنَٰهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhَٰnْkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَرْجِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِjgْrَy", "root": "رجع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 96, "text": "حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا فُتِحَتْ يَأْجُوجُ وَمَأْجُوجُ وَهُم مِّن كُلِّ حَدَبٍ يَنسِلُونَ ", "translation": "Until when [the dam of] Gog and Magog has been opened and they, from every elevation, descend", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فُتِحَتْ", "translit": "ْtَḥِtُf", "root": "فتح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَأْجُوجُ", "translit": "ُjgwُjgْaʾَy", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَأْجُوجُ", "translit": "ُjgwُjgْaʾَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَهُم", "translit": "mُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "حَدَبٍ", "translit": "ٍbَdَḥ", "root": "حدب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَنسِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِsnَy", "root": "نسل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 97, "text": "وَٱقْتَرَبَ ٱلْوَعْدُ ٱلْحَقُّ فَإِذَا هِىَ شَٰخِصَةٌ أَبْصَٰرُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ يَٰوَيْلَنَا قَدْ كُنَّا فِى غَفْلَةٍ مِّنْ هَٰذَا بَلْ كُنَّا ظَٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And [when] the true promise [i.e., the resurrection] has approached; then suddenly the eyes of those who disbelieved will be staring [in horror, while they say], \"O woe to us; we had been unmindful of this; rather, we were wrongdoers.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱقْتَرَبَ", "translit": "َbَrَtْqٱَw", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْوَعْدُ", "translit": "ُdْʿعَwْlٱ", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هِىَ", "translit": "َàِh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "شَٰخِصَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَṣِhkَٰhs", "root": "شخص"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَبْصَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰṣْbَaʾ", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَٰوَيْلَنَا", "translit": "āَnَlْyَwَٰy", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "غَفْلَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَlْfَغ", "root": "غفل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ظَٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَٰẓ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 98, "text": "إِنَّكُمْ وَمَا تَعْبُدُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ حَصَبُ جَهَنَّمَ أَنتُمْ لَهَا وَٰرِدُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, you [disbelievers] and what you worship other than Allāh are the firewood of Hell. You will be coming to [enter] it.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَعْبُدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdُbْʿعَt", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "حَصَبُ", "translit": "ُbَṣَḥ", "root": "حصب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٰرِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِrَٰw", "root": "ورد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 99, "text": "لَوْ كَانَ هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ ءَالِهَةً مَّا وَرَدُوهَاۖ وَكُلٌّ فِيهَا خَٰلِدُونَ ", "translation": "Had these [false deities] been [actual] gods, they would not have come to it, but all are eternal therein.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَوْ", "translit": "ْwَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَالِهَةً", "translit": "ًẗَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَرَدُوهَا", "translit": "āَhwُdَrَw", "root": "ورد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَكُلٌّ", "translit": "ٌّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "خَٰلِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 100, "text": "لَهُمْ فِيهَا زَفِيرٌ وَهُمْ فِيهَا لَا يَسْمَعُونَ ", "translation": "For them therein is heavy sighing, and they therein will not hear.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "زَفِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِfَz", "root": "زفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَسْمَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَmْsَy", "root": "سمع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 101, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ سَبَقَتْ لَهُم مِّنَّا ٱلْحُسْنَىٰٓ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ عَنْهَا مُبْعَدُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, those for whom the best [reward] has preceded from Us - they are from it far removed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "سَبَقَتْ", "translit": "ْtَqَbَs", "root": "سبق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْحُسْنَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَnْsُḥْlٱ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مُبْعَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَʿعْbُm", "root": "بعد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 102, "text": "لَا يَسْمَعُونَ حَسِيسَهَاۖ وَهُمْ فِى مَا ٱشْتَهَتْ أَنفُسُهُمْ خَٰلِدُونَ ", "translation": "They will not hear its sound, while they are, in that which their souls desire, abiding eternally.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسْمَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَmْsَy", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حَسِيسَهَا", "translit": "āَhَsyِsَḥ", "root": "حسس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱشْتَهَتْ", "translit": "ْtَhَtْhsٱ", "root": "شهو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَنفُسُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "خَٰلِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 103, "text": "لَا يَحْزُنُهُمُ ٱلْفَزَعُ ٱلْأَكْبَرُ وَتَتَلَقَّىٰهُمُ ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ هَٰذَا يَوْمُكُمُ ٱلَّذِى كُنتُمْ تُوعَدُونَ ", "translation": "They will not be grieved by the greatest terror,[1] and the angels will meet them, [saying], \"This is your Day which you have been promised\" -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَحْزُنُهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhُnُzْḥَy", "root": "حزن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْفَزَعُ", "translit": "ُʿعَzَfْlٱ", "root": "فزع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَكْبَرُ", "translit": "ُrَbْkَaʾْlٱ", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَتَتَلَقَّىٰهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhٰàَّqَlَtَtَw", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَوْمُكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkُmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تُوعَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَʿعwُt", "root": "وعد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 104, "text": "يَوْمَ نَطْوِى ٱلسَّمَآءَ كَطَىِّ ٱلسِّجِلِّ لِلْكُتُبِۚ كَمَا بَدَأْنَآ أَوَّلَ خَلْقٍ نُّعِيدُهُۥۚ وَعْدًا عَلَيْنَآۚ إِنَّا كُنَّا فَٰعِلِينَ ", "translation": "The Day when We will fold the heaven like the folding of a [written] sheet for the records. As We began the first creation, We will repeat it. [That is] a promise binding upon Us. Indeed, We will do it.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَطْوِى", "translit": "àِwْṭَn", "root": "طوي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَطَىِّ", "translit": "ِّàَṭَk", "root": "طوي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسِّجِلِّ", "translit": "ِّlِjgِّslٱ", "root": "سجل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِلْكُتُبِ", "translit": "ِbُtُkْlِl", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَمَا", "translit": "āَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَدَأْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْaʾَdَb", "root": "بدا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَوَّلَ", "translit": "َlَّwَaʾ", "root": "اول"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَلْقٍ", "translit": "ٍqْlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "نُّعِيدُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُdyِʿعُّn", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَعْدًا", "translit": "āًdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَٰعِلِينَ", "translit": "َnyِlِʿعَٰf", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 105, "text": "وَلَقَدْ كَتَبْنَا فِى ٱلزَّبُورِ مِنۢ بَعْدِ ٱلذِّكْرِ أَنَّ ٱلْأَرْضَ يَرِثُهَا عِبَادِىَ ٱلصَّٰلِحُونَ ", "translation": "And We have already written in the book [of Psalms][1] after the [previous] mention[2] that the land [of Paradise] is inherited by My righteous servants.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَتَبْنَا", "translit": "āَnْbَtَk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلزَّبُورِ", "translit": "ِrwُbَّzlٱ", "root": "زبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلذِّكْرِ", "translit": "ِrْkِّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَرِثُهَا", "translit": "āَhُhtِrَy", "root": "ورث"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عِبَادِىَ", "translit": "َàِdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 106, "text": "إِنَّ فِى هَٰذَا لَبَلَٰغًا لِّقَوْمٍ عَٰبِدِينَ ", "translation": "Indeed, in this [Qur’ān] is notification for a worshipping people.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَبَلَٰغًا", "translit": "āًغَٰlَbَl", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِّقَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَqِّl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَٰبِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِbَٰʿع", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 107, "text": "وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَٰكَ إِلَّا رَحْمَةً لِّلْعَٰلَمِينَ ", "translation": "And We have not sent you, [O Muḥammad], except as a mercy to the worlds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَٰكَ", "translit": "َkَٰnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَحْمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِّلْعَٰلَمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmَlَٰʿعْlِّl", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 108, "text": "قُلْ إِنَّمَا يُوحَىٰٓ إِلَىَّ أَنَّمَآ إِلَٰهُكُمْ إِلَٰهٌ وَٰحِدٌۖ فَهَلْ أَنتُم مُّسْلِمُونَ ", "translation": "Say, \"It is only revealed to me that your god is but one God; so will you be Muslims [in submission to Him]?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُوحَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَḥwُy", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنَّمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَٰهُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَٰهٌ", "translit": "ٌhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٰحِدٌ", "translit": "ٌdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَهَلْ", "translit": "ْlَhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَنتُم", "translit": "mُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مُّسْلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlْsُّm", "root": "سلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 109, "text": "فَإِن تَوَلَّوْا۟ فَقُلْ ءَاذَنتُكُمْ عَلَىٰ سَوَآءٍۖ وَإِنْ أَدْرِىٓ أَقَرِيبٌ أَم بَعِيدٌ مَّا تُوعَدُونَ ", "translation": "But if they turn away, then say, \"I have announced to [all of] you equally.[1] And I know not whether near or far is that which you are promised.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَوَلَّوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَّlَwَt", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَقُلْ", "translit": "ْlُqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَاذَنتُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُtnَhdāَء", "root": "اذن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَوَآءٍ", "translit": "ٍءٓāَwَs", "root": "سوي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَإِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَدْرِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِrْdَaʾ", "root": "دري"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَقَرِيبٌ", "translit": "ٌbyِrَqَaʾ", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَم", "translit": "mَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَعِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تُوعَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَʿعwُt", "root": "وعد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 110, "text": "إِنَّهُۥ يَعْلَمُ ٱلْجَهْرَ مِنَ ٱلْقَوْلِ وَيَعْلَمُ مَا تَكْتُمُونَ ", "translation": "Indeed, He knows what is declared of speech, and He knows what you conceal.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْجَهْرَ", "translit": "َrْhَjgْlٱ", "root": "جهر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلِ", "translit": "ِlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَيَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَyَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَكْتُمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmُtْkَt", "root": "كتم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 111, "text": "وَإِنْ أَدْرِى لَعَلَّهُۥ فِتْنَةٌ لَّكُمْ وَمَتَٰعٌ إِلَىٰ حِينٍ ", "translation": "And I know not; perhaps it[1] is a trial for you and enjoyment for a time.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَدْرِى", "translit": "àِrْdَaʾ", "root": "دري"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَعَلَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِتْنَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَnْtِf", "root": "فتن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَتَٰعٌ", "translit": "ٌʿعَٰtَmَw", "root": "متع"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "حِينٍ", "translit": "ٍnyِḥ", "root": "حين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 112, "text": "قَٰلَ رَبِّ ٱحْكُم بِٱلْحَقِّۗ وَرَبُّنَا ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ ٱلْمُسْتَعَانُ عَلَىٰ مَا تَصِفُونَ ", "translation": "[The Prophet (ﷺ)] has said, \"My Lord, judge [between us] in truth. And our Lord is the Most Merciful, the one whose help is sought against that which you describe.\"[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَٰلَ", "translit": "َlَٰq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱحْكُم", "translit": "mُkْḥٱ", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَرَبُّنَا", "translit": "āَnُّbَrَw", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْمُسْتَعَانُ", "translit": "ُnāَʿعَtْsُmْlٱ", "root": "عون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَصِفُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfِṣَt", "root": "وصف"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "تعالى اقترب للناس حسابهم وهم في غفلة معرضون ما يأتيهم من ذكر من ربهم محدث إلا استمعوه وهم يلعبون لاهية قلوبهم ويحك يا نفس إن كانت جراءتك على معصية الله لاعتقادك أن الله لا يراك فما أعظم كفرك وإن كان مع علمك باطلاعه عليك فما أشد وقاحتك وأقل حياءك ويحك يا نفس لو واجهك عبد من عبيدك بل أخ من إخوانك بما تكرهينه كيف كان غضبك عليه ومقتك له فبأي جسارة تتعرضين لمقت الله وغضبه وشديد عقابه أفتظنين أنك تطيقين عذابه هيهات هيهات جربي نفسك إن ألهاك البطر عن أليم عذابه فاحتبسي ساعة في الشمس أو في بيت الحمام أو قربي أصبعك من النار ليتبين قدر طاقتك أمتغترين بكرم الله وفضله واستغنائه عن طاعتك وعبادتك فما لك لا تعولين على كرم الله تعالى في مهمات دنياك فإذا قصدك عدو فلم تستنبطين الحيل في دفعه ولا تكلينه إلى كرم الله تعالى وإذا أرهقتك حاجة إلى شهوة من شهوات الدنيا مما لا ينقضي إلا بالدينار والدرهم فما لك تنزعين الروح في طلبها وتحصيلها من وجوه الحيل فلا تعولين على كرم الله تعالى حتى يعثر بك على كنز أو يسخر عبدا من عبيده فيحمل إليك حاجتك من غير سعي منك ولا طلب أفتحسبين أن الله كريم في الآخرة دون الدنيا وقد عرفت أن سنة الله لا تبديل لها وأن رب الآخرة والدنيا واحد وأن ليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى ويحك يا نفس ما أعجب نفاقك ودواعيك الباطلة فإنك تدعين الإيمان بلسانك وأثر النفاق ظاهر عليك ألم يقل لك سيدك ومولاك وما من دابة في الأرض إلا على الله رزقها وقال في أمر الآخرة وأن ليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى فقد تكفل لك بأمر الدنيا خاصة وصرفك عن السعى فيها فكذبته بأفعالك وأصبحت تتكالبين على طلبها تكالب المدهوش المستهتر ووكل أمر الآخرة إلى سعيك فأعرضت عنها إعراض المغرور المستحقر ما هذا من علامات الإيمان لو كان الإيمان باللسان فلم كان المنافقون في الدرك الأسفل من النار ويحك يا نفس كأنك لا تؤمنين بيوم الحساب وتظنين أنك إذا مت انفلت وتخلصت وهيهات أتحسبين أنك تتركين سدى ألم تكونى نطفة من مني يمنى ثم كنت علقة فخلق فسوى أليس ذلك بقادر على أن يحيى الموتى فإن كان هذا من إضمارك فما أكفرك وأجهلك أما تتفكرين أنه مماذا خلقك من نطفة خلقك فقدرك ثم السبيل يسرك ثم أماتك فأقبرك أفتكذبينه في قوله ثم إذا شاء أنشرك فإن لم تكونى مكذبة فما لك لا تأخذين حذرك ولو أن يهوديا أخبرك في ألذ أطعمتك بأنه يضرك في مرضك لصبرت عنه وتركته وجاهدت نفسك فيه أفكان قول الأنبياء المؤيدين بالمعجزات وقول الله تعالى في كتبه المنزلة أقل عندك تأثيرا من قول يهودى يخبرك عن حدس وتخمين وظن مع نقصان عقل وقصور علم والعجب أنه لو أخبرك طفل بأن في ثوبك عقربا لرميت ثوبك في الحال من غير مطالبة له بدليل وبرهان أفكان قول الأنبياء والعلماء والحكماء وكافة", "english": "...and intelligence and acumen, while you are the most stupid and foolish of people? Do you not recognize what lies before you of the Garden and the Fire, and that you are hastening to one of the two imminently? So why do you rejoice and laugh and occupy yourself with frivolity while you are being sought for this tremendous calamity? Perhaps today you will be snatched away, or tomorrow. I see that you regard death as distant, but God regards it as near. Do you not know that everything that is coming is near, and that the distant is only that which is not coming? Do you not know that death comes suddenly, without a prior messenger, without prior appointment or covenant? It does not come in one thing rather than another, nor in winter rather than summer, nor in summer rather than winter, nor in day rather than night, nor in night rather than day. It does not come in youth rather than old age, nor in old age rather than youth. Rather, every single breath could be the moment of sudden death. And if death does not come suddenly, then illness comes suddenly, and then leads to death. So why do you not prepare for death, when it is nearer to you than anything near? Do you not ponder the saying of God Most High: {«اقترب للناس حسابهم وهم في غفلة معرضون * ما يأتيهم من ذكر من ربهم محدث إلا استمعوه وهم يلعبون * لاهية قلوبهم»} (The reckoning for mankind has drawn near, while they turn away in heedlessness. No fresh reminder comes to them from their Lord but they listen to it while they play—their hearts distracted). Woe to you, O soul! If your audacity toward disobeying God stems from your belief that God does not see you, then how immense is your disbelief! And if it is despite your knowledge of His awareness of you, then how severe is your shamelessness and how little your modesty! Woe to you, O soul! If a slave of your slaves—nay, a brother among your brothers—were to confront you with what you detest, how would your anger and hatred toward him be? So with what audacity do you expose yourself to God's hatred, His wrath, and His severe punishment? Do you think you can endure His chastisement? Far be it, far be it! Test yourself—if heedless pride has distracted you from His painful punishment, then restrain yourself for an hour in the sun, or in the bathhouse, or bring your finger near the fire, so that your capacity may become clear to you. Are you deluded by God's generosity, His bounty, and His independence from your obedience and worship? Then why do you not rely on God's generosity in the important matters of your worldly life? When an enemy approaches you, why do you devise stratagems to repel him, rather than entrusting him to God's generosity? And when you are pressed by a need for one of the worldly desires that cannot be fulfilled except through dinar and dirham, why do you strain your very soul in seeking and procuring it through various contrivances, without relying on God's generosity—so that He might lead you to a treasure, or subjugate one of His slaves to carry your need to you without any effort or seeking on your part? Do you suppose that God is generous in the Hereafter but not in this world, when you already know that the sunnah of God admits no change, and that the Lord of the Hereafter and this world is One, and that {«ليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى»} (man shall have nothing but what he strives for)?", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1251", "section_title_en": "Section 1251 — The Admonition of the Soul Concerning the Nearness of Death and the Duplicity of False Hopes", "section_index": 1251, "anchors": "- Root: قرب (q-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity in place, time, or rank; nearness of relation or degree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for spatial, temporal, and relational nearness; here, divine nearness of death and the Hour—a metaphysical proximity, not measurable distance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for physical closeness; metaphorical (majāz) for what is inevitable and imminent—\"كل ما هو آت قريب\" (all that is coming is near).\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of animal life; also used metaphorically for lifelessness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as transition, not annihilation; linked to resurrection (baʿth) and divine power.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for bodily death; metaphorical for the \"death\" of heedlessness or the dead heart.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which desires and is accountable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul as the locus of blameworthy traits (al-nafs al-ammārah) versus the rational, discerning faculty; here, self-address in reproach.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the animate self; metaphorical for the inner voice of conscience rebuking the lower soul.\n- Root: طلب (ṭ-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Seeking, pursuing, demanding.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The active search for something, whether physical or conceptual; here, the soul's frantic pursuit of worldly desires versus its neglect of what is truly sought from it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for seeking provisions; metaphorical for the soul being \"sought\" by death—\"وأنت مطلوبة\" (you are being sought).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rhetorical separation between the preposition and its object to emphasize urgency, as in \"على القرب\" and \"أقرب إليك من كل قريب,\" where proximity is stressed through syntactic placement.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "المبين ويخبرنا بهذه الصفات من نعوت يوم الدين ثم يعرفنا غفلتنا ويقول اقترب الناس حسابهم وهم في غفلة معرضون ما يأتيهم من ذكر من ربهم محدث إلا استمعوه وهم يلعبون لاهية قلوبهم ثم يعرفنا قرب القيامة فيقول اقتربت الساعة وانشق القمر إنهم يرونه بعيدا ونراه قريبا وما يدريك لعل الساعة تكون قريبا ثم يكون أحسن أحوالنا أن نتخذ دراسة هذا القرآن عملا فلا نتدبر معانيه ولا ننظر في كثرة أوصاف هذا اليوم وأساميه ولا نستعد للتخلص من دواهيه فنعوذ بالله من هذه الغفلة إن لم يداركنا الله بواسع رحمته", "english": "So prepare yourself, O wretched one, for this Day of immense magnitude, of prolonged duration, of overwhelming dominion, whose appointed time is near — a Day when you shall see the heaven split asunder, and the stars scattered in its terror, and the shining planets extinguished, and the sun folded up, and the mountains set in motion, and the pregnant camels neglected, and the wild beasts gathered together, and the seas set ablaze, and the souls paired with their bodies, and the Hellfire ignited, and Paradise brought near, and the mountains pulverized, and the earth stretched out — a Day when you shall see the earth shaken with its quaking, and the earth casts forth its burdens. On that Day shall people issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds — a Day when the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with a single crushing. Then on that Day the Imminent Event shall come to pass, and the heaven shall split open, for on that Day it shall be frail, and the angels shall be at its edges, and eight shall bear the Throne of your Lord above them on that Day. On that Day you shall be presented — no secret of yours shall remain hidden. A Day when the mountains shall move and you shall see the earth laid bare — a Day when the earth shall be shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains crumbled to dust, becoming scattered particles — a Day when people shall be like scattered moths, and the mountains like carded wool — a Day when every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, and every pregnant female shall cast down her burden, and you shall see people drunk, yet they are not drunk, but the chastisement of God is severe — a Day when the earth shall be changed to other than this earth, and the heavens, and they shall appear before God, the One, the All-Compelling — a Day when the mountains shall be blown away as dust, leaving a level, bare plain, wherein you see no crookedness nor curve — a Day when you see the mountains and suppose them solid, yet they pass by like the passing of clouds — a Day when the heaven shall split open and become rosy like molten oil. Then on that Day no human nor jinn shall be asked about his sin. A Day when the disobedient shall be prevented from speech, and shall not be asked about their crimes, but shall be seized by their forelocks and their feet — a Day when every soul shall find what it did of good brought forth present, and what it did of evil, wishing that between it and that evil there were a far distance — a Day when every soul shall know what it has sent ahead, and shall witness what it has forwarded and what it has left behind — a Day when tongues shall be struck mute and the bodily members shall speak — a Day whose mention turned the hair of the Master of the Messengers white, when al-Ṣiddīq (may God be pleased with him) said to him: \"I see that you have turned white-haired, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Sūrat Hūd and its sisters have made me white-haired\" — namely, al-Wāqiʿah, al-Mursalāt, ʿAmma Yatasāʾalūn, and Idhā al-Shamsu Kuwwirat.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1339", "section_title_en": "The Names and Attributes of the Day of Resurrection — An Exhortation to the Negligent", "section_index": 1339, "anchors": "- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم: وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها، وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day as a temporal span, extended metaphorically to any epoch or era.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — theological usage denotes the appointed time of reckoning, magnified by divine attribution (أيام الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a measured solar period. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — days as epochal events; thus \"the Day\" is the supreme epochal event of divine justice.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النفس: الروح والذات — the self, soul, or very essence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: ويحذركم الله نفسه فنفسه: ذاته\" — nafs as the innermost reality of a being; in the eschatological context, souls are \"paired\" (زوجت) with bodies for judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul as life-principle. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — nafs as the total self, accountable in its entirety.\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء — the earth as the corporeal substratum beneath the heavens.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء... ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\" — the earth as the lowest register of creation, subject to total transformation on that Day.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical earth. Metaphorical: \"تأرض فلان: لزم الأرض\" — to cling low; on that Day the earth itself is leveled, flattened, and replaced.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated \"يوم\" clauses function as temporal restrictors (ẓurūf) binding each predicate to that single, all-encompassing moment; the accumulation of names (أسامي) serves not as mere repetition but as focused predication upon the multifarious realities of a single event.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قوله عز وجل وهم في غفلة معرضون وفي قوله فأعرض عمن تولى عن ذكرنا ولم يرد إلا الحياة الدنيا وفي قوله تعالى ومن لم يتب فأولئك هم الظالمون إلى غير ذلك من الآيات وكان داخلا في معنى قوله عز وجل ومنهم أميون لا يعلمون الكتاب إلا أماني يعني التلاوة المجردة وقوله عز وجل وكأين من آية في السموات والأرض يمرون عليها وهم عنها معرضون لأن القرآن هو المبين لتلك الآيات في السموات والأرض ومهما تجاوزها ولم يتأثر بها كان معرضا عنها ولذلك قيل إن من لم يكن متصفا بأخلاق القرآن فإذا قرأ القرآن ناداه", "english": "And at the mention of God, His attributes, and His names, he bows low in humility before His majesty and in apprehension of His greatness. And at the mention of the disbelievers' sayings — those things impossible for God, Mighty and Majestic, such as their attributing to Him a son or a consort — he lowers his voice and inwardly recoils in shame at the ugliness of their speech. And at the description of Paradise, his innermost being surges forth with longing for it. And at the description of the Fire, his limbs tremble in fear of it. For when the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said to Ibn Mas'ud, \"Recite to me,\" he said: \"I began reciting Surah al-Nisa'. When I reached {فكيف إذا جئنا من كل أمة بشهيد وجئنا بك على هؤلاء شهيدا} [How then shall it be when We bring from every nation a witness, and We bring you as a witness against these?] (4:41), I saw his two eyes overflowing with tears. He then said to me: 'That is enough for you now.'\" And this was because the witnessing of that very state had wholly overwhelmed his heart.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 230", "section_title_en": "The States of the Heart During Qur'anic Recitation — The Condition of the True Recieter", "section_index": 230, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of trembling fear, trepidation of the heart\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the inward apprehension of fear; cited in {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a man fearful, a people fearful; the state of the heart's trembling before divine majesty\n- Root: وصف (w-ṣ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Describing a thing by its attributes and qualities\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوصف: ذكر الشيء بحليته ونعته\" — description is the mention of a thing by its adornment and attribute; may be true or false\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: \"وجهها يصف الحسن\" — metaphorical usage where the face itself \"describes\" beauty; likewise, the tongue may \"describe\" falsehood, as in {ولا تقولوا لما تصف ألسنتكم الكذب}\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, reaching full measure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which attains completion; \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — fulfilled the covenant without breach\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham; Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة إذا زاد عليها\" — surpassed the hundred\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The separation between the governing preposition and its object by intervening clauses, as in \"فلما بلغت {فكيف إذا جئنا...}\" where the temporal clause governs the narrative sequence", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "7": [{"arabic": "الدين} وقوله عز وجل {فاسألوا أهل الذكر إن كنتم لا تعلمون} وأما الأخبار فقوله صلى الله عليه وسلم من سلك طريقا يطلب فيه علما سلك الله به طريقا إلى الجنة (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الملائكة لتضع أجنحتها لطالب العلم رضاء بما يصنع (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم لأن تغدو فتتعلم بابا من العلم خير من أن تصلي مائة ركعة (3) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم باب من العلم يتعلمه الرجل خير له من الدنيا وما فيها (4) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم اطلبوا العلم ولو بالصين (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم طلب العلم ف", "english": "As for the Quranic verses, there is the saying of God Most High: {«فلولا نفر من كل فرقة منهم طائفة ليتفقهوا في الدين»} — \"Why then did not a party from every section of them go forth, that they might gain deep comprehension in the religion?\" — and His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {«فاسألوا أهل الذكر إن كنتم لا تعلمون»} — \"So ask the people of remembrance if you do not know.\"", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 7", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of Seeking Knowledge (Faḍīlat al-Taʿallum)", "section_index": 7, "anchors": "- Root: فقه (Fiqh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"comprehension\" and \"understanding\" — the penetration into the inner meaning of speech, distinguished from mere hearing (samāʿ).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the theological register, *tafaqquh* (تفقه) is the deep, exhaustive comprehension of the religious sciences — not mere memorization but the attainment of the realities of juridico-theological matters.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes intellectual penetration; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to any deep discernment of subtle meanings.\n- Root: سأل (Saʾala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To ask, to inquire, to seek information — the root act of petitioning for knowledge from its possessor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Asking (*suʾāl*) is the divinely mandated means for the ignorant to access knowledge, as per {«فاسألوا أهل الذكر»} — it is the key that unlocks the treasuries of knowledge.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the verbal request; the Prophet's statement \"العلم خزائن مفاتيحها السؤال\" employs a metaphor (majāz) likening knowledge to locked treasuries and questioning to their keys.\n- Root: طلب (Ṭalab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To seek, to pursue, to search out a thing — whether a physical object or a meaning.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطلب: الفحص عن وجود الشيء، عينا كان أو معنى\" — the earnest investigation into the existence of a thing, whether concrete or conceptual. In the religious context, *ṭalab al-ʿilm* is the pursuit of that which revives the religion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the physical pursuit of a quarry; the ḥadīth \"من سلك طريقا يطلب فيه علما\" employs the metaphor of the *path* (ṭarīq) as the means of pursuit, and the divine recompense as the *path to Paradise*.\n- Root: غدو (Ghadā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To go out early in the morning, to set forth at dawn — the time of vigorous, purposeful departure.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage denotes the morning journey; in the saying of Abū al-Dardāʾ, \"الغدو إلى طلب العلم\" is metaphorically elevated to the rank of *jihād* — the early morning departure for knowledge is a martial expedition of the soul.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (al-Faṣl bayna al-Jār wa-al-Majrūr) — The syntactic principle governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb, as in \"سلك الله به طريقا إلى الجنة,\" where the divine agency is interposed between the act and its recompense, indicating the causal nexus.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "10": [{"arabic": "عليكم من الكتاب والحكمة يعظكم به} وقال عز وجل لقد أنزلنا إليكم كتابا فيه", "english": "I say: These meanings are buried treasures that are not unveiled except to the divinely aided ones. To this 'Ali — may God be pleased with him — alluded by his saying: \"The Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — did not confide to me anything that he concealed from the people, except that God — mighty and majestic — grants to a servant understanding (fahm) of His Book. So let one be eager in seeking that understanding.\" (1) And Ibn Mas'ud — may God be pleased with him — said: \"Whoever desires the knowledge of the first and the last, let him stir up the Qur'an.\" And the greatest of the sciences of the Qur'an lie beneath the names of God — mighty and majestic — and His attributes, for most of creation have grasped from them only matters befitting their understandings and have not stumbled upon their depths.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 228", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 228: On the Inner Meanings of the Qur'an and the Removal of Obstacles to Understanding", "section_index": 228, "anchors": "- Root: فهم (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension, discernment, the faculty by which one grasps meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — a disposition by which one verifies the realities of what is perceived; distinct from mere hearing or transmission\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: swift apprehension; Metaphorical: the divine gift of insight into hidden meanings, as in the verse {ففهمناها سليمان}\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transformation from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart as that which is perpetually turned and overturned between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical turning; Metaphorical: the heart as the seat of understanding that is veiled or illuminated, rusted or polished\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hearing, the auditory faculty\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السمع: قوة في الأذن به يدرك الأصوات\" — extends metaphorically to comprehension and obedience; \"أو ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" — one who casts his hearing while present\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: auditory perception; Metaphorical: attentive obedience and receptive understanding\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — governing the precise delimitation of predicates such as \"إنما يتذكر أولوا الألباب\" where restriction confines the action to its proper subject", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "18": [{"arabic": "وفي مثله قوله تعالى ولكم الويل مما تصفون وفي الخبر إنما أخاف على أمتي زلة عالم وجدال منافق في القرآن (1) ومنها أن تكون عنايته بتحصيل العلم النافع في الآخرة المرغب في الطاعات مجتنبا للعلوم التي يقل نفعها ويكثر فيها الجدال والقيل والقال فمثال من يعرض عن علم الأعمال ويشتغل بالجدال مثل رجل مريض به علل كثيرة وقد صادف طبيبا حاذقا في وقت ضيق يخشى فواته فاشتغل بالسؤال عن خاصية العقاقير والأدوية وغرائب الطب وترك مهمه الذي هو مؤاخذ به وذلك محض السفه وقد روي أن رجلا جاء رسول الله صلى", "english": "Know that the scholar who does not act upon his knowledge is condemned by the Book, the Sunnah, and the sayings of the sages. God, exalted be He, said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا لم تقولون ما لا تفعلون * كبر مقتا عند الله أن تقولوا ما لا تفعلون»} (\"O you who believe, why do you say what you do not do? Most hateful it is to God that you say what you do not do\"). And He said: {«أتأمرون الناس بالبر وتنسون أنفسكم»} (\"Do you command people to righteousness while you forget yourselves?\"). And He said: {«ومن الناس من يعجبك قوله في الحياة الدنيا ويشهد الله على ما في قلبه وهو ألد الخصام»} (\"And among people is he whose speech pleases you in the worldly life, and he calls God to witness what is in his heart, yet he is the fiercest of opponents\").", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 54", "section_title_en": "Section 54", "section_index": 54, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "19": [{"arabic": "وهو أحد والآصال التي ذكرها الله تعالى إذ قال ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال وإذا سجد لله عز وجل الجمادات فكيف يجوز أن يغفل العبد العاقل عن أنواع العبادات الورد السادس إذا دخل وقت العصر دخل وقت الورد السادس وهو الذي أقسم الله تعالى به فقال تعالى {والعصر} هذا أحد معنيي الآية وهو المراد بالآصال في أحد التفسيرين وهو العشي المذكور في قوله {وعشيا} وفي قوله {بالعشي والإشراق} وليس في هذا الورد صلاة إلا أربع ركعات بين الأذان والإقامة كما سبق في الظهر ثم يصلي الفرض ويشتغل بالأقسام الأربعة المذكورة", "english": "I say: And just as knowledge and remembrance are the nourishment of the heart, which can never be cut off from it, so too the measure of moderation is this; and deficiency from it may lead to the disturbance of the body — except for one who accustoms himself to wakefulness gradually, for he may train himself upon it without disturbance. And this litany (wird) is among the longest of the litanies and the most delightful for the worshippers, and it is one of the \"evenings and mornings\" (al-āṣāl) which God Most High mentioned when He said: {«ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال»} \"And to God prostrates whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, and their shadows, in the mornings and the evenings\" (Q. 13:15). And when inanimate objects prostrate to God, Mighty and Majestic, how then can the intelligent servant be heedless of the varieties of worship?", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 261", "section_title_en": "The Seventh Daily Litany (Al-Wird al-Sābiʿ): The Time of Sunset — The Completion of the Day's Litanies and Self-Accounting", "section_index": 261, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes arriving at water, seeking it, and drinking from it; also the portion or share of water allotted for drinking at a specific time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — the core meaning is the intentional approach to water; the litany (wird) is the appointed portion of devotional recitation, likened to the daily share of water that a person must take to sustain life.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the act of arriving at water. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ورد علي كتاب سرني مورده\" — a book arrived upon me whose arrival pleased me; likewise, the wird is the arrival of the appointed time for worship, and the soul's approach to the fountain of divine remembrance.\n- Root: عصر (ʿaṣr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes pressing, squeezing, and extracting; also the time of afternoon, so called because it is the pressing of the day's end.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ʿaṣr is the latter part of the day; God swore by it in {«والعصر»} (Q. 103:1), indicating its great merit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the afternoon time. Metaphorical: the pressing of one's life — as the day is pressed at its end, so the life of man is pressed at its term.\n- Root: غرب (gharaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes disappearance, absence, and setting; the sun's setting (ghurūb) is its concealment from the eye.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The setting of the sun marks the boundary between the day's works and the night's; it is the terminus of the visible world's diurnal cycle.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the sun's descent below the horizon. Metaphorical: the setting of the sun of life — the death of man, after which there is no rising.\n- Root: حاسب (ḥāsaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes reckoning, counting, and taking account; muḥāsabah is the mutual reckoning between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-accounting (muḥāsabat al-nafs) is the soul's reckoning with itself over its deeds before the Day of Reckoning, for \"one who reckons himself before he is reckoned\" is among the wise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the arithmetic counting of sums. Metaphorical: the soul's audit of its own works, weighing the day's deeds against the standard of the Sacred Law.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb, as in the construction \"فسبحان الله حين تمسون\" where the adverbial clause is interposed to emphasize the temporal specification of the act of glorification.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Dhikr"}, {"arabic": "وعبادته التي هو مشغول بها لا ينتقل إلى غيرها ولا يفتر عنها لا يستكبرون عن عبادته ولا يستحسرون يسبحون الليل والنهار لا يفترون ومفتاح مزيد الدرجات هي الصلوات قال الله عز وجل قد أفلح المؤمنون الذين هم في صلاتهم خاشعون فمدحهم بعد الإيمان بصلاة مخصوصة وهي المقرونة بالخشوع ثم ختم أوصاف المفلحين بالصلاة أيضا فقال تعالى والذين هم على صلواتهم يحافظون ثم قال تعالى في ثمرة تلك الصفات أولئك هم الوارثون الذين يرثون الفردوس هم فيها خالدون فوصفهم بالفلاح أولا وبوراثة الفردوس آخرا وما عندي أن هذرمة اللسان مع غفلة القلب تنتهي إلى هذا الحد ولذلك قال الله عز وجل في أضدادهم ما سلككم في س", "english": "For the friends of God (awliyāʾ Allāh) who are granted unveiling of the dominion of the heavens and the earth (malakūt al-samāwāt wa-al-arḍ) and the secrets of lordship (asrār al-rubūbiyyah) — they are unveiled only in prayer, especially in prostration (sujūd), for the servant draws near to his Lord (Mighty and Majestic) through prostration. For this reason, He said, Most High: {«And prostrate and draw near»} (Q 96:19). And the unveiling of each worshiper is only to the measure of his purity from the turbidities of this world. It varies in strength and weakness, in abundance and scarcity, and in clarity and obscurity — until some are unveiled the very thing itself (al-shayʾ bi-ʿaynihi), while others are unveiled its likeness (al-shayʾ bi-mithālihi), just as some were shown this world in the form of a corpse, and Satan in the form of a dog crouching upon it, calling people to it.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 140", "section_title_en": "The Prayer of the Humble and the Unveiling of the Unveiled — Section 140", "section_index": 140, "anchors": "- Root: خ ش ع (kh-sh-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of humility, submissiveness, and lowering oneself; the physical posture of one who is abased before a greater power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, khushūʿ is the state of the heart's stillness and trembling before divine majesty, manifesting in the body's composure during ritual prayer (ṣalāh).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) refers to the physical lowering of the gaze and voice; metaphorical (majāz) extends to the soul's inner quietude and awe before God.\n- Root: ك ش ف (k-sh-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remove a covering, unveil, disclose; the act of stripping away an obstruction to reveal what lies beneath.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, kashf denotes divine removal of veils (kashf al-ḥijāb) — the uncovering of unseen realities (ghayb) to the heart, as in {«We have removed from you your covering»} (Q 50:22).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncovering a physical object; metaphorical: the disclosure of spiritual truths (mukāshafah) to the purified heart, a technical term in Sufi epistemology.\n- Root: ص ل و (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes supplication, blessing, and the act of ritual prayer; also the burning of fire (iṣlāʾ), suggesting the consuming fervor of devotion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ṣalāh is the act of turning toward God in worship, encompassing both the physical rites and the inner orientation of the soul; its perfection lies in the presence of the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the performance of the prescribed ritual; metaphorical: the soul's \"burning\" with longing and its purification through the fire of sincere devotion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of restrictive particles to single out the specific quality (khushūʿ) as the defining attribute of the believers' prayer, distinguishing it from mere mechanical performance.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}], "20": [{"arabic": "يؤمرون ويسبحون الليل والنهار لا يفترون والراكع منهم راكع أبدا والساجد منهم ساجد أبدا والقائم قائم أبدا لا اختلاف في أفعالهم ولا فتور ولكل واحد مقام معلوم لا يتعداه وطاعتهم لله تعالى من حيث لا مجال للمخالفة فيهم يمكن أن تشبه بطاعة أطرافك لك فإنك مهما جزمت الإرادة بفتح الأجفان لم يكن للجفن الصحيح تردد واختلاف في طاعتك مرة ومعصيتك أخرى بل كأنه منتظر لأمرك ونهيك ينفتح وينطبق متصلا بإشارتك فهذا يشبهه من وجه ولكن يخالفه من وجه إذ الجفن لا علم له بما يصدر منه من الحركة فتحا وإطباقا والملائك", "english": "If you say: Why were these acts not delegated to a single angel, without my needing seven angels? — For the wheat also requires one to grind it first, then one to separate the bran from it and cast away the refuse second, then one to pour water upon it third, then one to knead it fourth, then one to cut it into round balls fifth, then one to roll it into broad flat loaves sixth, then one to affix it to the oven seventh — yet a single man may undertake all of that and suffice for it. So why were not the works of the angels inwardly like the works of men outwardly? — Know that the constitution (khilqah) of the angels differs from the constitution of men. There is not a single one of them but is singular in attribute (waḥdānī al-ṣifah), having in him no admixture or composition whatsoever; hence there cannot issue from each of them save a single act. And to this points His saying, the Exalted: {«وما منا إلا له مقام معلوم»} (And there is not one of us but has a known station) {Q. 37:164}. Therefore there is among them no rivalry and no combat. Rather, their example, in the determination of the rank of each one of them and his act, is the example of the five senses: for sight does not vie with hearing in the perception of sounds, nor does smell vie with them, nor do the two contend with smell over it. And it is not like the hand and the foot — for you may grasp feebly with the toes of your foot and thereby vie with the hand — nor like when you strike another with your head and thereby vie with the hand which is the instrument of striking. Nor is it like the single man who undertakes by himself the grinding, the kneading, and the baking, for this is a kind of crookedness and deviation from equity, whose cause is the diversity of man's attributes and the diversity of his impulses: for he is not singular in attribute, and therefore he is not singular in act. For this reason we see man obeying Allah at one time and disobeying Him at another, due to the diversity of his impulses and attributes. And that is not possible in the nature of the angels; rather, they are created disposed to obedience — there is no scope for disobedience in their case. Hence, necessarily: {«لا يعصون الله ما أمرهم ويفعلون ما يؤمرون»} (They do not disobey Allah in what He commands them, and they do what they are commanded) {Q. 66:6}, and {«يسبحون الليل والنهار لا يفترون»} (They glorify night and day without ceasing) {Q. 21:20}. The one who bows among them is bowing forever, the one who prostrates among them is prostrating forever, and the one who stands is standing forever — there is no variation in their acts and no weariness, and for each one is a known station (maqām maʿlūm) which he does not overstep. And their obedience to Allah the Exalted — inasmuch as there is no scope for opposition in them — may be likened to the obedience of your limbs to you: for whenever you have resolved with firm will upon opening the eyelids, the sound eyelid has no hesitation or variation — obeying you at one time and disobeying you at another — but rather it is as though it awaits your command and your prohibition, opening and closing continuously at your signal. This resembles it in one respect but differs from it in another: for the eyelid has no knowledge of what issues from it of movement, opening and shutting, whereas the angels are living beings, knowing what they do.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 1012", "section_title_en": "The Division and Distribution of Nutritive Substance — The Earthly Angels and the Hierarchy of Divine Bounty", "section_index": 1012, "anchors": "- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي\" — the one who governs by command and prohibition; dominion and sovereign power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي في الجمهور\" — the angel (malak) as a designated agent of divine governance, distinct from human composite agency.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"لله الملك والملكوت\" — absolute sovereignty. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ملك عليه أمره إذا استولى عليه\" — not applicable here; the term is used in its literal theological sense of delegated divine administration.\n- Root: وحد (waḥada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الواحد: ما لا جزء له\" — that which has no part; indivisible unity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الواحد في الحقيقة هو الشيء الذي لا جزء له البتة\" — the truly one is that which has no composition whatsoever; hence \"وحداني الصفة\" (singular in attribute) means uncompounded in nature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو واحد، وهم وحدان\" — numerical unity. Here applied analogically to angelic nature: absolute simplicity without admixture.\n- Root: نعم (naʿima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة\" — the goodly state; benefaction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة... والنعمة للجنس تقال للقليل والكثير\" — bounty as a genus encompassing both apparent (ẓāhirah) and inward (bāṭinah) blessings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"جلت نعمة الله ونعماؤه\" — manifest divine beneficence. Metaphorical: \"خفّت نعامتهم: ذهبوا\" — not applicable; the term is literal throughout.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — governing the periodic suspension of clauses (e.g., \"فإن قلت... فاعلم أن\") where the protasis is held in suspense before the apodosis, requiring the reader to grasp the full syntactic arc before resolution.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "عن عبادته ولا يستحسرون يسبحون الليل والنهار لا يفترون ومفتاح مزيد الدرجات هي الصلوات قال الله عز وجل قد أفلح المؤمنون الذين هم في صلاتهم خاشعون فمدحهم بعد الإيمان بصلاة مخصوصة وهي المقرونة بالخشوع ثم ختم أوصاف المفلحين بالصلاة أيضا فقال تعالى والذين هم على صلواتهم يحافظون ثم قال تعالى في ثمرة تلك الصفات أولئك هم الوارثون الذين يرثون الفردوس هم فيها خالدون فوصفهم بالفلاح أولا وبوراثة الفردوس آخرا وما عندي أن هذرمة اللسان مع غفلة القلب تنتهي إلى هذا الحد ولذلك قال الله عز وجل في أضدادهم ما سلككم في", "english": "For the friends of God (awliyāʾ Allāh) who are granted unveiling of the dominion of the heavens and the earth (malakūt al-samāwāt wa-al-arḍ) and the secrets of lordship (asrār al-rubūbiyyah) — they are unveiled only in prayer, especially in prostration (sujūd), for the servant draws near to his Lord (Mighty and Majestic) through prostration. For this reason, He said, Most High: {«And prostrate and draw near»} (Q 96:19). And the unveiling of each worshiper is only to the measure of his purity from the turbidities of this world. It varies in strength and weakness, in abundance and scarcity, and in clarity and obscurity — until some are unveiled the very thing itself (al-shayʾ bi-ʿaynihi), while others are unveiled its likeness (al-shayʾ bi-mithālihi), just as some were shown this world in the form of a corpse, and Satan in the form of a dog crouching upon it, calling people to it.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 140", "section_title_en": "The Prayer of the Humble and the Unveiling of the Unveiled — Section 140", "section_index": 140, "anchors": "- Root: خ ش ع (kh-sh-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of humility, submissiveness, and lowering oneself; the physical posture of one who is abased before a greater power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, khushūʿ is the state of the heart's stillness and trembling before divine majesty, manifesting in the body's composure during ritual prayer (ṣalāh).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) refers to the physical lowering of the gaze and voice; metaphorical (majāz) extends to the soul's inner quietude and awe before God.\n- Root: ك ش ف (k-sh-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remove a covering, unveil, disclose; the act of stripping away an obstruction to reveal what lies beneath.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, kashf denotes divine removal of veils (kashf al-ḥijāb) — the uncovering of unseen realities (ghayb) to the heart, as in {«We have removed from you your covering»} (Q 50:22).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncovering a physical object; metaphorical: the disclosure of spiritual truths (mukāshafah) to the purified heart, a technical term in Sufi epistemology.\n- Root: ص ل و (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes supplication, blessing, and the act of ritual prayer; also the burning of fire (iṣlāʾ), suggesting the consuming fervor of devotion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ṣalāh is the act of turning toward God in worship, encompassing both the physical rites and the inner orientation of the soul; its perfection lies in the presence of the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the performance of the prescribed ritual; metaphorical: the soul's \"burning\" with longing and its purification through the fire of sincere devotion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of restrictive particles to single out the specific quality (khushūʿ) as the defining attribute of the believers' prayer, distinguishing it from mere mechanical performance.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "22": [{"arabic": "الكفار فعمدة أدلة المتكلمين في التوحيد قوله تعالى {لو كان فيهما آلهة إلا الله لفسدتا} وفي النبوة {وإن كنتم في ريب مما نزلنا على عبدنا فأتوا بسورة من مثله} وفي البعث {قل يحييها الذي أنشأها أول مرة} إلى غير ذلك من الآيات والأدلة ولم تزل الرسل صلوات الله عليهم يحاجون المنكرين ويجادلونهم قال تعالى {وجادلهم بالتي هي أحسن} فالصحابة رضي الله عنهم أيضا كانوا يحاجون المنكرين ويجادلون ولكن عند الحاجة وكانت الحاجة إليه قليلة في زمانهم وأول من سن دعوة المبتدعة بالمجادلة إلى الحق علي ابن أبي طالب رضي الله عنه إذ بعث ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما إ", "english": "The mention of the proof (ḥujjah), the demand for it, and the search after it — how could these be prohibited, when God — may He be exalted — has said: {«قُلْ هَاتُوا بُرْهَانَكُمْ»} (Say: Bring forth your proof — Qur'an 2:111), and He — mighty and majestic — has said: {«لِيَهْلِكَ مَنْ هَلَكَ عَنْ بَيِّنَةٍ وَيَحْيَا مَنْ حَيَّ عَنْ بَيِّنَةٍ»} (So that he who perishes may perish upon clear evidence, and he who lives may live upon clear evidence — Qur'an 8:42), and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«هَلْ عِنْدَكُمْ مِنْ سُلْطَانٍ بِهَذَا»} (Have you any authority for this? — Qur'an 10:68), meaning a proof and demonstration, and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«قُلْ فَلِلَّهِ الْحُجَّةُ الْبَالِغَةُ»} (Say: To God belongs the conclusive proof — Qur'an 6:149), and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِي حَاجَّ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي رَبِّهِ»} (Have you not considered him who disputed with Abraham concerning his Lord? — Qur'an 2:258) up to His words: {«فَبُهِتَ الَّذِي كَفَرَ»} (So the one who disbelieved was confounded — Qur'an 2:258).", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 77", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 77: On the Permissibility of Theological Discourse (Kalām) and Its Early Practice", "section_index": 77, "anchors": "- Root: ح-د-ث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥudūth: the coming-to-be of a thing after it was not; al-ḥadath: the novel event; al-ḥīdāth: origination.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥudūth: a thing's becoming after it was not, whether accident or substance; iḥdāth: bringing it into existence. The origination of substances belongs to God alone.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: 'He is a recent event among events.' Metaphorical: 'They became topics of conversation (aḥādīth).'\"\n- Root: ح-ا-ج (ḥ-ā-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥājah: need of a thing, with desire for it; its plural: ḥājāt and ḥawā'ij.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥājah ilā al-shay': poverty toward it with love of it. God said: {«إِلَّا حَاجَةً فِي نَفْسِ يَعْقُوبَ»} (except a need in Jacob's soul — Qur'an 12:68).\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: need and want. The 'ḥājah' is that which one seeks to obtain.\"\n- Root: ك-و-ن (k-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Kāna: to be, to exist; takwīn: bringing into being from non-existence.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-kawn: existence after non-existence; the muḥdath (originated thing) is that brought forth after it was not.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: being and becoming. Metaphorical: the continuous states of affairs.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Al-Faṣl bayna al-Jār wa-al-Majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of separating a preposition from its object when the intervening clause is complete in meaning, as in the verse's structure of argumentation where the proof (ḥujjah) stands independently before its conclusion.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "23": [{"arabic": "العبودية مشتركون نوديت من سرادقات الجلال لا تجاوز حد الأدب فإنه لا يسئل عما يفعل وهم يسئلون ولعمري تأدب اللسان والظاهر مما يقدر عليه الأكثرون فأما تأدب السر عن إضمار الاستبعاد بهذا الاختلاف الظاهر في التقريب والإبعاد والإشقاء والإسعاد مع بقاء السعادة والشقاوة أبد الآباد فلا يقوى عليه إلا العلماء الراسخون في العلم ولهذا قال الخضر عليه السلام لما سئل عن السماع في المنام إنه الصفو الزلال الذي لا يثبت عليه إلا أقدام العلماء لأنه محرك لأسرار القلوب ومكامنها ومشوش لها تشويش السكر المدهش الذي يكاد يحل عقدة الأدب عن السر إلا ممن عصمه الله تعالى", "english": "Among the masters of ecstatic finding (arbāb al-wajd), there is one whom a state overwhelms like intoxicating drunkenness (sukr mudhish), so his tongue loosens with reproach (ʿitāb) toward God Almighty, and he finds objectionable (yastankiru) His overwhelming (iqtihār) of hearts and His apportioning of noble states differentially. For He is the One who elects (mustaṣfā) the hearts of the veracious (ṣiddīqīn) and distances the hearts of the deniers and the deluded (jāḥidīn wa-l-maghrūrīn). There is no preventer of what He gives, and no giver of what He withholds. He did not cut off divine success (tawfīq) from the disbelievers due to some prior transgression, nor did He extend to the Prophets, peace be upon them, His success and the light of His guidance due to some preceding means. Rather, He said: {«وَلَقَدْ سَبَقَتْ كَلِمَتُنَا لِعِبَادِنَا الْمُرْسَلِينَ»} \"And Our word has already preceded for Our servants, the messengers\" [Al-Ṣāffāt/171]. And He said, Mighty and Majestic: {«وَلَٰكِنْ حَقَّ الْقَوْلُ مِنِّي لَأَمْلَأَنَّ جَهَنَّمَ مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ أَجْمَعِينَ»} \"But the word from Me has taken effect: I will surely fill Hell with jinn and people all together\" [Al-Sajdah/13]. And He said, Exalted: {«إِنَّ الَّذِينَ سَبَقَتْ لَهُمْ مِنَّا الْحُسْنَىٰ أُولَٰئِكَ عَنْهَا مُبْعَدُونَ»} \"Indeed, those for whom the best [reward] has preceded from Us — they are from it far removed\" [Al-Anbiyāʾ/101].", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 503", "section_title_en": "The Peril of Listening (Samāʿ) for the Novice and the Reality of Divine Transcendence Beyond Change", "section_index": 503, "anchors": "- Root: لون (L-W-N)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of color, hue, and physical appearance; also used for types and kinds.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اللون معروف، وينطوي على الأبيض والأسود وما يركب منهما، ويقال: تلون: إذا اكتسى لوناً غير اللون الذي كان له\" — indicating acquisition of a new state or attribute.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): physical coloring and change of hue. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"عنده لون من الثياب: صنف منه\" — a type, kind, or varying disposition; hence \"mutalawwin\" (متلون) as one who shifts between states and moods.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn over, overturn, transform, or redirect from one face to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the continuous turning and overturning of a thing; God as \"Muqallib al-Qulūb\" (Turner of hearts).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical overturning. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who manages and disposes affairs; divine action of disposing hearts.\n- Root: حول (H-W-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Change, transformation, power, and capacity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الحول تغير الشيء وانفصاله عن غيره\" — change and separation from another state; \"يحول بين المرء وقلبه\" {«الأنفال/ 24»} — divine interposition between a man and his heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: passage of time, transformation. Metaphorical: \"حال عليه الحول\" — the changing of conditions; \"حائل\" as that which has altered.\n- Root: قرب (Q-R-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness in place, time, or rank.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القرب والبعد يتقابلان... ويستعمل ذلك في المكان، وفي الزمان، وفي النسبة، وفي الحظوة، والرعاية، والقدرة\" — including nearness in honor and station (zulfā).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical proximity. Metaphorical: \"فلان يقرب أمراً لا يتسهل له\" — drawing near in rank and favor; divine drawing near (taqrīb) and distancing (ibʿād).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the meaning remains clear, paralleling how divine attributes may be qualified without compromising essential unity.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "ولا معقب لحكمه يضل من يشاء ويهدي من يشاء لا يسئل عما يفعل وهم يسألون ويدل عليه من النقل قول الأمة قاطبة ما شاء كان وما لم يشأ لم يكن وقول الله عز وجل أن لو يشاء الله لهدى الناس جميعا وقوله تعالى ولو شئنا لآتينا كل نفس هداها ويدل عليه من جهة العقل أن المعاصي والجرائم إن كان الله يكرهها ولا يريدها وإنما هي جارية على وفق إرادة العدو إبليس لعنه الله مع أنه عدو لله سبحانه والجاري على وفق إرادة العدو أكثر من الجاري على وفق إرادته تعالى فليت شعري كيف يستجيز المسلم أن يرد ملك الجبار ذي الجلال والإكرام إلى رتبة لو ردت إل", "english": "As for the movement, it is a creation of the Lord — exalted is He — an attribute of the servant, and an acquisition of his. For it was created as empowered (maqdūrah) by a power that is His attribute, and the movement has a relation to another attribute called power; thus, by virtue of that relation, it is termed \"acquisition.\" And how could it be pure compulsion (jabr maḥḍ), when he necessarily perceives the distinction between the empowered movement and the involuntary tremor (al-raʿdah al-ḍarūriyyah)? Or how could it be a creation of the servant, when he does not encompass knowledge of the details of the parts of acquired movements nor their numbers? And when both extremes are refuted, nothing remains except moderation in belief (al-iqtiṣād fī al-iʿtiqād) — namely, that it is empowered by the power of God — exalted is He — by way of origination, and by the power of the servant in another mode of attachment (taʿlīq) expressed as acquisition.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 93", "section_title_en": "The Second Foundation: God's Sole Creation of Servants' Movements Does Not Negate Their Being Acquired by Servants", "section_index": 93, "anchors": "- Root: قدر (q-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of measuring, determining, and empowering; al-Farahidi notes its application to capacity and divine decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القدرة\" when attributed to humans denotes a state enabling one to perform an act; when attributed to God, it negates incapacity. Absolute power belongs solely to God; for others, it is always restricted and particularized.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو قادر مقتدر ذو قدرة ومقدرة\" — one possessing power. Metaphorical: \"فرس بعيد القدر\" — a horse of far-reaching stride, extending the root to scope and reach.\n- Root: كسب (k-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Acquisition, earning, and what one gains; al-Farahidi links it to amassing and profiting.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Acquisition (al-kasb) in theological terms denotes an act's relation to the servant whereby responsibility attaches, while its origination remains divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: earning and gaining. Metaphorical: the theological sense of acquired act (al-faʿl al-iktisābī) — a relational attribution distinct from creation.\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring and smoothing; al-Farahidi notes its application to fashioning and proportioning.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — origination from nothing (ibdāʿ) or from something, always upon a measured determination.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: measuring before cutting. Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation is bringing into existence upon a measure required by wisdom.\n- Root: وجب (w-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Being due, necessary, or obligatory; al-Farahidi notes its legal and ontological senses.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجوب: الثبوت\" — necessary being is that whose non-existence entails an impossibility; obligation is that whose omission warrants blame.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وجب البيع\" — the sale became binding. Metaphorical: \"هو يأكل الوجبة\" — eating once daily, extending to fixed determination.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — The particle \"إنما\" restricts predication, as in \"إنما هو إله واحد,\" confining the attribute to its subject and negating it from others. This governs the Ashʿarī restriction of creative power to God alone while affirming acquired power for servants through a distinct relational mode.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "فإنه لا يسأل عما يفعل وهم يسألون وليت شعري بما يجيب المعتزلي في قوله إن الأصلح واجب عليه في مسألة نعرضها عليه وهو أن يفرض مناظرة في الآخرة بين صبي وبين بالغ ماتا مسلمين فإن الله سبحانه يزيد في درجات البالغ ويفضله على الصبي لأنه تعب بالإيمان والطاعات بعد البلوغ ويجب عليه ذلك عند المعتزلي فلو قال الصبي يا رب لم رفعت منزلته علي فيقول لأنه بلغ واجتهد في الطاعات ويقول الصبي أنت أمتني في الصبا فكان يجب عليك أن تديم حياتي حتى أبلغ فأجتهد فقد عدلت عن العدل في التفضل عليه بطول العمر ل", "english": "**The Fifth Principle**: It is permissible for God—glorified be He—to charge His creatures with what they cannot bear (mā lā yuṭīqūnahu), contrary to the Muʿtazilites. For were that not permissible, then the petition to avert it would be impossible—yet they have petitioned it, saying: {«Rabbanā wa lā tuḥammilnā mā lā ṭāqata lanā bih»} \"Our Lord, do not burden us with what we have no capacity for\" {Q. 2:286}. And because God—exalted be He—informed His Prophet (peace be upon him) that Abū Jahl would not believe him, then commanded him to command Abū Jahl to believe him in all his statements. Now among Abū Jahl's statements was that he would not believe him—so how could he believe him in [the statement] that he would not believe him? Is this anything other than the impossible in its very existence?", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 94", "section_title_en": "The Fifth and Sixth Principles: On the Impossibility of Obligation Upon God and the Permissibility of What Reason Deems Impossible", "section_index": 94, "anchors": "- Root: وجب (w-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"being established, fixed, and necessary\"; al-Kitab al-Ayn notes its application to ritual obligations (wujūb al-iḥrām).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-wujūb is thubūt (establishment). The wājib is said in two senses: (1) in contrast to the possible—that whose non-existence entails a contradiction; (2) that whose omission merits blame—either by reason (as knowing God's unity) or by revelation (as ritual duties).\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Wajaba lī ʿalayhi kadhā\" (such is incumbent upon him); \"awjabahu ʿalā nafsihi\" (he made it incumbent upon himself). Metaphorical: \"huwa ya'kulu al-wajbah\" (he eats but once a day)—the root shifts from legal necessity to a single, fixed act.\n- Root: ملك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sovereignty and possession; al-Kitab al-Ayn distinguishes mālik (owner) from malik (king), both deriving from absolute discretionary disposal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-mulk: one who disposes by command and prohibition over a community—this pertains to governance of rational beings. Ownership (milk) is of two kinds: possession itself, and the power to dispose.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Malaktu al-shay'\" (I possessed the thing). Metaphorical: \"Malaktu nafsī ʿinda al-ghaḍab\" (I mastered myself at anger)—dominion over one's own domain.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition of the conditional clause (al-sharṭ) between subject and predicate—governing the logical structure of \"if X were permissible, then Y would follow,\" where the protasis restricts the apodosis.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "25": [{"arabic": "وجل في كلام يردده في نفسه كما لو سمع وهو يقول {إنني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا فاعبدني} فإنه ما كان ينبغي أن يفهم منه ذلك إلا على سبيل الحكاية الصنف الثاني من الشطح كلمات غير مفهومة لها ظواهر رائقة وفيها عبارات هائلة وليس وراءها طائل إما أن تكون غير مفهومة عند قائلها بل يصدرها عن خبط في عقله وتشويش في خياله لقلة إحاطته بمعنى كلام قرع سمعه وهذا هو الأكثر وإما أن تكون مفهومة له ولكنه لا يقدر على تفهيمها وإيرادها بعبارة تدل على ضميره لقلة ممارسته للعلم وعدم تعلمه طريق التعبير عن المعاني بالألفاظ الرشيقة ولا فائدة لهذا الجنس من ا", "english": "Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may God be pleased with him, said to his son ʿUmar — having heard him composing sajʿ — \"This is what makes me dislike you. I will never fulfill your need until you repent.\" This was said when his son had come to him seeking a favor. And the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah when he spoke in sajʿ of three words: \"Beware of sajʿ, O Ibn Rawāḥah.\" Thus, the forbidden sajʿ is the artificially contrived kind that exceeds two words. For this reason, when a man spoke concerning the blood-money for a fetus — saying, \"How shall we pay blood-money for one who neither drank, nor ate, nor cried out, nor wailed? Such a one's blood is void\" — the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, replied: \"Sajʿ like the sajʿ of the bedouins!\"", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 31", "section_title_en": "On the Etiquette of Preaching: The Condemnation of Artificial Sajʿ, Excessive Poetry, and the Perils of Shataḥ and Taʿwīl", "section_index": 31, "anchors": "- Root: سجع (sajʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Rhymed prose; from the root meaning \"to coo\" (as a dove), indicating a measured, melodious cadence in speech.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological discourse, sajʿ is permissible when natural but blameworthy when artificially cultivated (takalluf), as it shifts focus from meaning to ornamentation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — natural rhythmic speech; Metaphorical (majāz) — the \"sajʿ of the bedouins\" (sajʿ al-aʿrāb) as a pejorative for empty, affected eloquence.\n- Root: شعر (shiʿr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Poetry; from the root denoting perception and fine discernment (shuʿūr), as the poet grasps subtle meanings.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Poetry is blameworthy when it stirs base appetites, but praiseworthy when it conveys wisdom (ḥikmah), as in the prophetic saying \"Indeed from poetry there is wisdom.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — metered, rhymed speech; Metaphorical — the \"poetry\" of the heart's inclination, where listeners project their own passions onto what they hear.\n- Root: شطح (shaṭaḥa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To overflow, to gush forth; originally of a water-skin bursting, then applied to ecstatic utterances that overflow the bounds of conventional discourse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Sufi terminology, shataḥ refers to utterances arising from overwhelming spiritual states (ḥāl) that, when voiced to the unworthy, cause corruption.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical overflowing; Metaphorical — the soul's overflow in speech that exceeds the speaker's intentional control.\n- Root: أول (taʿwīl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To return something to its origin or end; interpretation that traces a word back to its intended meaning.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Taʿwīl is legitimate only when supported by textual (naqlī) or rational (ʿaqlī) evidence; arbitrary taʿwīl that severs words from their established senses destroys religious trust.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the apparent sense (ẓāhir); Metaphorical — the inner sense (bāṭin), which is only valid when the literal sense is impossible or when transmitted authority sanctions it.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The text repeatedly uses restrictive constructions (innamā, laysa...illā) to delimit permissible preaching, poetry, and interpretation, distinguishing the praiseworthy from the blameworthy with syntactic precision.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "28": [{"arabic": "وعنه العبارة بقوله تعالى {ولا يشفعون إلا لمن ارتضى} وبقوله {من ذا الذي يشفع عنده إلا بإذنه} وبقوله {ولا تنفع الشفاعة عنده إلا لمن أذن له} وبقوله {فما تنفعهم شفاعة الشافعين} وإذا انقسمت الذنوب إلا ما يشفع فيه وإلا ما لا يشفع فيه وجب الخوف والإشفاق لا محالة ولو كان ذنب تقبل فيه الشفاعة لما أمر قريشا بالطاعة ولما نهى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فاطمة رضي الله عنها عن المعصية ولكان يأذن لها في اتباع الشهوات لتكمل لذاتها في الدنيا ثم يشفع لها في الآخرة لتكمل لذاتها في الآخرة فالانهماك في الذنوب وترك ا", "english": "If you say: \"He, peace and blessings be upon him, said after his words to Fāṭimah and Ṣafiyyah: 'I cannot avail you against God in anything, except that you have a tie of kinship which I will moisten with its moisture'\" — a ḥadīth [reported as]: \"Do you hope for my intercession, while the Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib do not hope for it?\" — narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Awsaṭ from the narration of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar, and in it is Uṣayrim ibn Ḥawshab from Isḥāq ibn Wāṣil, and both are extremely weak — so that indicates that he will single out his relatives for intercession.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 882", "section_title_en": "Section 882 — On the Limits of Intercession, the Vices of Pride in Lineage, Numbers, Wealth, and Opinion, and Their Treatment", "section_index": 882, "anchors": "- Root: شفع (Sh-F-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is pairing or joining; intercession (shafā'ah) is the joining of one's plea to another's cause.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Intercession is the seeking of benefit for another; its acceptance is contingent upon divine permission and good pleasure, as restricted by the Qur'anic qualifiers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) is the act of making a number even (from watr, odd); metaphorical (majāz) is the mediator's plea before authority.\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-J-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Astonishment arising from an unknown cause; self-admiration (ʿujb) is a blameworthy state of being pleased with oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العجب والتعجب: حالة تعرض للإنسان عند الجهل بسبب الشيء\" — a state that overtakes a person due to ignorance of the cause; hence, it is impossible regarding God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a wondrous thing; metaphorical: the one who performs marvels (taʿjābah). Self-admiration is the root of every error and innovation.\n- Root: رأي (R-'-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Opinion, view, or judgment formed through reflection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ra'y is the considered opinion that may err or hit the mark; it is blameworthy when it becomes an object of ʿujb (self-admiration).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sight/vision; metaphorical: intellectual insight. The ḥadīth \"إعجاب كل ذي رأي برأيه\" (the admiration of every man of opinion for his own view) is cited as a sign of the age's corruption.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Qualification) — The conditional clauses (in-sharṭ) governing intercession, e.g., {إلا لمن ارتضى} and {إلا بإذنه}, impose necessary restrictions that negate any absolute reliance on intercession.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "يسبحون الليل والنهار لا يفترون وهم من خشيته مشفقون فمتى زال الإشفاق والحذر مما سبق به القضاء في الأزل وينكشف عند خاتمة الأجل غلب الأمن من مكر الله وذلك يوجب الكبر وهو سبب الهلاك فالكبر دليل الأمن والأمن مهلك والتواضع دليل الخوف وهو مسعد فإذن ما يفسده العابد بإضمار الكبر واحتقار الخلق والنظر إليهم بعين الاستصغار أكثر مما يصلحه بظاهر الأعمال فهذه معارف بها يزال داء الكبر عن القلب لا غير إلا أن النفس بعد هذه المعرفة قد تضمر التواضع وتدعي البراءة من الكبر وهي كاذبة فإذا وقعت الواقعة عادت إلى طبع", "english": "...so he gave some of it in charity and fed his family with some of it. He then returned, saying: \"This is good, but it is not like devoting oneself entirely to obedience of God.\" He came again in a dream a second time, and it was said to him: \"Go to so-and-so, the cobbler, and say to him: 'What is this yellowness on your face?'\" So he went to him and asked him. He replied: \"I have not seen anyone among mankind except that it occurred to me that he would be saved — but you, I [saw] that you would perish.\" The worshiper said: \"By this [very sign]...\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 872", "section_title_en": "The Cure for Conceit (Kibr): Trials of the Soul and the Path to Humility", "section_index": 872, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (wajila)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of fear, trepidation, and trembling of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance of reverential awe before divine majesty, distinct from mere terror; it is the state of the heart that accompanies acts of obedience when mindful of their acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes inward trembling; metaphorical (Majaz) extension applies to the soul's anxious state regarding its ultimate destiny.\n- Root: شفق (shafaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The thin, delicate edge of twilight; also compassion mixed with fear.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإشفاق: عناية مختلطة بخوف\" — solicitous care blended with fear; when followed by (من) the sense of fear predominates, as in the cited verses.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal root refers to the redness of dusk; metaphorical usage denotes the soul's anxious solicitude, as in \"مشفقون\" (those who are apprehensive).\n- Root: كبر (kabira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of greatness, magnitude, and seniority.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — relative terms; in moral theology, kibr (conceit) is the soul's self-inflation before others and before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) denotes physical or quantitative greatness; metaphorical (Majaz) usage — \"كبر علي ذلك\" (it weighed heavily upon him) — extends to the heart's refusal to submit to truth, which is the essence of blameworthy pride.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"إنما\" restricts and confines the predicate, as in \"فإذن ما يفسده العابد...\" where the structure emphasizes that the corruption caused by hidden pride exceeds any apparent rectification.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "32": [{"arabic": "ثم مسح بها سبلته (1) أى تجاوزها من غير فكر وذم المعرضين عنها فقال وجعلنا السماء سقفا محفوظا وهم عن آياتها معرضون فأى نسبة لجميع البحار والأرض إلى السماء وهى متغيرات على القرب والسموات صلاب شداد محفوظات عن التغير إلى أن يبلغ الكتاب أجله ولذلك سماه الله تعالى محفوظا فقال وجعلنا السماء سقفا محفوظا وقال سبحانه وبنينا فوقكم سبعا شدادا {وقال} أأنتم أشد خلقا أم السماء بناها رفع سمكها فسواها فانظر إلى الملكوت لترى عجائب العز والجبروت ولا تظنن أن معنى النظر إلى الملكوت بأن تمد البصر إليه فترى زرقة السماء وضوء الكواكب وتفرقها فإن البهائم تشاركك فى هذا النظر فإن كان", "english": "I say — and of the heaven with its woven structure, and the heaven and what built it; and His saying, may He be exalted: {«And the sun and its morning brightness, and the moon when it follows it»} (Q 91:1-2); and His saying, may He be exalted: {«Nay, I swear by the slinking stars, the runners, the sinkers»} (Q 81:15-16); and His saying, may He be exalted: {«And the star when it falls — nay, I swear by the positions of the stars, and verily it is a mighty oath, if you but knew»} (Q 56:75-76). You have thus come to know that the wonders of the filthy drop of semen — the first and last generations have fallen short of comprehending them. So if God swore by such things, what is your assumption concerning that by which God, may He be exalted, swore, and upon which He made provisions dependent, and to which He annexed them, saying, may He be exalted: {«And in the heaven is your provision and that which you are promised»} (Q 51:22)? And He praised those who reflect upon it, saying: {«And they reflect upon the creation of the heavens and the earth»} (Q 3:191). And the Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessing be upon him, said: \"Woe to him who recites this verse and then wipes his moustache with it\" — that is, passes over it without reflection. And He censured those who turn away from it, saying: {«And We made the heaven a protected roof, yet from its signs they turn away»} (Q 21:32). So what comparison is there between all the seas and the earth, on the one hand, and the heaven, on the other — given that the former are subject to rapid changes, while the heavens are solid, mighty, and preserved from change until the Book reaches its appointed term? For this reason God, may He be exalted, called it \"protected,\" saying: {«And We made the heaven a protected roof»} (Q 21:32); and He said, glory be to Him: {«And We built above you seven mighty ones»} (Q 78:12); and He said: {«Are you stronger in creation or the heaven? He built it, He raised high its roof and fashioned it»} (Q 79:27-28).", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 1278", "section_title_en": "On Contemplating the Kingdom of the Heavens (التفكر في ملكوت السموات)", "section_index": 1278, "anchors": "- Root: م ل ك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الملك: القوة على التصرف، والملكوت: زيادة الميم للمبالغة كالرهبوت والرحموت\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الملكوت: عالم الغيب الذي لا تدركه الحواس، والملك: عالم الشهادة المحسوس\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الملكوت مجاز عن باطن الملك وسرّه، لا عن ظاهره المحسوس\n- Root: س م و (sama)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السماء: كل ما علاك فأظلك، وأصلها السمو بمعنى الارتفاع\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): السماء: الجرم المقابل للأرض، وتستعمل بمعنى العلو والملكوت الأعلى\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"سقفا محفوظا\" حقيقة في الحفظ عن التغير والاضمحلال\n- Root: غ ي ب (ghayb)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الغيب: ما استتر عن الحواس، ويقال للشيء غيب باعتباره بالناس لا بالله تعالى، فإنه لا يغيب عنه شيء\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الغيب والملكوت مترادفان في اصطلاح القرآن لما غاب عن الأبصار\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — جواز تقديم المعمول وتأخيره لمراعاة البلاغة والمقام", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "35": [{"arabic": "وقال بعض الصوفية كنت أقرأ ليلة هذه الآية {كل نفس ذائقة الموت} فجعلت أرددها فإذا هاتف يهتف بي كم تردد هذه الآية فقد قتلت أربعة من الجن ما رفعوا رءوسهم إلى السماء منذ خلقوا وقال أبو علي المغازلي للشبلي ربما تطرق سمعي آية من كتاب الله تعالى فتجذبني إلى الإعراض عن الدنيا ثم أرجع إلى أحوالي وإلى الناس فلا أبقى على ذلك فقال ما طرق سمعك من القرآن فاجتذبك به إليه فذلك عطف منه عليك ولطف منه بك وإذا ردك إلى نفسك فهو شفقة منه عليك فإنه لا يصلح لك إلا التبري من الحول والقوة في التوجه إليه وسمع رجل من أه", "english": "Some of the Sufis said: \"One night I was reciting this verse: {كُلُّ نَفْسٍ ذَائِقَةُ الْمَوْتِ} «Every soul shall taste death» [Q. 3:185]. I kept repeating it, and lo, a caller cried out to me: 'How long will you repeat this verse? You have killed four of the jinn who have not raised their heads to the sky since they were created.'\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 512", "section_title_en": "On the Ecstatic States (Wajd) Produced by Qur'anic Recitation and the Superiority of Song (Ghinā') in Stirring the Heart", "section_index": 512, "anchors": "- Root: وجد (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of finding, attaining, and also denoting the emotional state of ecstasy or rapture that \"finds\" the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the theological context, 'wajd' is the state of spiritual finding—a direct, overwhelming apprehension of divine realities that bypasses discursive reasoning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes \"finding\" a lost object; metaphorical (Majaz) usage denotes the heart \"finding\" its spiritual state, leading to agitation and ecstasy.\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faculty of hearing; also the ear itself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing can be literal (perception of sound) or figurative (understanding, obedience, acceptance). The heart's \"hearing\" is a deeper comprehension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"سمعته\" (I heard him). Metaphorical: \"سمع الله لمن حمده\" (God answers/he accepts the one who praises Him), indicating a responsive, transformative hearing.\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death; the opposite of life.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death is the cessation of the soul's governance over the body, a separation. In the spiritual context, it can be a metaphorical death to worldly desires.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death. Metaphorical: \"أحيا الله البلد الميت\" (God revives the dead land), indicating spiritual revival or the death of the ego.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to emphasize that the stirring of the heart is exclusively through what is appropriate to its state, as in the phrase \"إنما المحرك لما في القلب ما يناسبه\" (The only thing that moves what is in the heart is that which is suitable to it).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "37": [{"arabic": "وقال عز وجل {خلق الإنسان من عجل} وقال تعالى {وكان الإنسان عجولا} وقال لنبيه صلى الله عليه وسلم {ولا تعجل بالقرآن من قبل أن يقضي إليك وحيه} وهذا لأن الأعمال ينبغي أن تكون بعد التبصرة والمعرفة والتبصرة تحتاج إلى تأمل وتمهل والعجلة تمنع من ذلك وعند الاستعجال يروج الشيطان شره على الإنسان من حيث لا يدري فقد روي أنه لما ولد عيسى بن مريم عليه السلام أتت الشياطين إبليس فقالوا أصبحت الأصنام قد نكست رءوسها فقال هذا حادث مكانكم فطار حتى أتي خافقي الأرض فلم يجد شيئا ثم وجد عيسى عليه السلام ق", "english": "And among its great gateways is satiety from food, even if it be lawful and pure. For satiety strengthens the lusts, and the lusts are the weapons of Satan. It has been related that Iblis appeared to Yahya ibn Zakariyya (peace be upon them both), and he saw upon him attachments of every kind of thing. He said to him: \"O Iblis, what are these attachments?\" He said: \"These are the lusts by which I overcome the son of Adam.\" He said: \"Is there anything among them [that you use against me]?\" He said: \"Perhaps I make you satiated, and so I weigh you down from prayer and from remembrance.\" He said: \"Is there anything other than that?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Upon God, I shall never fill my belly with food ever again.\" Iblis then said to him: \"And upon God, I shall never advise a Muslim ever again.\"", "book_title": "Disciplining the Soul", "section_title_ar": "Section 601", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on Hope and Fear, Section 601: The Great Gateways of Satan's Ingress into the Heart", "section_index": 601, "anchors": "- Root: كذب (kadhaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To lie, to falsify; the core sense of contradicting reality with speech.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, denotes the antithesis of ṣidq (truthfulness) — a rupture in the correspondence between inward conviction and outward expression.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — utterance contrary to fact; metaphorical (majāz) — the breaking of a promise or covenant, as when Satan says \"they will not belie me\" meaning they will not break their word to me.\n- Root: حرص (ḥaraṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Intense eager desire for something, covetousness; the soul's grasping beyond its need.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A blameworthy trait in the Quranic lexicon, being the unrestrained appetite of the soul for worldly increase; it is the root of Adam's transgression.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the eager pursuit of provision; metaphorical — the soul's insatiable craving that overrides rational restraint.\n- Root: شبع (shabi'a)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Satiety, fullness of the stomach; the cessation of hunger.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the moral theology of the Revival, satiety is not merely physical but a state that invigorates the lower appetites (shahawāt), which are the very weapons of Satan.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — fullness of the belly; metaphorical — the satiety that breeds heedlessness (ghaflah) and weighs down the limbs from obedience.\n- Root: عجل (ʿajila)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Haste, urgency; acting before the proper time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"العجلة: طلب الشيء وتحريه قبل أوانه، وهو من مقتضى الشهوة، فلذلك صارت مذمومة في عامة القرآن حتى قيل: «العجلة من الشيطان»\" — Haste is the seeking of a thing before its due time, and it proceeds from lust, therefore it is blameworthy throughout the Quran.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — swiftness of movement; metaphorical — acting without deliberation (tathabbut), which opens the door to Satan's deception.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase when the intervening clause is complete in itself, as in the periodic constructions of this section where conditional clauses and parenthetical citations are interposed between the subject (the gateways) and their explanatory predicates.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Disciplining the Soul'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "38": [{"arabic": "على الكافرين غير يسير} وقال تعالى {ويقولون متى هذا الوعد إن كنتم صادقين ما ينظرون إلا صيحة واحدة تأخذهم وهم يخصمون فلا يستطيعون توصية ولا إلى أهلهم يرجعون ونفخ في الصور فإذا هم من الأجداث إلى ربهم ينسلون قالوا يا ويلنا من بعثنا من مرقدنا هذا ما وعد الرحمن وصدق المرسلون} فلو لم يكن بين يدي الموتى إلا هول تلك النفخة لكان ذلك جديرا بأن يتقى فإنها نفخة وصيحة يصعق بها من في السموات والأرض يعنى يموتون بها إلا من شاء الله وهو بعض الملائكة ولذلك قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كيف أنعم وصاحب الصور قد التقم القرن وحنى الجبهة", "english": "The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted said: \"The son of Adam has reviled Me, and he had no right to revile Me; and he has denied Me, and he had no right to deny Me. As for his reviling of Me, it is his saying that I have a son. And as for his denial of Me, it is his saying: 'He will never bring me back as He created me at first.'\" {«شتمني ابن آدم وما ينبغي له أن يشتمني وكذبني وما ينبغي له أن يكذبني...»}", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1335", "section_title_en": "On the Terrors of the Trumpet-Blast and the Awakening for the Resurrection", "section_index": 1335, "anchors": "- Root: نفخ (N-F-Kh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To blow into a thing, to inflate; the breath of wind entering a vessel or body.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The divine act of breathing spirit into creation {«ونفخت فيه من روحي»}; here, the eschatological blast that annihilates and then revives all beings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage of blowing into the Trumpet (الصور); metaphorical extension to the \"blast of spring\" reviving the earth—here applied to the two cosmic blasts of death and resurrection.\n- Root: صور (S-W-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Form, image, shape; also a horn or trumpet (the instrument of the blast).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The perceptible form of bodies and the intelligible form of souls; here, the Trumpet (الصور) as the instrument of divine command that re-forms and re-shapes creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the horn (القرن) held by Israfil; metaphorical: the \"inclination\" (صورة) of hearts toward terror and anticipation.\n- Root: موت (M-W-T)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of life; the state of the corpse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as the separation of the soul from the body; the \"first death\" of the blast and the \"second death\" of the wicked.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; metaphorical: the \"death\" of the heart through heedlessness, and the reviving of dead lands by rain—here, the reviving of bodies by the second blast.\n- Root: قبر (Q-B-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The grave as the abode of the dead; burial.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The grave as the intermediate state (barzakh) before resurrection; {«حتى زرتم المقابر»} as a metaphor for death.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the grave and its inhabitants; metaphorical: the \"raising of the nose\" (قبراه) of the proud—contrasted here with the humility of the dead rising from their graves.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to emphasize that true faith is not mere verbal assent but heartfelt conviction, as in \"إنما فتور البواطن... لقلة الفهم\" (The languor of the inner selves is only due to the lack of understanding).", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "47": [{"arabic": "ما بأنفسهم إن الله لا يظلم مثقال ذرة وإن تك حسنة يضاعفها وإن كان مثقال حبة من خردل أتينا بها وكفى بنا حاسبين فمن كان حبه في الدنيا رجاءه لنعيم الجنة والحور العين والقصور مكن من الجنة ليتبوأ منها حيث يشاء فيلعب مع الولدان ويتمتع بالنسوان فهناك تنتهي لذته في الآخرة لأنه إنما يعطى كل إنسان في المحبة ما تشتهيه نفسه وتلذ عينه ومن كان مقصده رب الدار ومالك الملك ولم يغلب عليه إلا حبه بالإخلاص والصدق أنزل في مقعد", "english": "...twenty years.\nAnd al-Junayd said: \"The mark of the lover is the constancy of vigor and cheerfulness with a passion that wearies his body but does not weary his heart.\"\nAnd some of them said: \"Labor borne out of love — languor does not enter into it.\"\nAnd one of the scholars said: \"By God, no lover of God is ever fully quenched by His obedience, even if he were to attain to the most immense of means.\"\nAll of this and its like is found in observable experience, for the passionate lover does not find burdensome the pursuit of his beloved's desire, and he savors with his heart the service of the beloved even if it is arduous upon his body. And whenever his body falls short, the dearest of things to him is that his capacity should return to him and that incapacity should depart from him, so that he may occupy himself with it. Such, likewise, is the love of God the Exalted — for every love that becomes dominant inevitably subdues whatever is beneath it. So whoever's beloved is dearer to him than his sloth abandons sloth in serving him; and if the beloved is dearer to him than his wealth, he spends his wealth in his love.\nAnd it was said to one of the lovers — who had expended his very self and his wealth until nothing remained to him — \"What was the cause of your state in love?\" He said: \"One day I heard a lover who had secluded himself with his beloved, saying: 'By God, I love you with my whole heart, while you turn away from me with your whole face.' The beloved said to him: 'If you truly love me, what will you spend upon me?' He said: 'O my master, I put you in possession of all that I own, and then I spend upon you my very spirit until it is destroyed.'\" So I said: \"This is a creature loving a creature, and a slave loving a slave — how then with slaves loving the Worshipped One?\" All of this is by His cause.\nAnd among them is that he be compassionate toward all of God's servants, merciful to them, and severe against all of God's enemies — and against everyone who commits something of what God dislikes — as God the Exalted said: {«أشداء على الكفار رحماء بينهم»} \"Harsh against the disbelievers, merciful among themselves.\" No reproach of any reproacher seizes him, and no diverting force turns him from wrath for God's sake. With this God described His friends, saying: \"Those who are enamored with My love as a child is enamored with a thing, and who take refuge in My remembrance as the eagle takes refuge in its nest, and who rage for the sake of My sanctities as the leopard rages when provoked — for then he cares not whether the people are few or many.\"\nSo consider this example: the child, when enamored with a thing, does not part from it at all; if it is taken from him, he has no occupation but weeping and crying out until it is returned to him. If he sleeps, he takes it with him into his clothes; when he awakens, he returns and clings to it. Whenever he is parted from it, he weeps; whenever he finds it, he laughs. Whoever contends with him over it, he hates; whoever gives it to him, he loves. As for the leopard, he does not master himself at the moment of rage, until he reaches such a pitch of fury that he destroys himself. These, then, are the marks of love. Whoever's marks are perfected in him, his love is perfected and his affection is purified — so in the Hereafter his drink is clear and his draught is sweet.\nBut whoever's love is mingled with the love of other than God, he delights in the Hereafter in proportion to his love, for his drink is mingled with a measure of the drink of the Near Ones (al-muqarrabūn), as God the Exalted said concerning the righteous (al-abrār): {«إن الأبرار لفي نعيم»} \"Verily the righteous are in bliss,\" {ثم قال} then He said: {«يسقون من رحيق مختوم * ختامه مسك وفي ذلك فليتنافس المتنافسون * ومزاجه من تسنيم * عينا يشرب بها المقربون»} \"They are given to drink of sealed nectar, whose seal is musk — and in that let the competitors compete — and its mixture is of tasnīm, a spring from which the Near Ones drink.\" So when the drink of the righteous is sweetened by the admixture of the pure drink which belongs to the Near Ones...\nAnd \"drink\" is an expression for the totality of the bliss of the Gardens, just as the Book expressed by it all deeds, for He said: {«إن كتاب الأبرار لفي عليين»} \"Verily the book of the righteous is in 'Illiyyūn,\" {ثم قال} then He said: {«يشهده المقربون»} \"The Near Ones bear witness to it.\" So it was a sign of the loftiness of their book that it ascended to where the Near Ones bear witness to it. And just as the righteous find increase in their state and their knowledge by their proximity to the Near Ones and their beholding of them, so likewise is their state in the Hereafter — {«ما خلقكم ولا بعثكم إلا كنفس واحدة»} \"Your creation and your resurrection are but as a single soul,\" just as He said — as we began the first creation, We shall repeat it — and as the Exalted said: {«جزاء وفاقا»} \"A recompense fully matching\" — that is, the recompense matched their deeds. So the pure was met with the unmixed of the drink, and the mingled was met with the mingled.\nAnd the admixture of every drink is in proportion to what preceded of admixture in his love and his deeds. For {«من يعمل مثقال ذرة خيرا يره * ومن يعمل مثقال ذرة شرا يره»} \"Whoever does an atom's weight of good shall see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil shall see it.\" And {«إن الله لا يغير ما بقوم حتى يغيروا ما بأنفسهم * إن الله لا يظلم مثقال ذرة وإن تك حسنة يضاعفها وإن كان مثقال حبة من خردل أتينا بها وكفى بنا حاسبين»} \"Verily God does not change what is in a people until they change what is in themselves. Verily God does not wrong an atom's weight; and if it be a good deed, He multiplies it, and if it be a grain of mustard-seed, We bring it — and sufficient are We as reckoners.\"\nSo whoever's love in this world was his hope for the bliss of the Garden, the wide-eyed houris, and the palaces — he is granted the Garden to settle therein wherever he wishes, to sport with the immortal youths and take pleasure with the women. There his delight in the Hereafter reaches its end, because every man is given in love only what his soul desires and his eye delights in.\nBut whoever's aim was the Lord of the Abode and the Owner of the Kingdom, and nothing overcame him save His love with sincerity and truthfulness — he is lodged in the seat...", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1179", "section_title_en": "The Marks of True Love and the Degrees of the Hereafter", "section_index": 1179, "anchors": "- Root: نشط (n-sh-ṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of vitality, energy, and alacrity in action.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Activity of the soul (nafs) in pursuing what it loves, unflagging in its ardor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bodily vigor; Metaphorical: the heart's persistent zeal that overrides physical exhaustion.\n- Root: شرب (sh-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Drinking; the act of imbibing liquids.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, a metaphor (majaz) for the soul's reception of spiritual bliss and states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical quenching; Metaphorical: the tasting of paradisiacal delight, graded by purity (safā') versus admixture (shawb).\n- Root: صفو (ṣ-f-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Purity, clarity, and freedom from turbidity or mixture.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unalloyed state of sincere love (mahabbah) and its corresponding reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: clear liquid; Metaphorical: the soul's exclusive devotion to God, yielding unmixed bliss.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to delimit the final reward strictly by the object and purity of one's love, as in \"إنما يعطى كل إنسان في المحبة ما تشتهيه نفسه.\"", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "أما بعد فقد قال الله تعالى {ونضع الموازين القسط ليوم القيامة فلا تظلم نفس شيئا وإن كان مثقال حبة من خردل أتينا بها وكفى بنا حاسبين} وقال تعالى {ووضع الكتاب فترى المجرمين مشفقين مما فيه ويقولون يا ويلتنا ما لهذا الكتاب لا يغادر صغيرة ولا كبيرة إلا أحصاها ووجدوا ما عملوا حاضرا ولا يظلم ربك أحدا} وقال تعالى {يوم يبعثهم الله جميعا فينبئهم بما عملوا أحصاه الله ونسوه والله على كل شيء شهيد} وقال تعالى {يومئذ يصدر الناس أشتاتا ليروا أعمالهم فمن يعمل مثقال ذرة خيرا يره ومن يعمل مثقال ذرة شرا يره} وقال تعالى {ثم توفى كل نفس ما كسبت وهم لا يظلمون} وقال تعالى {يوم تجد كل نفس ما عملت من خي", "english": "To proceed: God Most High has said: {And We shall set up the scales of justice for the Day of Resurrection, so no soul shall be wronged in anything; and if it be the weight of a grain of mustard seed, We shall bring it forth, and sufficient are We as Reckoners} (21:47). And He said: {And the Book shall be placed, and you will see the criminals fearful of what is in it, and they will say, \"Woe to us! What is this book that leaves neither small nor great but has enumerated it?\" And they will find what they did present, and your Lord wrongs no one} (18:49). And He said: {On the Day when God will raise them all together and inform them of what they did — God has enumerated it, and they have forgotten it, and God is Witness over all things} (58:6). And He said: {On that Day, people will issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds. So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it} (99:6-8). And He said: {Then every soul shall be paid in full for what it has earned, and they shall not be wronged} (2:281). And He said: {On the Day when every soul will find what it did of good present, and what it did of evil, it will wish that between it and that there were a far distance; and God warns you of Himself} (3:30). And He said: {And know that God knows what is in your souls, so beware of Him} (2:235).", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1230", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Reality of Truthfulness (Al-Sidq), Its Meaning, and Its Grades — Book of Vigilance and Self-Reckoning, Being the Eighth Book of the Quarter of Deliverance from the Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 1230, "anchors": "- Root: رقب (Raqaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To watch, guard, observe; a watcher (raqīb) is one who stands vigilant over something, awaiting its occurrence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Raqaba denotes protective watchfulness; al-raqīb is a divine attribute signifying His perpetual oversight of all that transpires, with no moment of neglect.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): physical watching/awaiting. Metaphorical (majāz): \"This matter is upon your necks (fī riqābikum)\" — i.e., an obligation you must vigilantly uphold; \"the death is in the necks\" — imminent and inescapable.\n- Root: حسب (Hasaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To count, reckon, compute; al-ḥisāb is the act of enumeration and settlement of accounts.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ḥisāb is the employment of number; divine reckoning (ḥisāb) on the Day of Resurrection is the exacting appraisal of deeds, even to the atom's weight.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: counting wealth or deeds. Metaphorical: \"they reckon news (yaḥtasibūna al-akhbār)\" — they investigate and ascertain, as one places knowledge in place of conjecture.\n- Root: قلب (Qalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, transform; the heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning of states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qalaba is the turning of a thing from one state to another; the heart's inner thoughts (khaṭarāt) are its successive movements, which God surveys.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: turning a garment or object. Metaphorical: \"the teacher turned the boys\" (sent them home); \"the merchant turned the goods\" (scrutinized them) — thus, inner reflection (taqallub) is the soul's scrutiny of its own motives.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in \"إنما يحاسبهم لتعلم كل نفس\" — the sole purpose of reckoning is specified, excluding all other ends.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "الأصل الرابع الميزان وهو حق قال الله تعالى ونضع الموازين القسط ليوم القيامة وقال تعالى فمن ثقلت موازينه فأولئك هم المفلحون ومن خفت موازينه الآية ووجهها أن الله تعالى يحدث في صحائف الأعمال وزنا بحسب درجات الأعمال عند الله تعالى فتصير مقادير أعمال العباد معلومة للعباد حتى يظهر لهم العدل في العقاب أو الفضل في العفو وتضعيف الثواب الأصل الخامس الصراط وهو جسر ممدود على متن جهنم أرق من الشعرة وأحد من السيف قال الله تعالى فاهدوهم إلى صراط الجحيم وقفوهم إنهم مسئولون وهذا ممكن فيجب التصديق به فإن القادر على أن يطي", "english": "For they, despite their distinction in eloquence and rhetorical perfection, aimed at reviling him, forbidding him, killing him, and expelling him — as God, Mighty and Majestic, informed us about them — yet they were unable to produce anything to rival the Qur'an. For it was not within the capacity of human beings to combine the grandeur of the Qur'an's diction with its harmonious order, especially given what it contains of the accounts of the ancients, while he was illiterate, never having practiced the study of books — and [given] its announcement of the unseen in matters whose truthfulness was verified in the future, such as His saying, Exalted is He: {«لَتَدْخُلُنَّ الْمَسْجِدَ الْحَرَامَ إِنْ شَاءَ اللَّهُ آمِنِينَ مُحَلِّقِينَ رُءُوسَكُمْ وَمُقَصِّرِينَ»} (You shall surely enter the Sacred Mosque, if God wills, secure, with your heads shaved and cut short) [Al-Fatḥ 48:27], and such as His saying: {«الم غُلِبَتِ الرُّومُ فِي أَدْنَى الْأَرْضِ وَهُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ غَلَبِهِمْ سَيَغْلِبُونَ فِي بِضْعِ سِنِينَ»} (Alif Lām Mīm. The Romans have been defeated in the nearest land, and they, after their defeat, shall be victorious within a few years) [Al-Rūm 30:1-3].", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 96", "section_title_en": "The Fourth Pillar: On Matters of Hearing (al-Samʿiyyāt) and the Verification of the Prophet ﷺ in What He Reported — Comprising Ten Fundamental Principles", "section_index": 96, "anchors": "- Root: حشر (ḥ-sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To gather, assemble, drive together; al-ḥashr is the gathering of creation on the Day of Resurrection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ḥashr is the bringing together of a people from their various places of dispersal; it is used specifically for the resurrection gathering.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for driving cattle together; metaphorical (majāz) for the ingathering of souls for judgment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Protasis (al-sharṭ) — \"If you are truthful, then rise...\" (fa-in kunta ṣādiqan fa-qum...) — the apodosis (jawāb al-sharṭ) functions as the proof of the protasis, establishing necessary knowledge (ʿilm ḍarūrī).\n\n- Root: نشر (n-sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To spread, unfold, revive after death; al-nushūr is the revivification of the dead.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nashr is the spreading of the soul back into the body after death; it is the opposite of al-ṭayy (folding up).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for unfolding a garment; metaphorical for the resurrection when the earth \"spreads forth\" what it contains.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Coordination (al-ʿaṭf) — \"al-ḥashr wa-l-nashr\" (the gathering and the spreading) — two coordinated nouns forming a single compound concept of resurrection.\n\n- Root: مكن (m-k-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To establish, empower, give firm footing; makkana means to make something firm and stable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-makān is the place that contains a thing; tamakkun is the firm establishment of a thing in its place.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical placement; metaphorical for divine power — \"God is able\" (qādir) means He has full capacity and authority over all things.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Possibility (al-imkān) — \"that is possible\" (dhālika mumkin) — the predicate of possibility is established by the absence of logical contradiction, not by empirical observation.\n\n- Root: عذب (ʿ-dh-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sweet, pleasant (of water); al-ʿadhāb is the punishment that is \"sweet\" to the inflictor but bitter to the recipient — used predominantly for divine chastisement.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ʿadhāb is the infliction of pain that deters; it is specifically the painful consequence of disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for sweet water; metaphorical (majāz) for punishment that is \"appointed\" as a deterrent.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Exception (al-istithnāʾ) — \"except as God wills\" (illā mā shāʾa) — the exception clause restricts the scope of human perception, not divine action.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يدخل الجنة من كان في قلبه مثقال حبة من خردل من كبر ولا يدخل الجنة من كان في قلبه مثقال حبة من خردل من إيمان (1) حديث أبي هريرة يقول الله تعالى الكبرياء ردائي والعظمة إزاري فمن نازعني واحدا منهما ألقيته في جهنم أخرجه مسلم وأبو داود وابن ماجه واللفظ له وقال أبو داود قذفته في النار وقال مسلم عذبته وقال رداؤه وإزاره بالغيبة وزاد مع أبي هريرة أبا سعيد أيضا وعن أبي سلمة بن عبد الرحمن قال التقى عبد الله بن عمرو وعبد الله بن عمر على الصفا فتواقفا فمضى ابن عمرو وأقام ابن عمر يبكي فقالوا ما يب", "english": "To proceed: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said — God, exalted be He, said: \"Pride (al-kibriyāʾ) is My cloak, and greatness (al-ʿaẓamah) is My wrapper; whoever contends with Me over either of them, I crush him.\" {«الكبرياء ردائي والعظمة إزاري فمن نازعني فيهما قصمته»} (1) — A hadith of the three destructive matters, related by al-Bazzār, al-Ṭabarānī, and al-Bayhaqī in al-Shuʿab, from Anas with a weak chain, and it has also been mentioned previously.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 847", "section_title_en": "The Book of Condemnation of Pride and Self-Admiration — Being the Ninth Book of the Quarter of Destructive Matters from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 847, "anchors": "- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes magnitude, whether physical size, age, or rank; \"al-kibriyā'\" is the supreme majesty exclusive to God.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"al-kabīr wa al-ṣaghīr\" are relative terms; used for quantity, quality, and dignity. \"al-kibriyā'\" is a divine attribute signifying His exclusive greatness, which none may contest.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"kabura al-amr\" (the matter became grave); \"kabura ʿalayhi\" (it weighed heavily upon him). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"takabbara\" (to act haughtily) is a figurative extension of claiming a magnitude one does not possess, as in the divine saying.\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Astonishment at something extraordinary; \"al-ʿujb\" is self-amazement, a state where one marvels at his own qualities.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Astonishment arises from ignorance of the true cause; self-admiration (ʿujb) blinds one to one's own defects and to the divine origin of all good.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ʿajiba minhu\" (he marveled at it). Metaphorical: \"iʿjāb al-nafs\" is the soul's complacency with itself, a hidden form of pride that does not require another object.\n- Root: هلك (h-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perishing, destruction, ruin; \"al-muhlikāt\" are the things that cause perdition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Destruction is of three kinds: loss, corruption, and death. Here it is spiritual perdition — the ruin of the soul's eternal felicity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical ruin. Metaphorical: \"tahālaka ʿalayhi\" (to be consumed with craving for something); the soul's destruction through vice is a figurative death.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Apposition and Parenthetical Clauses (al-badal wa al-iʿtirāḍ) — The opening doxology employs extended appositional chains (badal) and parenthetical invocations that delay the main clause, requiring the translator to preserve the periodic structure without collapsing the theological weight of each divine epithet.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "51": [{"arabic": "وتفتره عما فيه فساده ويكون ذلك من الباطن كما قال تعالى {ولقد آتينا إبراهيم رشده من قبل وكنا به عالمين} فالرشد عبارة عن هداية باعثة إلى جهة السعادة محركة إليها فالصبي إذا بلغ خبيرا بحفظ المال وطرق التجارة والاستنماء ولكنه مع ذلك يبذر ولا يريد الاستنماء لا يسمى رشيدا لا لعدم هدايته بل لقصور هدايته عن تحريك داعيته فكم من شخص يقدم على ما يعلم أنه يضره فقد أعطي الهداية وميز بها عن الجاهل الذي لا يدري أنه يضره ولكن ما أعطى الرشد فالرشد بهذا الاعتبار أكمل من مجرد الهداية إلى وجوه الأعمال وهي نعمة عظيمة وأما التسديد فهو توجيه حركاته إلى صوب المطلوب", "english": "PageV04P107\nBut if one does not know what constitutes the welfare of his hereafter—so that he even supposes corruption to be welfare—then from where would mere volition benefit him? There is no benefit in volition, power, and causes except after guidance (hidāyah). For this reason, God—exalted is He—said: {«ربنا الذي أعطى كل شيء خلقه ثم هدى»} \"Our Lord who gave everything its creation, then guided.\" And He said—exalted is He—: {«ولولا فضل الله عليكم ورحمته ما زكى منكم أحد أبدا ولكن الله يزكي من يشاء»} \"And were it not for God's bounty upon you and His mercy, not one of you would ever be purified; but God purifies whom He wills.\" And the Prophet—peace be upon him—said: \"No one enters Paradise except by the mercy of God—exalted is He—,\" that is, by His guidance. So it was said: \"Not even you, O Messenger of God?\" He said: \"Not even I\" (1). And guidance (hidāyah) has three stations (manāzil):", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 999", "section_title_en": "The Degrees of Divine Guidance (Hidāyah), Righteous Discernment (Rushd), Straightening (Tasdīd), and Divine Support (Ta'yīd)", "section_index": 999, "anchors": "- Root: هدي (H-D-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of gentle, gracious direction (dalālah bi-luṭf); the root also yields \"gift\" (hadiyyah) and the leading beasts of prey (hawādī) that guide the herd.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Guidance is \"directional indication with gentleness\" (dalālah bi-luṭf), distinct from mere intellectual proof; it is a divine act that may be general (to all) or specific (to the elect), and its withholding is a form of abandonment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He guided him to the path\" (hadāhu lil-sabīl). Metaphorical (majāz): \"He guided him\" meaning \"he preceded him\" (taqaddamahu), as a leader precedes the caravan; hence guidance is not merely pointing but active leading.\n- Root: رشد (R-SH-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The state of being rightly directed in one's affairs; integrity of judgment; names Rāshid, Rushayd derive from it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Rushd is the opposite of ghayy (error/ruin); it is used like hidāyah but with a nuance of practical, executive discernment—knowing the good AND being moved to pursue it. Al-Raghib explicitly distinguishes the rushd of Abraham (given before, as a special gift) from the mere legal competence (rushd) of an orphan.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"A man is rāshid or rashīd\" (possessing rightness in conduct). Metaphorical: \"He is of sound lineage\" (huwa li-rushdah), i.e., legitimate—showing the root's extension to ontological soundness.\n- Root: سدد (S-D-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To aim correctly, to hit the mark; the arrow that flies straight (sadīd); the opposite of deviation (ighrāḍ).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (Not in the retrieved scholia, but by extension from the root's usage in the Qur'an, e.g., {wa-saddidū}, \"and be straight/aim rightly\")—it denotes the divine facilitation of the creature's movements so they align with the intended good.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He aimed the arrow and it went straight.\" Metaphorical: \"He was granted success in speech and action\" (sudda li-l-ṣawāb).\n- Root: أيد (A-Y-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Strength, support, reinforcement; the \"aid\" (ayd) of God is His empowering of His servant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (By extension from {إذ أيدتك بروح القدس})—divine reinforcement through a holy spirit, i.e., an immaterial power that fortifies both inner perception (baṣīrah) and outer action.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He strengthened him\" (ayyadahu). Metaphorical: Used for the conferral of spiritual power that makes the servant invincible against error.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Gradation and Specification (Tartīb wa-Takhṣīṣ) — The author structures a sequence of terms (hidāyah → rushd → tasdīd → ta'yīd) where each subsequent term is syntactically and semantically superordinate, building a hierarchy of divine acts. The particle \"wa-ammā\" (وأما) marks a new topic with contrastive focus, and the anaphoric references (هو، وهي) bind the exposition into a single periodic argument.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "56": [{"arabic": "وهو ثناء فيقرأ معه فيقول مثل قوله أو يقول بلى وأنا على ذلك من الشاهدين أو صدقت وبررت وما أشبه ذلك وقد روي حديث في رفع اليدين في القنوت (7) فإذا صح", "english": "And it has been said: When two persons were present for the congregational prayer, they would not wait for a third; and when four were present for a funeral prayer, they would not wait for a fifth. And the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, was once delayed from the dawn prayer while they were on a journey — his delay was only for the sake of purification — yet he did not wait, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf stepped forward and led them in prayer, until one unit (rakʿah) escaped the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, and he stood to make it up. The narrator said: \"We were concerned about that.\" Then the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: \"You have done well. Thus do, and so act.\" And he was once delayed from the noon prayer, so they put forward Abū Bakr, may God be pleased with him, until the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, arrived while he was in the prayer, and he stood beside him. And it is not upon the Imam to wait for the caller to prayer (mu'adhdhin); rather, it is upon the caller to wait for the Imam for the commencement (iqāmah). So when the Imam has arrived, he should not wait for anyone else.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 144", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 144: On the Inward and Outward Functions of the Prayer (Continued)", "section_index": 144, "anchors": "- Root: صلا (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denoting the act of prayer (ṣalāh), derived from supplication and drawing near; also the fire of worship's burning devotion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṣalāh as the spiritual connection to God, encompassing both outward ritual and inward sincerity (ikhlāṣ); its perfection lies in presence of heart and adherence to prophetic practice.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — the prescribed ritual prayer with its conditions and pillars; Metaphorical (majāz) — prayer as the soul's \"burning\" yearning toward God, purified from ostentation.\n- Root: رسل (r-s-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sending forth with ease and gentleness; a messenger (rasūl) dispatched with a mission.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Imam as a \"delegate\" (rasūl) of the congregation, bearing the trust (amānah) of leading them in worship before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the Imam's role in conveying the prayer; Metaphorical — the Imam as intercessor (shafīʿ) and representative for the people, requiring moral excellence.\n- Root: عله (ʿ-l-h) — Note: Not directly applicable; substituted by root: أمن (a-m-n) for amānah (trust).\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Trustworthiness and fidelity; the opposite of betrayal (khiyānah).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Amānah as the divine trust reposed in the Imam — both outward purity (ṭahārah) from ritual impurity and inward purity from sin.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — safeguarding the prayer's conditions; Metaphorical — the Imam's moral state as a reflection of the congregation's spiritual standing.\n- Root: سور (s-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Leaping, ascending; a wall (sūr) enclosing a city.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The \"leap\" of the Imam into the prayer's spiritual domain, and the congregation's measured following — neither racing ahead nor lagging.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the physical postures of bowing and prostration; Metaphorical — the soul's ascent through humility, avoiding the \"leap\" of pride or haste.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish what is obligatory (farḍ) from what is recommended (mandūb), and to specify the precise limits of the Imam's and congregation's actions, ensuring no overstepping of the prophetic model.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "60": [{"arabic": "والخير كله في الشباب ثم تلا قوله عز وجل {قالوا سمعنا فتى يذكرهم يقال له إبراهيم} وقوله تعالى {إنهم فتية آمنوا بربهم وزدناهم هدى} وقوله تعالى {وآتيناه الحكم صبيا} وكان أنس رضي الله عنه يقول قبض رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وليس في رأسه ولحيته عشرون شعرة بيضاء فقيل له يا أبا حمزة فقد أسن فقال لم يشنه الله بالشيب فقيل أهو شين فقال كلكم يكرهه (1) ويقال إن يحيى بن أكثم ولي القضاء وهو ابن إحدى وعشرين سنة فقال له رجل في مجلسه يريد أن يخجله بصغر سنه كم سن القاضي أيده الله فقال مثل سن عتاب بن أسيد حين ولاه رسول الله صلى الله عليه وس", "english": "As for the first — namely, dyeing it black — it is prohibited, for the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"The best of your young men are those who resemble your old men, and the worst of your old men are those who resemble your young men.\" (1) What is meant by resembling the old men is in dignity (al-waqār), not in whitening the hair. And he prohibited dyeing with black, (2) saying: \"It is the dye of the inhabitants of the Fire.\" (3) In another wording: \"Dyeing with black is the dye of the disbelievers.\" A man married during the caliphate of ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, while dyeing his hair black; his dye faded and his whiteness appeared. The woman's family brought him to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, who annulled his marriage, struck him painfully, and said: \"You deceived the people with youth and disguised your whiteness from them.\" It is said that the first to dye with black was Pharaoh, may Allah curse him. And it is related from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, that he said: \"There will come at the end of time a people who dye with black like the crops of pigeons; they will not smell the fragrance of Paradise.\" (4)", "book_title": "Mysteries of Purity", "section_title_ar": "Section 119", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Secrets of Purification — Section 119: On the Beard and Its Disapproved Practices", "section_index": 119, "anchors": "- Root: شيب (sh-y-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Whiteness of hair; the first appearance of white hairs marking the transition from youth to age.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الشيب والمشيب: بياض الشعر\" — cited with {واشتعل الرأس شيبا} [Maryam/4]; theologically, it is the visible sign of decrepitude and the approach of death, thus a divine summons.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"شاب شيبة، ورجل أشيب\" — literal whitening of hair. Majaz: \"شابت رءوس الإكام\" — metaphorical whitening of mountain peaks with frost/snow, indicating that the physical whiteness of the beard is but a surface sign of an inner reality.\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as a mark, sign, or banner (ʿalam); the act of knowing by direct apprehension.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing by its true reality; it is a fruit of the intellect (ʿaql), not of accumulated years.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" — knowledge as direct awareness. The scholia here ground the author's argument that age does not confer knowledge; rather, knowledge is a divinely-given subtle reality (laṭīfah rabbāniyyah) independent of bodily whitening.\n- Root: وقد (w-q-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fire blazing and burning intensely.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"وقدت النار تقد وقودا ووقدا\" — the kindling and blazing of fire; cited with {وقودها الناس والحجارة} [Al-Baqara/24].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Majaz: \"قلب وقاد\" — a blazing, keen intellect. This anchors the contrast between the superficial blaze of dyed hair (a false fire) and the inner blaze of youthful intelligence (a true light).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule permits interposition between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase when the intervening element is semantically complete. This governs the periodic structure of the author's long enumerations, where each disapproved characteristic is stated, then separated from its governing judgment by explanatory clauses, requiring the translator to preserve the suspended syntactic tension until the judgment is delivered.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Purity'", "badge": "Insight"}], "67": [{"arabic": "مثل قول إبراهيم عليه السلام أف لكم ولما تعبدون من دون الله أفلا تعقلون ولسنا نعني بالسب والفحش بما فيه نسبة إلى الزنا ومقدماته ولا الكذب بل أن يخاطبه بما فيه مما لا يعد من جملة الفحش كقوله يا فاسق يا أحمق يا جاهل ألا تخاف الله وكقوله يا سوادى يا غبي وما يجري هذا المجرى فإن كل فاسق فهو أحمق وجاهل ولولا حمقه لما عصى الله تعالى بل كل من ليس", "english": "And at this point it is said to him what was said to Jesus, peace be upon him: \"O son of Mary, exhort yourself; if you are admonished, then exhort the people — and if not, then be ashamed before Me.\"", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 540", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 540: On Gentleness in Forbidding Wrong and the Hidden Diseases of the Enjoiner", "section_index": 540, "anchors": "- Root: لطف (luṭf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Gentleness, subtlety, refinement; the opposite of harshness and roughness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine gentleness (luṭf) as that which draws the servant toward obedience through kindness rather than compulsion\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical softness/delicacy; Metaphorical: kindness of manner and speech in dealing with others\n- Root: عجب (ʿujb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self-admiration; the soul's being pleased with itself and its own states\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A hidden disease of the heart wherein one admires his own qualities, forgetting that they are divine gifts\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder/astonishment; Metaphorical: the soul's complacent delight in its own excellence\n- Root: حسب (ḥasaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To reckon, count, account; the one who enjoins right and forbids wrong (muḥtasib)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The act of calling to account; the muḥtasib as one who holds himself and others to divine standards\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: numerical reckoning; Metaphorical: moral accountability and the office of the public censor\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التدرج في الأحكام (Graduated Progression of Rulings) — The text moves through ascending degrees of severity in forbidding wrong, each stage conditional upon the failure of the preceding, requiring precise syntactic parsing of conditional and concessive clauses.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "79": [{"arabic": "وجل ففهمناها سليمان وكلا آتينا حكما وعلما سمى ما آتاهما علما وحكما وخصص ما انفرد به سليمان بالتفطن له باسم الفهم وجعله مقدما على الحكم والعلم فهذه الأمور تدل على أن في فهم معاني القرآن مجالا رحبا ومتسعا بالغا وأن المنقول من ظاهر التفسير ليس منتهى الإدراك فيه فأما قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم من فسر القرآن برأيه ونهيه عنه (2) صلى الله عليه وسلم وقول أبي بكر رضي الله عنه أي أرض تقلني وأي سماء تظلني إذا قلت في القرآن برأيي إلى غير ذلك مما ورد في الأخبار والآثار في النهي عن تفسير القرآن بالرأي فل", "english": "I say: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the means of deliverance from these trials, and he said: \"The Book of Allah — act by what is in it, for it is that which delivers one from them.\" The questioner repeated this three times, and he (peace be upon him) said three times: \"Learn the Book of Allah, Mighty and Glorious, and act by what is in it, for in it is deliverance.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "84": [{"arabic": "بعد المحنة العظيمة مكن قال الله تعالى وآتيناه أهله ومثلهم معهم الآية فهذا الكلام من الشافعي رحمه الله يدل على تبحره في أسرار القرآن واطلاعه على مقامات السائرين إلى الله تعالى من الأنبياء والأولياء وكل ذلك من علوم الآخرة وقيل للشافعي رحمه الله متى يكون الرجل عالما قال إذا تحقق في علم الدين فعلمه وتعرض لسائر العلوم فنظر فيما فاته فعند ذلك يكون عالما فإنه قيل لجالينوس إنك تأمر للداء الواحد بالأدوية الكثيرة المجمعة فقال إنما المقصود منها واحد وإنما يجعل معه غيره لتسكن حدته لأن الإفراد قاتل فهذا وأمثاله مما لا يح", "english": "And al-Shafi'i, may God Most High have mercy upon him, said: \"If you fear for your deed the blight of self-admiration (al-ʿujb), then consider: whose pleasure do you seek? Toward what reward do you yearn? From what punishment do you flee? What well-being do you thank? And what affliction do you recall? For if you reflect upon even one of these five qualities, your deed will appear small in your eyes.\" Observe how he mentioned the reality of ostentation and the remedy for self-admiration—both being among the gravest maladies of the heart.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 21", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 21: The Sciences of the Hereafter and the Signs of Sincere Intention in Knowledge", "section_index": 21, "anchors": "- Root: مكن (m-k-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of establishing, empowering, and granting firm footing in a place or station.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"المكان عند أهل اللغة: الموضع الحاوي للشيء\" — the place that encompasses a thing; theological nuance: divine empowerment (tamkīn) as a station granted after trial.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"مكنته من الشيء وأمكنته منه، فتمكن منه واستمكن\" — physical enabling. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"الناس على مكناتهم: على مقارهم\" — people upon their settled abodes/stations; here, the spiritual station of the prophets after tribulation.\n- Root: محن (m-ḥ-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Testing, refining, and proving; a worn garment (mamḥūn) that has been stretched and expanded by use.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"المحن والامتحان نحو الابتلاء\" — trial and testing akin to affliction; Quranic: {فامتحنوهن} (Al-Mumtahanah: 10) — the testing of hearts for sincerity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وقع في محنة ومحن\" — falling into trial. Metaphorical: \"محن الأديم: مدده حتى وسعه\" — stretching hide until it widens; thus {امتحن الله قلوبهم} — God expands and purifies the heart through trial.\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the apprehension of a thing's reality; the mark (ʿalam) by which one is guided.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the perception of a thing in its true reality; two types: apprehension of essence, and judgment of existence or negation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" — to be aware. Metaphorical: \"خفيت معالم الطريق\" — the traces of the path that guide the seeker; here, the signs (ʿalāmāt) of sincere intention.\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, and transforming a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart as that which is constantly turned and transformed; the seat of spiritual perception.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — physical overturning. Metaphorical: \"قلب التاجر السلعة\" — the merchant scrutinizing his goods; \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور\" — one who turns matters over; here, the heart's gaze upon its own intentions.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to confine a predicate to a single subject, as in \"إنما المقصود منها واحد\" (the intended aim is but one), emphasizing exclusive focus.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "87": [{"arabic": "له وأشهد أن محمدا عبده ورسوله سبحانك اللهم وبحمدك لا إله إلا أنت عملت سوءا وظلمت نفسي أستغفرك اللهم وأتوب إليك فاغفر لي وتب على إنك أنت التواب الرحيم اللهم اجعلني من التوابين واجعلني من المتطهرين واجعلني من عبادك الصالحين واجعلني عبدا صبورا شكورا واجعلني أذكرك كثيرا وأسبحك بكرة وأصيلا يقال إن من قال هذا بعد الوضوء ختم على وضوئه بخاتم ورفع له تحت العرش فلم يزل يسبح الله تعالى ويقدسه ويكتب له ثواب ذلك إلى يوم القيامة ويكره في الوضوء أمور منها أن يزيد على الثلاث فمن زاد فقد ظلم وأن يسرف في الماء توضأ عليه السلا", "english": "Know that the use of the tooth-stick is a confirmed Sunnah. It is reported concerning its virtue from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar through a weak chain of transmission, and Abū Dāwūd and al-Ḥākim narrated it, and the latter declared it sound (ṣaḥīḥ). Al-Bayhaqī, however, deemed it weak from the ḥadīth of ʿĀʾishah, and he weakened the version containing the wording \"from seventy prayers.\" There is the ḥadīth: \"Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my community, I would have commanded them to use the tooth-stick at every prayer\" — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh) from the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah. There is the ḥadīth: \"Why do I see you entering upon me with yellowed teeth? Use the tooth-stick!\" — al-Bazzār and al-Bayhaqī narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib; Abū Dāwūd and al-Baghawī from the ḥadīth of Tammām ibn al-ʿAbbās; and al-Bayhaqī from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās — and it is disordered (muḍṭarib). There is the ḥadīth: \"He used the tooth-stick multiple times during the night\" — Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās. And the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās: \"The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, did not cease commanding us to use the tooth-stick until we thought that something concerning it would be revealed to him\" — Aḥmad narrated it.", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 112", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Tooth-Stick (Siwāk) and the Etiquette of Ablution (Wuḍūʾ)", "section_index": 112, "anchors": "- Root: س و ك (s-w-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes rubbing or cleansing the teeth with a stick (siwāk); al-Farāhīdī notes its use for the act of friction against a surface to remove residue.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the religious lexicon, siwāk is a purifying act (taṭhīr) of the mouth, tied to devotional hygiene and the Sunnah's emphasis on preparing the body for prayer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage refers to the physical stick (ʿūd) and the mechanical action; metaphorical (majāz) extension applies to spiritual purification, as the mouth's cleanliness mirrors the heart's purity.\n- Root: و ض ء (w-ḍ-ʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Wuḍūʾ signifies washing specific bodily parts for ritual purity; al-Khalīl links it to brightness and radiance (ḍawʾ), as the washed limbs shine on the Day of Resurrection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): It is a legislated act of cleansing (ṭahārah) that removes minor ritual impurity (ḥadath), preconditioning the soul for communion with God in prayer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: washing with water; metaphorical: the inner purification of the heart from vices, as the outward washing symbolizes inward renewal.\n- Root: غ ر ر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Ghurrah denotes a blaze or white mark on a horse's forehead; al-Ayn records its use for a prominent, shining feature.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the ḥadīth, \"ghurrah\" refers to the luminous mark on the forehead of the believer from washing in ablution, a sign of honor on the Day of Judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the white star on a horse's brow; metaphorical: the radiant trace of worship on the believer's body, visible in the Hereafter.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ʿAṭf ʿalā al-Sabab (Coordination of Causes) — When linking actions or supplications (duʿāʾ) with fa- or thumma, the syntactic sequence mirrors the chronological order of ritual acts, ensuring that each clause is semantically complete and temporally precise.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "تسيل على لحيته جعل يقول {لا إله إلا أنت سبحانك إني كنت من الظالمين} اللهم إني أستعديك عليهم وأستعينك على جميع أموري وأسألك الصبر على ما ابتليتني وفاة علي كرم الله وجهه قال الأصبغ الحنظلي لما كانت الليلة التي أصيب فيها علي كرم الله وجهه اتاه ابن التياح حين طلع الفجر يؤذنه بالصلاة وهو مضطجع متثاقل فعاد الثانية وهو كذلك ثم عاد الثالثة فقام علي يمشي وهو يقول اشدد حيازيمك للموت .", "english": "ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām said: I came to my brother ʿUthmān to greet him while he was besieged, and I entered upon him. He said, \"Welcome, O my brother. I saw the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, tonight in this niche\"—and it was a niche in the house—\"and he said, 'O ʿUthmān, they have besieged you.' I said, 'Yes.' He said, 'They have thirsted you.' I said, 'Yes.' So he lowered a bucket containing water, and I drank until I was quenched, until I felt its coolness between my chest and my shoulders. And he said to me, 'If you wish, I will grant you victory over them, and if you wish, you may break your fast with us.' So I chose to break my fast with him, and he was killed that very day, may Allah be pleased with him.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1305", "section_title_en": "The Martyrdoms of ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn (May Allah Be Pleased with Them)", "section_index": 1305, "anchors": "- Root: قتل (q-t-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of killing, slaying, or putting to death; the act of taking life.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, distinguishes between lawful execution (qisās) and unlawful murder (qatl ʿudwān), often tied to martyrdom (shahādah) when the victim is righteous.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for physical killing; metaphorical (majāz) for spiritual death or the \"killing\" of the soul through sin.\n- Root: شهد (sh-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To witness, testify, or be present; also denotes martyrdom (shahīd) as one who bears witness with their life.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Shahīd in the Quranic sense is one who is killed in the path of Allah, their testimony being accepted by Allah; also the Prophet as a witness over his community.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: presence at an event; metaphorical: the martyr's soul is \"present\" with Allah, alive in a spiritual sense.\n- Root: حصر (ḥ-ṣ-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To confine, besiege, or restrict; a state of being hemmed in.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, denotes physical constraint (ḥaṣr) versus spiritual freedom; the besieged believer remains inwardly free.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: military siege; metaphorical: the soul's confinement in worldly trials.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Clauses (Bāb al-Sharṭ) — The protasis (sharṭ) and apodosis (jawāb) structure in \"إن شئت نصرت عليك\" (If you wish, I will grant you victory), where the conditional particle governs both clauses, requiring precise temporal and modal sequencing.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "والشهادة رب كل شيء ومليكه أشهد أن لا إله إلا أنت أعوذ بك من شر نفسي وشر الشيطان وشركه (4) وقل اللهم إني أسألك العفو والعافية في ديني ودنياي وأهلي ومالي اللهم استر عوراتي وآمن روعاتي وأقل عثراتي واحفظني من بين يدي ومن خلفي وعن يميني وعن شمالي ومن فوقي وأعوذ بك أن أغتال من تحتي (1) اللهم لا تؤمني مكرك ولا تولني غيرك ولا تنزع عني سترك ولا تنسني ذكرك ولا تجعلني من الغافلين (2) وقل اللهم أنت ربي لا إله إلا أنت خلقتني وأنا عبدك وأنا على عهدك ووعدك ما استطعت أعوذ بك من شر ما صنعت أبو", "english": "\"Glory be to my Lord, the Most High, the Most Exalted, the Bestower (al-Wahhāb). There is no god but God alone, with no partner. His is the sovereignty and His is the praise, and He is over all things powerful.\"", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 251", "section_title_en": "The Fourth Chapter on Supplications Narrated from the Prophet (Peace Be Upon Him) and His Companions (May God Be Pleased with Them)", "section_index": 251, "anchors": "- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of swallowing/ingesting (iltihām), from which ilhām (divine inspiration) derives—that which is cast into the heart\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإلهام: إلقاء الشيء في الروع\" — divine inspiration is the casting of something into the inner faculty of consciousness (rawʿ), specifically from God and the higher assembly (malā aʿlā)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): \"ألهمه الله الخير: ألقاه في روعه\" (God inspired him with good: He cast it into his inner consciousness). Metaphorical (majāz): \"جيش لهام: يغتمر من يدخله\" (an overwhelming army that engulfs whoever enters it)\n- Root: غيب (gh-y-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: That which is hidden from sensory perception\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغيب: مصدر غابت الشمس وغيرها: إذا استترت عن العين\" — that which is concealed from the eye; applied to all that is absent from the senses and from human knowledge. Notably: \"ويقال للشيء: غيب وغائب باعتباره بالناس لا بالله تعالى، فإنه لا يغيب عنه شيء\" (it is called ghayb relative to people, not relative to God, for nothing is hidden from Him)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وتكلم بذلك عن ظهر الغيب\" (he spoke of that from behind the unseen). Metaphorical: \"أتونا في غابة أي في رماح كثيرة\" (they came to us in a thicket—i.e., amidst numerous spears like dense trees)\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the apprehension of a thing's reality\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; two types: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what exists for it or is negated from it\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" (I did not know of your news: I was not aware of it). \"وهو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" (he is among the lofty banners of knowledge)\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — governing the permissible separation between the preposition and its object for rhetorical emphasis, as in the construction \"عالم الغيب والشهادة\" where the attributive relationship is maintained through the genitive construction (iḍāfah)", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "90": [{"arabic": "السادس التضرع والخشوع والرغبة والرهبة قال الله تعالى {إنهم كانوا يسارعون في الخيرات ويدعوننا رغبا ورهبا} وقال عز وجل {ادعوا ربكم تضرعا وخفية} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إذا أحب الله عبدا ابتلاه حتى يسمع تضرعه (3) السابع أن يجزم الدعاء ويوقن بالإجابة ويصدق رجاءه فيه قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يقل أحدكم إذا دعا اللهم اغفر لي إن شئت اللهم ارحمني إن شئت ليعزم المسألة فإنه لا مكره له (4) وقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم إذا دعا أحدكم فليعظم الرغبة فإن الله لا يتعاظمه شيء (5) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم ادعوا الله وأنتم موقنون بالإجابة وا", "english": "[Root: دعو]\n  • Lisan al-Arab: \"الدعاء: الرغبة إلى الله تعالى، والابتهال في المسألة. والدعوة: الكلمة التي يدعو بها. وأصل الدعاء: طلب الإقبال من الغير.\"\n  • Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدعاء: ضربان: أحدهما دعاء العبادة، والآخر دعاء المسألة. فدعاء العبادة: هو توجه العبد إلى الله تعالى بالطاعة. ودعاء المسألة: طلب الحاجة من الله تعالى. قال تعالى: {وقال ربكم ادعوني أستجب لكم}.\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"دعا فلان ربه: ابتهل إليه بالسؤال. ودعا الله: عبده. ومنه قوله تعالى: {إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي} أي عن دعائي.\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"دعا عليه: سأل الله أن يصيبه بمكروه. ودعا له: سأل الله أن يحسن إليه. ومن المجاز: دعا الأمر إلى كذا، أي أدى إليه.\"", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 242", "section_title_en": "Section 242", "section_index": 242, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Insight"}], "91": [{"arabic": "قال تعالى فنفخنا فيها من روحنا وكان النافخ جبريل عليه السلام وكما قال تعالى فإذا قرأناه فاتبع قرآنه قيل في التفسير معناه إذا قرأه عليك جبريل وقال تعالى قاتلوهم يعذبهم الله بأيديكم فأضاف القتل إليهم والتعذيب إلى نفسه والتعذيب هو عين", "english": "Thus should you understand the issuance of determined things (al-maqdūrāt) from eternal power — even though the power is eternal (qadīmah) and the objects of power are originated (ḥādithah). And this is a knocking upon another door of another world among the worlds of unveilings (mukāshafāt). So let us leave all of that, for our purpose is only to alert to the path of unification (tawḥīd) in the act — for the agent in truth (al-fāʿil bi-al-ḥaqīqah) is One. He is the feared and the hoped, and upon Him is reliance and trust. And we have not been able to mention from the seas of tawḥīd save a drop from the ocean of the third station among the stations of tawḥīd. And exhausting that within the lifetime of Noah is impossible — like exhausting the water of the sea by taking droplets from it. And all of that is subsumed under the saying: \"There is no god but God\" (lā ilāha illā Allāh). How light is its burden upon the tongue, how easy is the belief in the meaning of its utterance upon the heart, and how precious is its reality and its kernel (lubbuhu) among the scholars firmly rooted in knowledge — so how [much more so] with others?", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1114", "section_title_en": "The Oneness of the Actor (Tawḥīd al-Fiʿl) — Reconciling Divine and Human Agency", "section_index": 1114, "anchors": "- Root: قدر (q-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes measure, decree, and capacity; al-Farāhīdī anchors it in the physical act of measuring or sizing, extended to divine determining.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-qudrah\" when ascribed to God negates incapacity (ʿajz) absolutely; when ascribed to man, it names a state (hayʾah) enabling a specific act — never absolute power, but restricted capacity over particular objects.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He is qādir, possessing power.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"faras baʿīd al-qadr\" (a far-striding horse) — power's \"reach\" extended figuratively; here, divine power's \"presence\" with its objects is literal for God, metaphorical for creatures.\n- Root: حدث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥudūth\": coming-to-be after non-existence; al-ḥadath: ritual impurity (as in this text's water analogy); al-muḥdath: the originated thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥudūth: the thing's being after it was not — whether accident or substance. Its iḥdāth (bringing-about) is exclusive to God.\" Creaturely acts are originated, but their origination is divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: recent, newly arisen. Metaphorical: \"ʿUmar was muḥaddath\" (inspired in judgment) — as if spoken to by unseen truth; here, the \"newly arisen\" (ḥādith) effect is tied to a condition (sharṭ), not a new power.\n- Root: ماء (m-ā-ʾ)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Water as the origin of life ({وجعلنا من الماء كل شيء حي}); its root is m-w-h, with hāʾ dropped and wāw shifted — a paradigm for how a thing's \"presence\" (mulāqāh) does not entail its \"effect\" (taʾthīr) absent a condition.\n  * Kitab al-Ayn: Water's essence is unchanged; the analogy hinges on its constancy.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-wajh\" is the face; then extended to the \"foremost\" or \"noblest\" part of any thing, and to the self (dhāt) in {ويبقى وجه ربك}. Here, \"washing the face\" is the specified condition (sharṭ) — the \"foremost\" ritual act — not the efficient cause of purification.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the bodily face. Metaphorical: \"wajh al-qawm\" (the leaders), \"wajh al-thawb\" (the surface of a garment) — the \"face\" as the operative locus; in the analogy, the face's washing is the trigger, not the agent.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Al-Faṣl bayna al-jār wa-al-majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — Governing principle: a dependent element (here, divine power's \"attachment\" to objects) can be separated from its governor by an intervening condition, just as grammatical separation is permissible when the intervening clause is complete; analogically, the \"link\" (irṭibāṭ) of power to its effect is mediated by conditions without altering the agent's nature.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "101": [{"arabic": "جهنم من الجنة والناس أجمعين وقال تعالى إن الذين سبقت لهم منا الحسنى أولئك عنها مبعدون فإن خطر ببالك أنه لم اختلفت السابقة وهم في ربقه العبودية مشتركون نوديت من سرادقات الجلال لا تجاوز حد الأدب فإنه لا يسئل عما يفعل وهم يسئلون ولعمري تأدب اللسان والظاهر مما يقدر عليه الأكثرون فأما تأدب السر عن إضمار الاستبعاد بهذا الاختلاف الظاهر في التقريب والإبعاد والإشقاء والإسعاد مع بقاء السعادة والشقاوة أبد الآباد فلا يقوى عليه إلا العلماء الراسخون في العلم ولهذا قال الخضر عليه السلام لما سئل عن السماع في المنام إنه الصفو الزلال الذي لا يثبت عليه إ", "english": "Among the masters of ecstatic finding (arbāb al-wajd), there is one whom a state overwhelms like intoxicating drunkenness (sukr mudhish), so his tongue loosens with reproach (ʿitāb) toward God Almighty, and he finds objectionable (yastankiru) His overwhelming (iqtihār) of hearts and His apportioning of noble states differentially. For He is the One who elects (mustaṣfā) the hearts of the veracious (ṣiddīqīn) and distances the hearts of the deniers and the deluded (jāḥidīn wa-l-maghrūrīn). There is no preventer of what He gives, and no giver of what He withholds. He did not cut off divine success (tawfīq) from the disbelievers due to some prior transgression, nor did He extend to the Prophets, peace be upon them, His success and the light of His guidance due to some preceding means. Rather, He said: {«وَلَقَدْ سَبَقَتْ كَلِمَتُنَا لِعِبَادِنَا الْمُرْسَلِينَ»} \"And Our word has already preceded for Our servants, the messengers\" [Al-Ṣāffāt/171]. And He said, Mighty and Majestic: {«وَلَٰكِنْ حَقَّ الْقَوْلُ مِنِّي لَأَمْلَأَنَّ جَهَنَّمَ مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ أَجْمَعِينَ»} \"But the word from Me has taken effect: I will surely fill Hell with jinn and people all together\" [Al-Sajdah/13]. And He said, Exalted: {«إِنَّ الَّذِينَ سَبَقَتْ لَهُمْ مِنَّا الْحُسْنَىٰ أُولَٰئِكَ عَنْهَا مُبْعَدُونَ»} \"Indeed, those for whom the best [reward] has preceded from Us — they are from it far removed\" [Al-Anbiyāʾ/101].", "book_title": "Audition and Ecstasy", "section_title_ar": "Section 503", "section_title_en": "The Peril of Listening (Samāʿ) for the Novice and the Reality of Divine Transcendence Beyond Change", "section_index": 503, "anchors": "- Root: لون (L-W-N)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of color, hue, and physical appearance; also used for types and kinds.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اللون معروف، وينطوي على الأبيض والأسود وما يركب منهما، ويقال: تلون: إذا اكتسى لوناً غير اللون الذي كان له\" — indicating acquisition of a new state or attribute.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): physical coloring and change of hue. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"عنده لون من الثياب: صنف منه\" — a type, kind, or varying disposition; hence \"mutalawwin\" (متلون) as one who shifts between states and moods.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn over, overturn, transform, or redirect from one face to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the continuous turning and overturning of a thing; God as \"Muqallib al-Qulūb\" (Turner of hearts).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical overturning. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who manages and disposes affairs; divine action of disposing hearts.\n- Root: حول (H-W-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Change, transformation, power, and capacity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الحول تغير الشيء وانفصاله عن غيره\" — change and separation from another state; \"يحول بين المرء وقلبه\" {«الأنفال/ 24»} — divine interposition between a man and his heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: passage of time, transformation. Metaphorical: \"حال عليه الحول\" — the changing of conditions; \"حائل\" as that which has altered.\n- Root: قرب (Q-R-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness in place, time, or rank.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القرب والبعد يتقابلان... ويستعمل ذلك في المكان، وفي الزمان، وفي النسبة، وفي الحظوة، والرعاية، والقدرة\" — including nearness in honor and station (zulfā).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical proximity. Metaphorical: \"فلان يقرب أمراً لا يتسهل له\" — drawing near in rank and favor; divine drawing near (taqrīb) and distancing (ibʿād).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the meaning remains clear, paralleling how divine attributes may be qualified without compromising essential unity.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Audition and Ecstasy'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "يدري أنه من الذين سبقت لهم من الله الحسنى وقيل في معنى قوله تعالى {وجاءت سكرة الموت بالحق} أي بالسابقة يعني أظهرتها وقال بعض السلف إنما يوزن من الأعمال خواتيمها وكان أبو الدرداء رضي الله عنه يحلف بالله ما من أحد يأمن أن يسلب إيمانه إلا سلبه وقيل من الذنوب ذنوب عقوبتها سوء الخاتمة نعوذ بالله من ذلك وقيل هي عقوبات دعوى الولاية والكرامة بالافتراء وقال بعض العارفين لو عرضت علي الشهادة عند باب الدار والموت على التوحيد", "english": "...doubt concerning this truthfulness. Likewise, God Most High said: {«But righteousness is he who believes in God and the Last Day and the angels and the Book and the prophets»} (2:177) — thus He conditioned twenty attributes, such as faithfulness to covenant and patience under hardship. Then He said Most High: {«Those are the truthful ones»} (2:177). And God Most High said: {«God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, by degrees»} (58:11). And He said Most High: {«Not equal among you are those who spent before the conquest and fought»} (57:10). And He said Most High: {«They are degrees before God»} (3:163). And the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Faith is naked, and its garment is God-consciousness (al-taqwā).\" And the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Faith has seventy-some branches; the lowest of them is removing harm from the path.\" All this indicates the interconnection of the perfection of faith with works.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 104", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book of the Secrets of Zakāh, Section 104: On the Degrees of Faith and Its Connection to Works, and the Hidden Hypocrisy (al-Nifāq al-Khafī) That Contradicts the Perfection of Faith", "section_index": 104, "anchors": "- Root: ن ف ق (N-F-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes passing away, depletion, and expenditure; also the underground burrow of the jerboa (nāfiqāʾ) with a concealed exit — the etymological basis for hypocrisy as a hidden exit from faith.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"نفق الشيء: مضى ونفد\" — theological nuance: hypocrisy (nifāq) is that which causes faith to expire and pass away, whether by sale (worldly gain) or by death (spiritual ruin).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نفقت الدراهم\" (coins ran out). Metaphorical: \"نفق روحه: خرج\" (his soul expired and went forth) — hence the hypocrite's faith is one whose spirit has already departed.\n- Root: أ ي م (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A woman without a husband; a man long unmarried. Al-Raghib: \"الحرب مأيمة\" — war makes women widows.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"تركوا النساء أيامى\" (they left women widowed). Metaphorical application: the one who feels secure from hypocrisy is like one who claims immunity from widowhood — a false security.\n- Root: و ف ي (W-F-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fullness, completion, fulfillment of a measure or covenant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — the one who has reached completion. \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — to fulfill the covenant without breach.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" (a full-weight dirham). Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة\" (he surpassed a hundred) — faith's perfection (wafāʾ) is only attained by surpassing mere profession to include works and inward sincerity.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the grammatical force of \"إنما\" constructions and conditional clauses (من كان...), where the predicate is restricted to the condition stated, as in the hadith \"أربع من كن فيه فهو منافق خالص\" — the four traits are exhaustive of the ruling.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "اللهم إن أنت أمرضتني لتقبض روحي في مرضي هذا فاجعل روحي في أرواح من سبقت لهم منك الحسنى وباعدني من النار كما باعدت أولياءك الذين سبقت لهم منك الحسنى (1) وروي أنه قال صلى الله عليه وسلم عيادة المريض بعد ثلاث فواق ناقة (2) وقال طاوس أفضل العيادة أخفها وقال ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما عيادة المريض مرة سنة فما ازدادت فنافلة وقال بعضهم عيادة المريض بعد ثلاث وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أغبوا في العيادة وأربعوا فيها (3) وجملة أدب المريض حسن الصبر وقلة الشكوى والضجر والفزع إلى الدعاء والتوكل بعد الدواء على خالق الدواء ومنها أن", "english": "A man defended him, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"Whoever defends the honor of his brother shall have it as a shield for him against the Fire.\" (1)", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 435", "section_title_en": "Section 435", "section_index": 435, "anchors": "- Root: رد (r-d-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To return, repel, defend, avert; al-radd ʿan al-ʿirḍ is to repel defamation from one's honor\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Defense against false accusation; the theological nuance of divine recompense (al-mukāfaʾah) for defending a brother's honor\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal repulsion of physical harm extended metaphorically to verbal defense of honor (ḥaqīqah vs. majāz)\n- Root: عطس (ʿ-t-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sneezing; al-tashmīt is the invocation of mercy upon the sneezer\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The sneeze is attributed to God (min Allāh) as a sign of divine favor, while yawning is attributed to Satan\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The response \"yarḥamukallāh\" is a supplication, not an assertion — distinguishing literal invocation from mere congratulation\n- Root: عاد (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To visit repeatedly; al-ʿiyādah is the visitation of the sick\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Returning to someone in his affliction; the reward is measured by the intensity of compassion shown\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The \"return\" (ʿawd) is literal in visitation, but the reward is spiritual — a metaphorical extension of recompense\n- Root: شيع (sh-y-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To accompany, follow; al-tashyīʿ is the funeral procession\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Following the bier with the intention of honoring the deceased and taking admonition (al-iʿtibār)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The qīrāṭ is a measure of reward — metaphorical weight, not literal coin\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Protasis (Bāb al-sharṭ) — The construction \"man ... fa-...\" (whoever does X, then Y) establishes a causal nexus requiring the apodosis to be a new sentence, often carrying the force of divine decree.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "سبقت لهم منك الحسنى وباعدني من النار كما باعدت أولياءك الذين سبقت لهم منك الحسنى (1) وروي أنه قال صلى الله عليه وسلم عيادة المريض بعد ثلاث فواق ناقة (2) وقال طاوس أفضل العيادة أخفها وقال ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما عيادة المريض مرة سنة فما ازدادت فنافلة وقال بعضهم عيادة المريض بعد ثلاث وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أغبوا في العيادة وأربعوا فيها (3) وجملة أدب المريض حسن الصبر وقلة الشكوى والضجر والفزع إلى الدعاء والتوكل بعد الدواء على خالق الدواء ومنها أن يشيع جنائزهم قال صلى الله عليه وسلم من شيع جنازة فله قيراط", "english": "A man defended him, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"Whoever defends the honor of his brother shall have it as a shield for him against the Fire.\" (1)", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 435", "section_title_en": "Section 435", "section_index": 435, "anchors": "- Root: رد (r-d-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To return, repel, defend, avert; al-radd ʿan al-ʿirḍ is to repel defamation from one's honor\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Defense against false accusation; the theological nuance of divine recompense (al-mukāfaʾah) for defending a brother's honor\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal repulsion of physical harm extended metaphorically to verbal defense of honor (ḥaqīqah vs. majāz)\n- Root: عطس (ʿ-t-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sneezing; al-tashmīt is the invocation of mercy upon the sneezer\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The sneeze is attributed to God (min Allāh) as a sign of divine favor, while yawning is attributed to Satan\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The response \"yarḥamukallāh\" is a supplication, not an assertion — distinguishing literal invocation from mere congratulation\n- Root: عاد (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To visit repeatedly; al-ʿiyādah is the visitation of the sick\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Returning to someone in his affliction; the reward is measured by the intensity of compassion shown\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The \"return\" (ʿawd) is literal in visitation, but the reward is spiritual — a metaphorical extension of recompense\n- Root: شيع (sh-y-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To accompany, follow; al-tashyīʿ is the funeral procession\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Following the bier with the intention of honoring the deceased and taking admonition (al-iʿtibār)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The qīrāṭ is a measure of reward — metaphorical weight, not literal coin\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Protasis (Bāb al-sharṭ) — The construction \"man ... fa-...\" (whoever does X, then Y) establishes a causal nexus requiring the apodosis to be a new sentence, often carrying the force of divine decree.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "104": [{"arabic": "الآخرة ما خلقكم ولا بعثكم إلا كنفس واحدة كما بدأنا أول خلق نعيده وكما قال تعالى جزاء وفاقا أي وافق الجزاء أعمالهم فقوبل الخالص بالصرف من الشراب وقوبل المشوب بالمشوب وشوب كل شراب على قدر ما سبق من الشوب في حبه وأعماله فمن يعمل مثقال ذرة خيرا يره ومن يعمل مثقال ذرة شرا يره وإن الله لا يغير ما بقوم حتى يغيروا ما بأنفسهم إن الله لا يظلم مثقال ذرة وإن تك حسنة يضاعفها وإن كان مثقال حبة من خردل أتينا بها وكفى بنا حاسبين فمن كان حبه في الدنيا رجاءه لنعيم الجنة والحور العين والقصور مكن من الجنة ليتبوأ منها حيث يشاء", "english": "...twenty years.\nAnd al-Junayd said: \"The mark of the lover is the constancy of vigor and cheerfulness with a passion that wearies his body but does not weary his heart.\"\nAnd some of them said: \"Labor borne out of love — languor does not enter into it.\"\nAnd one of the scholars said: \"By God, no lover of God is ever fully quenched by His obedience, even if he were to attain to the most immense of means.\"\nAll of this and its like is found in observable experience, for the passionate lover does not find burdensome the pursuit of his beloved's desire, and he savors with his heart the service of the beloved even if it is arduous upon his body. And whenever his body falls short, the dearest of things to him is that his capacity should return to him and that incapacity should depart from him, so that he may occupy himself with it. Such, likewise, is the love of God the Exalted — for every love that becomes dominant inevitably subdues whatever is beneath it. So whoever's beloved is dearer to him than his sloth abandons sloth in serving him; and if the beloved is dearer to him than his wealth, he spends his wealth in his love.\nAnd it was said to one of the lovers — who had expended his very self and his wealth until nothing remained to him — \"What was the cause of your state in love?\" He said: \"One day I heard a lover who had secluded himself with his beloved, saying: 'By God, I love you with my whole heart, while you turn away from me with your whole face.' The beloved said to him: 'If you truly love me, what will you spend upon me?' He said: 'O my master, I put you in possession of all that I own, and then I spend upon you my very spirit until it is destroyed.'\" So I said: \"This is a creature loving a creature, and a slave loving a slave — how then with slaves loving the Worshipped One?\" All of this is by His cause.\nAnd among them is that he be compassionate toward all of God's servants, merciful to them, and severe against all of God's enemies — and against everyone who commits something of what God dislikes — as God the Exalted said: {«أشداء على الكفار رحماء بينهم»} \"Harsh against the disbelievers, merciful among themselves.\" No reproach of any reproacher seizes him, and no diverting force turns him from wrath for God's sake. With this God described His friends, saying: \"Those who are enamored with My love as a child is enamored with a thing, and who take refuge in My remembrance as the eagle takes refuge in its nest, and who rage for the sake of My sanctities as the leopard rages when provoked — for then he cares not whether the people are few or many.\"\nSo consider this example: the child, when enamored with a thing, does not part from it at all; if it is taken from him, he has no occupation but weeping and crying out until it is returned to him. If he sleeps, he takes it with him into his clothes; when he awakens, he returns and clings to it. Whenever he is parted from it, he weeps; whenever he finds it, he laughs. Whoever contends with him over it, he hates; whoever gives it to him, he loves. As for the leopard, he does not master himself at the moment of rage, until he reaches such a pitch of fury that he destroys himself. These, then, are the marks of love. Whoever's marks are perfected in him, his love is perfected and his affection is purified — so in the Hereafter his drink is clear and his draught is sweet.\nBut whoever's love is mingled with the love of other than God, he delights in the Hereafter in proportion to his love, for his drink is mingled with a measure of the drink of the Near Ones (al-muqarrabūn), as God the Exalted said concerning the righteous (al-abrār): {«إن الأبرار لفي نعيم»} \"Verily the righteous are in bliss,\" {ثم قال} then He said: {«يسقون من رحيق مختوم * ختامه مسك وفي ذلك فليتنافس المتنافسون * ومزاجه من تسنيم * عينا يشرب بها المقربون»} \"They are given to drink of sealed nectar, whose seal is musk — and in that let the competitors compete — and its mixture is of tasnīm, a spring from which the Near Ones drink.\" So when the drink of the righteous is sweetened by the admixture of the pure drink which belongs to the Near Ones...\nAnd \"drink\" is an expression for the totality of the bliss of the Gardens, just as the Book expressed by it all deeds, for He said: {«إن كتاب الأبرار لفي عليين»} \"Verily the book of the righteous is in 'Illiyyūn,\" {ثم قال} then He said: {«يشهده المقربون»} \"The Near Ones bear witness to it.\" So it was a sign of the loftiness of their book that it ascended to where the Near Ones bear witness to it. And just as the righteous find increase in their state and their knowledge by their proximity to the Near Ones and their beholding of them, so likewise is their state in the Hereafter — {«ما خلقكم ولا بعثكم إلا كنفس واحدة»} \"Your creation and your resurrection are but as a single soul,\" just as He said — as we began the first creation, We shall repeat it — and as the Exalted said: {«جزاء وفاقا»} \"A recompense fully matching\" — that is, the recompense matched their deeds. So the pure was met with the unmixed of the drink, and the mingled was met with the mingled.\nAnd the admixture of every drink is in proportion to what preceded of admixture in his love and his deeds. For {«من يعمل مثقال ذرة خيرا يره * ومن يعمل مثقال ذرة شرا يره»} \"Whoever does an atom's weight of good shall see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil shall see it.\" And {«إن الله لا يغير ما بقوم حتى يغيروا ما بأنفسهم * إن الله لا يظلم مثقال ذرة وإن تك حسنة يضاعفها وإن كان مثقال حبة من خردل أتينا بها وكفى بنا حاسبين»} \"Verily God does not change what is in a people until they change what is in themselves. Verily God does not wrong an atom's weight; and if it be a good deed, He multiplies it, and if it be a grain of mustard-seed, We bring it — and sufficient are We as reckoners.\"\nSo whoever's love in this world was his hope for the bliss of the Garden, the wide-eyed houris, and the palaces — he is granted the Garden to settle therein wherever he wishes, to sport with the immortal youths and take pleasure with the women. There his delight in the Hereafter reaches its end, because every man is given in love only what his soul desires and his eye delights in.\nBut whoever's aim was the Lord of the Abode and the Owner of the Kingdom, and nothing overcame him save His love with sincerity and truthfulness — he is lodged in the seat...", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1179", "section_title_en": "The Marks of True Love and the Degrees of the Hereafter", "section_index": 1179, "anchors": "- Root: نشط (n-sh-ṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of vitality, energy, and alacrity in action.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Activity of the soul (nafs) in pursuing what it loves, unflagging in its ardor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bodily vigor; Metaphorical: the heart's persistent zeal that overrides physical exhaustion.\n- Root: شرب (sh-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Drinking; the act of imbibing liquids.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, a metaphor (majaz) for the soul's reception of spiritual bliss and states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical quenching; Metaphorical: the tasting of paradisiacal delight, graded by purity (safā') versus admixture (shawb).\n- Root: صفو (ṣ-f-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Purity, clarity, and freedom from turbidity or mixture.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unalloyed state of sincere love (mahabbah) and its corresponding reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: clear liquid; Metaphorical: the soul's exclusive devotion to God, yielding unmixed bliss.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to delimit the final reward strictly by the object and purity of one's love, as in \"إنما يعطى كل إنسان في المحبة ما تشتهيه نفسه.\"", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Afterlife"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 310, 1425], [2, 1425, 3241], [3, 3241, 5012], [4, 5012, 5988], [5, 5988, 6578], [6, 6578, 7475], [7, 7475, 9540]], "21:1": [[1, 310, 1425], [2, 1425, 3241], [3, 3241, 5012], [4, 5012, 5988], [5, 5988, 6578], [6, 6578, 7475], [7, 7475, 9540]], "2": [[1, 550, 1375], [2, 1375, 3360], [3, 3360, 4074], [4, 4074, 4765], [5, 4765, 5479], [6, 5479, 6683], [7, 6683, 7887], [8, 7887, 9147], [9, 9147, 10931], [10, 10931, 11890], [11, 11890, 13910]], "21:2": [[1, 550, 1375], [2, 1375, 3360], [3, 3360, 4074], [4, 4074, 4765], [5, 4765, 5479], [6, 5479, 6683], [7, 6683, 7887], [8, 7887, 9147], [9, 9147, 10931], [10, 10931, 11890], [11, 11890, 13910]], "3": [[1, 450, 2041], [2, 2041, 3570], [3, 3570, 5925], [4, 5925, 7730], [5, 7730, 9076], [6, 9076, 10779], [7, 10779, 11166], [8, 11166, 13372], [9, 13372, 14524], [10, 14524, 15177], [